window.HOLUS_DATA=window.HOLUS_DATA||{};window.HOLUS_DATA["ns"]={"id":"ns","num":2,"name":"Nutrition: Studies and Applications","area":"Nutrition science","lessons":[{"n":1,"title":"Water and Carbohydrates","kicker":"Lesson 1 of your course, with your official Lesson 1 Test.","steps":[{"id":"ns1-0","chapter":"Why water matters","title":"Why water matters","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> <b>Water is the environment in which much of the body's chemistry happens.</b> Your body isn't a collection of dry organs — cells exist in fluid environments, and blood, digestive secretions, urine, sweat, lymph and tears all contain water.</p> <p>Think of your body as a city. Food provides the materials. Oxygen helps provide the energy. Organs are the buildings and factories. But without a transportation system, materials can't get where they need to go, and waste can't leave. <b>Water is that transportation system.</b></p> ","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Why water matters","items":[{"label":"MOVE","detail":"Nutrients and substances get carried where they need to go."},{"label":"MIX","detail":"Provides a medium for biochemical processes."},{"label":"REMOVE","detail":"Helps carry waste products out of the body."}]}},{"id":"ns1-1","chapter":"Body water","title":"Body water","html":"<p>The book describes the human body as being roughly <b>60% water</b>, although the actual percentage varies from person to person. The important concept is that water represents a very large portion of human body mass and is fundamental to normal physiology.</p> <p>Water occurs throughout the body:</p> <p>🩸 Blood</p><p>🧬 Cells</p><p>🧠 Tissues</p> <p>💧 Lymph</p><p>🍽️ Digestive secretions</p><p>🚽 Urine</p> <p>💦 Sweat</p><p>😢 Tears</p> <p></p> ","visual":null},{"id":"ns1-2","chapter":"Water + electrolytes","title":"Water + electrolytes","html":"<p>Water doesn't work alone. This brings in <b>electrolytes</b> — sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium and chloride. Learn the relationship between water, electrolytes, nerve function, muscle function and fluid balance — it becomes especially important later, in Unit 4: Minerals.</p> <p>💧 WATER</p> <p> Sodium (Na)Potassium (K)Calcium (Ca) Magnesium (Mg)Chloride (Cl) </p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns1-3","chapter":"How much water do we need?","title":"How much water do we need?","html":"<p>The Lesson 1 test asks which factors influence individual water requirements — and the answer is <b>all of the above</b>. Water requirements are individual. Use the mnemonic <b>C A D B</b>.</p> <p>CClimate</p> <p>AActivity</p> <p>DDiet</p> <p>BBody size</p> <p>Instead of \"everyone needs exactly eight glasses,\" ask: <i>what could change this particular person's fluid requirement?</i> That's practitioner thinking.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns1-3-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which set matches what Alive tests as factors influencing individual water requirements?","options":[{"label":"Body size, eye colour, climate, and activity level","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"Climate, activity level, diet, and body size","correct":true,"feedback":""},{"label":"Age, sleep duration, hair colour, and climate","correct":false,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns1-4","chapter":"What's actually in water?","title":"What's actually in water?","html":" <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> <b>Not everything dissolved in water is a \"toxin.\"</b> Water can naturally acquire minerals — when it passes through limestone and dolomite, it picks up <b>calcium and magnesium</b>. That's the beginning of understanding hard water.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"What's actually in water?","items":[{"label":"Microorganisms","detail":"Bacteria, viruses, parasites and similar organisms."},{"label":"Disinfectants","detail":"Substances such as chlorine used to treat the water supply."},{"label":"Inorganic chemicals","detail":"Non-organic compounds and elements that can be present."},{"label":"Radionuclides","detail":"Radioactive elements that can occur in water sources."}]}},{"id":"ns1-5","chapter":"Activated carbon","title":"Activated carbon","html":" <p>Activated carbon has an enormous microscopic surface area. As water passes through it, certain compounds <b>stick to its surface</b> — reducing particular substances and improving taste and odour. Don't learn \"carbon removes everything\" — different filters target different substances.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> CARBON = STICK</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns1-5-x","title":"Explore: Activated carbon","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"Absorption","text":"🧽 → 💧 (soaks in)Something goes into another material — like water entering a sponge."},{"label":"Adsorption","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns1-6","chapter":"Reverse osmosis","title":"Reverse osmosis","html":"<p>Reverse osmosis uses a <b>membrane</b>. Pressure forces water through a semipermeable membrane, and some dissolved substances pass through much less readily than water does — like a security checkpoint.</p> <p>🚰Water + dissolved material</p> <p>→</p> <p>🛂Membrane checkpoint</p> <p>→</p> <p>💧Water passes through</p> <p>The mechanism to remember is <b>membrane separation</b>.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> RO = BLOCK</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns1-7","chapter":"Distillation","title":"Distillation","html":" <p><p>1</p><p>🔥 <b>Heated</b> — water is brought to a boil.</p> <p>2</p><p>☁️ <b>Vaporized</b> — it turns into steam.</p> <p>3</p><p>💧 <b>Condensed</b> — the steam cools back into liquid water.</p> </p> <p>The water changes phase while many dissolved substances are left behind.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> DISTILLATION = EVAPORATE</p> <h3>The water filtration triangle</h3> <p>This is the diagram to have burned into memory:</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> STICK → BLOCK → EVAPORATE</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns1-9","chapter":"What is a carbohydrate?","title":"What is a carbohydrate?","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> <b>Carbohydrates are one of the three macronutrients</b> — carbohydrate, protein, fat. Alive wants you to associate them primarily with energy, but that's not their only role: they also provide fibre, contribute to the functioning of internal organs, and help regulate the metabolism of the other macronutrients.</p> <p>The test asks which is <b>not</b> a function of carbohydrates, with \"encourages ketogenesis\" as the contrasting option — ketogenesis happens when carbohydrate intake is <i>low</i>, so it isn't something carbs themselves do.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> CARBS = FUEL + FIBRE</p> ","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"What is a carbohydrate?","items":[{"label":"Photosynthesis","detail":"Plants use sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide to produce carbohydrate. Alive explicitly tests that carbohydrates are produced by photosynthesis in plants."},{"label":"Food sources","detail":"Fruit, vegetables, potatoes, grains, and legumes all carry carbohydrate from plant to plate."}]}},{"id":"ns1-10","chapter":"Sugars, starches & fibre","title":"Sugars, starches & fibre","html":"<p>The book organizes carbohydrates around three principal categories: <b>sugars, starches, and fibre.</b> Within sugars, building blocks matter more than memorizing names.</p> <p>MONO = ONE</p> <p>GlucoseFructoseGalactose</p> <p>DI = TWO (mono + mono)</p> <p>Sucrose = glucose + fructoseLactose = glucose + galactoseMaltose = glucose + glucose</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🧠 <b>The -ose / -ase trick:</b> \"-ose\" usually marks a sugar, \"-ase\" usually marks the enzyme that breaks it down. Lact<b>ose</b> is the sugar, lact<b>ase</b> is the enzyme; sucr<b>ose</b> is the sugar, sucr<b>ase</b> is the enzyme. Alive spends real time on lactose specifically — it appears in breast milk, and lactase is the enzyme responsible for digesting it.</p> <p>Polysaccharides (\"poly\" = many) are long chains — think starch. Picture a pearl necklace: digestion cuts the chain down, piece by piece, until only single, absorbable units remain.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns1-11","chapter":"From food to monosaccharide","title":"From food to monosaccharide","html":"<p>🔥 One of the most important parts of Unit 1. Alive explicitly tests that digestion involves enzymes, that disaccharides become monosaccharides, and that monosaccharides are the end products.</p> <p><p>1</p><p>👄 <b>Mouth</b> — salivary amylase starts working on starch.</p> <p>2</p><p>🫃 <b>Stomach</b> — not the main site of carbohydrate digestion.</p> <p>3</p><p>🌀 <b>Small intestine</b> — pancreatic and intestinal enzymes continue: complex carbs → smaller carbs → disaccharides.</p> <p>4</p><p>🍬 <b>Monosaccharides</b> — the true end products, absorbed through the intestinal wall.</p> <p>5</p><p>🩸🏭 <b>Bloodstream → liver</b> — absorbed sugars travel onward for processing.</p> </p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🔥 <b>Sucrose example (Alive asks this directly):</b> Sucrose → <b>sucrase</b> → glucose + fructose, in the <b>small intestine</b>. Sugar → matching enzyme: lactose → lactase, sucrose → sucrase, maltose → maltase.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns1-12","chapter":"Glucose = blood sugar","title":"Glucose = blood sugar","html":"<p>Stop seeing the liver as only a \"detox organ\" — nutritionally, it's an enormous <b>metabolic processing centre.</b> Alive specifically tests that a healthy liver helps regulate the glucose available in circulation for cells, and expects you to recognize the relationship between fructose/galactose and glucose metabolism.</p> <p>🍞Food</p> <p>→</p> <p>🍬Glucose</p> <p>→</p> <p>🩸Blood</p> <p>→</p> <p>⚡Cell energy</p> <p>The book describes glucose as the principal sugar used by tissues and cells for energy — and it can be measured through blood-sugar testing.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns1-13","chapter":"Pancreas → insulin → glucose management","title":"Pancreas → insulin → glucose management","html":"<p>Alive explicitly tests: the pancreas secretes insulin; sugars from food enter the bloodstream; insulin helps move glucose from blood toward cells.</p> <p> 🏠 Cell = house 🚪 Membrane = door 🍬 Glucose = package 🔑 Insulin = key/signal </p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> PANCREAS → INSULIN → GLUCOSE MANAGEMENT</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns1-13-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which organ secretes insulin?","options":[{"label":"Liver","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"Pancreas","correct":true,"feedback":""},{"label":"Stomach","correct":false,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns1-14","chapter":"GI = speed, not sweetness","title":"GI = speed, not sweetness","html":"<p>Alive asks this directly: the glycemic index determines <b>how quickly a food raises blood sugar levels</b> — not how much sugar or how many calories it contains.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🥔 <b>The potato/ice cream surprise:</b> Alive's true/false item — \"potatoes are lower on the glycemic index than ice cream\" — exists specifically to challenge \"sweet taste = higher GI.\" GI isn't a sweetness scale; food structure, starch type, processing, and mixed nutrients all change the glycemic response.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns1-14-x","title":"Explore: GI = speed, not sweetness","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"Higher GI","text":"📈Generally a faster blood-glucose rise."},{"label":"Lower GI","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns1-15","chapter":"Soluble vs. insoluble","title":"Soluble vs. insoluble","html":"<p>Fibre is carbohydrate, but unlike digestible carbohydrate, much of it isn't broken down into glucose by human digestive enzymes.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> SOLUBLE → SOAKS · INSOLUBLE → BULK</p> <p>Fibre connects carbs to digestion → bowel function → gut microbiota → metabolic health → cardiovascular health. That's why \"carbohydrates are bad\" is nutritionally useless — fruit, beans, oats, and table sugar are all carbohydrate, but not remotely interchangeable. Alive itself tests that carbohydrates are <b>not healthy in every form.</b></p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Soluble vs. insoluble","items":[{"label":"Soluble","detail":"Interacts with water, may form viscous gels (oats, legumes, some fruits). Can influence stool characteristics, glucose absorption, and cholesterol metabolism — hemicellulose compounds like glucomannans and galactomannans are specifically tested for cholesterol-lowering activity."},{"label":"Insoluble","detail":"Adds bulk and supports bowel function (whole grains, many vegetables)."}]}},{"id":"ns1-16","chapter":"Storage, building, and breaking down","title":"Storage, building, and breaking down","html":"<p>If glucose is available but not needed immediately, some is stored as <b>glycogen</b> — the body's quick-access carbohydrate storage, mainly in liver and muscle.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> LIPOGENESIS = MAKE/STORE ↕ LIPOLYSIS = BREAK DOWN/USE</p> <h3>The entire Unit 1 carbohydrate map</h3> <p>🍞Carbohydrate</p> <p>→</p> <p>🍬Monosaccharides</p> <p>→</p> <p>🩸Blood glucose</p> <p>→</p> <p>🔑Insulin</p> <p>→</p> <p>⚡Energy / 🔋 Glycogen / 🧈 Fat</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Storage, building, and breaking down","items":[{"label":"Lipogenesis","detail":"LIPO = fat, GENESIS = create. The conversion of excess glucose into fatty acids and triglycerides that can be stored as body fat."},{"label":"Lipolysis","detail":"LIPO = fat, LYSIS = break apart. Converting stored fat reserves back into fatty acids for fuel."}]}}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is adsorption?","back":"Attachment of molecules to a surface."},{"front":"Activated carbon works primarily through?","back":"Adsorption."},{"front":"RO works primarily through?","back":"Membrane separation."},{"front":"Distillation works through?","back":"Vaporization followed by condensation."},{"front":"What four factors does Alive test as affecting water requirements?","back":"Climate, activity, diet and body size."},{"front":"Five important electrolytes?","back":"Sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium and chloride."},{"front":"Water passing through limestone/dolomite can acquire which minerals?","back":"Calcium and magnesium."},{"front":"Main nutritional role associated with carbohydrates?","back":"Energy."},{"front":"Three broad carbohydrate categories?","back":"Sugars, starches, and fibre."},{"front":"Three important monosaccharides?","back":"Glucose, fructose, and galactose."},{"front":"What does –ose usually indicate?","back":"Sugar."},{"front":"What does –ase usually indicate?","back":"Enzyme."},{"front":"Enzyme for lactose?","back":"Lactase."},{"front":"Enzyme for sucrose?","back":"Sucrase."},{"front":"Sucrose breaks into what?","back":"Glucose + fructose."},{"front":"Where does Alive test sucrose digestion by sucrase?","back":"Small intestine."},{"front":"End products of carbohydrate digestion?","back":"Monosaccharides."},{"front":"Organ that secretes insulin?","back":"Pancreas."},{"front":"What does insulin help regulate?","back":"Blood glucose and cellular glucose uptake/use."},{"front":"What does glycemic index describe?","back":"How quickly a carbohydrate-containing food raises blood glucose."},{"front":"Two fibre categories?","back":"Soluble and insoluble."},{"front":"Storage form of carbohydrate?","back":"Glycogen."},{"front":"Lipogenesis?","back":"Formation/storage of fat from excess glucose."},{"front":"Lipolysis?","back":"Breakdown/mobilization of stored fat."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Possible contaminants in our drinking water include:","o":["Microorganisms","Disinfectants","Inorganic chemicals","Radionucleotides","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All four categories are named as possible contaminants."},{"q":"When water passes through limestone and dolomite, it picks up naturally occurring:","o":["Iron","Calcium","Magnesium","B and C","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Limestone and dolomite contribute calcium and magnesium — the basis of hard water — not iron."},{"q":"What conditions have been linked to dehydration?","o":["Back pain","Kidney stones","Migraine","Chronic fatigue","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Confirmed: dehydration is commonly linked to all four — reduced spinal disc hydration/back pain, kidney stone formation from concentrated urine, migraine triggers, and fatigue."},{"q":"Factors that can influence our individual water requirements include:","o":["Climate","Activity Level","Diet","Body size","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"C-A-D-B: Climate, Activity level, Diet, and Body size all influence individual water needs."},{"q":"Which of the following is true about carbohydrates:","o":["They play an important role in helping to regulate the metabolism of their fellow macronutrients","Carbohydrates are produced by photosynthesis in plants","All carbohydrates serve as quick energy sources","A and C","A and B"],"a":4,"e":"A and B are both true. C is false — fibre is a carbohydrate that is not a quick energy source."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a function of carbohydrates?","o":["Plays an important role in the functioning of internal organs","Provides energy","Provides fiber","Encourages ketogenesis"],"a":3,"e":"Ketogenesis happens when carbohydrate intake is low, not because of carbohydrates."},{"q":"Which of the following sugars are broken down into glucose in the liver?","o":["Lactose and fructose","Sucrose and maltose","Galactose and fructose","All of the above"],"a":2,"e":"Galactose and fructose are the two monosaccharides the liver converts into glucose."},{"q":"Which of the following is found in human milk that helps digestion of lactose?","o":["Sucrose","Lactase","Fructose","Maltose"],"a":1,"e":"Lactase, the enzyme that breaks down lactose, is present in human milk."},{"q":"Which hemicelluloses have cholesterol-lowering activity?","o":["Glucomannans","Galactomannans","Xylans","Beta-glucans","A and B"],"a":4,"e":"Confirmed by research: glucomannan and galactomannan fibers (found in foods like konjac and guar/legumes) have both been shown to lower blood/liver cholesterol, distinct from xylans and beta-glucans."},{"q":"The glycemic index determines:","o":["How much sugar is in a food","How many calories are in a food","How quickly a food raises blood sugar levels","None of the above"],"a":2,"e":"Glycemic index ranks foods by how quickly they raise blood sugar."},{"q":"Which of the following statements are true of blood sugar regulation?","o":["The pancreas is responsible for secreting insulin","Normal blood sugar levels can be misleading","Sugars from food are quickly absorbed in the bloodstream","Insulin helps the sugar move from the bloodstream into cells","All of these statements are true"],"a":4,"e":"All four statements describe accurate pieces of blood sugar regulation."},{"q":"Which of the following is FALSE concerning carbohydrate digestion and metabolism?","o":["Carbohydrates are broken down in the gastrointestinal track by various enzymes","Monosaccharides are the end product of carbohydrate digestion","The disaccharides are converted into monosaccharides","The polysaccharides are converted in the mouth by maltase enzymes","None of these statements are false"],"a":3,"e":"Polysaccharide digestion begins in the mouth via amylase, not maltase."},{"q":"Where is sucrose changed into glucose and fructose in the body using the enzyme sucrase?","o":["The liver","The pancreas","The bloodstream","The small intestine"],"a":3,"e":"Sucrase acts in the small intestine."},{"q":"A healthy liver regulates the use of _______ by allowing certain levels to circulate in the blood for use by the cells of the body.","o":["Glucose","Sucrose","Insulin","Galactose"],"a":0,"e":"The liver regulates circulating glucose levels."},{"q":"Lipogenesis is:","o":["The conversion of excess glucose into fatty acids and triglycerides that can be stored as body fat","The process that occurs when fewer carbohydrates are consumed than are needed or can be converted to glycogen","The process of sugar being converted to glucose to be used for energy","The process of weight loss due to a decrease in carbohydrate intake"],"a":0,"e":"Lipogenesis is the conversion of excess glucose into fatty acids/triglycerides for storage."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about glucose is NOT true?","o":["Glucose can be measured by blood sugar testing","High glucose levels can indicate hypoglycemia","Glucose is found in some fruits","Stress can generate elevated glucose levels in the blood"],"a":1,"e":"High glucose indicates hyperglycemia, not hypoglycemia."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about lactose is true?","o":["It is the sugar of the mother's breast milk","It is broken down by the enzyme lactase","It is found in higher quantities in human milk than in cow's milk","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"All three statements about lactose are accurate."},{"q":"Fibre is broken down into two types:","o":["Soluble and crude fibre","Soluble and roughage fibre","Dietary fibre and total fibre","Soluble and insoluble fibre"],"a":3,"e":"The two recognized categories of fibre are soluble and insoluble."},{"q":"Which of the following statements concerning carbohydrates is FALSE?","o":["They contain essential vitamins and minerals","They contain fibre","They are the main source of energy for the body","They are healthy in any form"],"a":3,"e":"Not all carbohydrate forms are healthy — refined sugars are a common counterexample."},{"q":"Water is involved in almost every body function.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Water is central to nearly every physiological process."},{"q":"One water molecule is composed of 3 molecules: 2 hydrogen and 1 oxygen.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"A water molecule is 2 hydrogen atoms and 1 oxygen atom forming one molecule — not 3 molecules."},{"q":"Hyaluronan is believed to promote skin healing by increasing skin hydration.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Hyaluronan is associated with retaining moisture in skin, linked to healing support."},{"q":"On average we need three quarts of water daily, including water contained in our food.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Confirmed: \"three quarts (96 ounces), including food and water\" is the exact average intake figure commonly cited in nutrition course materials on hydration."},{"q":"Three to four cups of water a day is enough to reduce the risk of developing dehydration-related symptoms.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Three to four cups (24-32 oz) is well below typical daily recommendations of roughly 11-15 cups (2.7-3.7 L) for adults, so it would not be considered adequate on its own."},{"q":"More than 77% of river discharge in North America is affected by dams.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Confirmed by research (Dynesius & Nilsson, Science, 1994): 77% of the total water discharge of the largest river systems in North America, Europe, and the former USSR is strongly or moderately affected by dams and flow regulation."},{"q":"Coffee is a diuretic and can cause us to excrete more water than it contains.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Confirmed as a myth: while caffeine has a mild diuretic effect, multiple studies and meta-analyses show moderate coffee intake does not cause net fluid loss beyond what's consumed — coffee still counts toward daily hydration."},{"q":"Many carbohydrates are not easily digested.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Fibre and some resistant starches are not easily digested by human enzymes."},{"q":"A diet high in fibre may prevent gastrointestinal disorders and colon cancer.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"High-fibre diets are commonly associated with reduced GI disorder and colon cancer risk."},{"q":"Potatoes are lower on the glycemic index than ice cream.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Potatoes often rank higher on the glycemic index than ice cream, since ice cream's fat/protein content slows blood sugar response."},{"q":"Lipolysis is the name of the process when fat reserves are converted back to fatty acids for body fuel.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Lipolysis is the breakdown of stored fat into usable fatty acids."}],"perStepChapter":false},{"n":2,"title":"Protein and Fats","kicker":"Lesson 2 of your course, with your official Lesson 2 Test.","steps":[{"id":"ns2-0","chapter":"What actually is protein?","title":"What actually is protein?","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> <b>Amino acids = individual LEGO pieces.</b> They connect together to form proteins — complex structures built from those pieces.</p> <p>Your Alive material describes proteins as complex molecules made from combinations of amino acids — and it explicitly tests that the statement <b>\"proteins contain only carbon, oxygen and hydrogen\" is false.</b></p> ","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"What actually is protein?","items":[{"label":"Protein = amino acids + N","detail":"N = nitrogen. This is what chemically separates protein from carbohydrates and fats."},{"label":"~22 amino acids","detail":"Proteins are built from combinations of about 22 amino acids."}]}},{"id":"ns2-1","chapter":"What does protein actually do?","title":"What does protein actually do?","html":"<p>Protein is not just \"protein → muscle.\" Alive explicitly tests growth and maintenance, energy, fluid/salt balance and acid-alkaline buffering as protein functions.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> BUILD → BALANCE → DEFEND → COMMUNICATE</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"What does protein actually do?","items":[{"label":"Build","detail":"Growth and maintenance of body tissues."},{"label":"Balance","detail":"Fluid and salt balance — keeping the correct amount of water in cells — plus acid-alkaline buffering."},{"label":"Defend","detail":"Needed for a healthy immune system."},{"label":"Communicate","detail":"Signalling and communication-related functions, plus energy when needed."}]}},{"id":"ns2-2","chapter":"Essential vs. nonessential","title":"Essential vs. nonessential","html":"<p>\"Nonessential\" does not mean your body doesn't need them — it means they don't necessarily have to be supplied directly by the diet. That distinction is exam-worthy.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns2-2-x","title":"Explore: Essential vs. nonessential","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"Essential","text":"🍽️ → 🧬The body cannot make enough to meet its needs — so it must come from food."},{"label":"Nonessential","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns2-3","chapter":"Amino acids do more than build protein","title":"Amino acids do more than build protein","html":"<p>One amino acid explicitly tested by Alive is <b>tryptophan</b> — a precursor in the pathway leading toward serotonin.</p> <p>🥩Food</p> <p>→</p> <p>🧬Tryptophan</p> <p>→</p> <p>🧠Serotonin</p> <p>The glutamate family — worth remembering as a group:</p> <p>GLUTAMATE</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns2-3-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which amino acid is the precursor to serotonin?","options":[{"label":"Threonine","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"Tryptophan","correct":true,"feedback":""},{"label":"Leucine","correct":false,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns2-4","chapter":"The digestion pathway","title":"The digestion pathway","html":" <p><p>1</p><p>👄 <b>Mouth</b> — mostly mechanical breakdown.</p> <p>2</p><p>🫃 <b>Stomach</b> — hydrochloric acid + pepsin attack peptide bonds, breaking protein into long-chain polypeptides.</p> <p>3</p><p>🌀 <b>Small intestine</b> — pancreatic trypsin continues breaking longer chains into shorter peptides.</p> <p>4</p><p>🧬 <b>Peptidases</b> — turn polypeptides → tripeptides/dipeptides → single amino acids.</p> <p>5</p><p>🩸 <b>Absorption</b> — amino acids cross the intestinal wall into the blood, via the portal vein, to the liver.</p> </p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> STOMACH → PEPSIN → TRYPSIN → PEPTIDASES → AMINO ACIDS → PORTAL VEIN → LIVER</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns2-5","chapter":"Complementary plant proteins","title":"Complementary plant proteins","html":"<p>A \"complete\" dietary protein provides all essential amino acids in adequate proportions. One plant food may be relatively lower in a particular essential amino acid — another food can complement it. The concept matters more than memorizing combinations.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🔥 <b>Alive says know this:</b> the tested protein RDA is <b>0.8 g/kg body weight/day</b>. Example: a 70 kg person → 70 × 0.8 = <b>56 g/day</b>. Practitioner brain: a population-level RDA isn't automatically the ideal intake for every individual — age, goals, overall diet, activity and health circumstances can change the conversation.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Complementary plant proteins","items":[{"label":"Brown rice + sunflower seeds","detail":"A classic complementary plant-protein pairing tested by Alive."},{"label":"Beans + corn","detail":"Beans and corn complement each other's amino acid profiles."},{"label":"Peas + wheat","detail":"Another tested complementary combination."}]}},{"id":"ns2-6","chapter":"9 kcal/g — spot the exam trap","title":"9 kcal/g — spot the exam trap","html":"<p>Carbohydrate and protein each provide about <b>4 kcal/g</b>. Fat provides about <b>9 kcal/g</b> — more than twice as much energy per gram.</p> <p>🍞Carb: 4 kcal/g</p> <p>🥩Protein: 4 kcal/g</p> <p>🥑Fat: 9 kcal/g</p> <p>Alive's test also explicitly challenges the idea that eating fat automatically causes excessive weight gain and should therefore be avoided.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns2-6-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"A quiz option claims fat \"packs more than three times\" the calories of protein or carbs. Is that accurate?","options":[{"label":"True — 9 is more than three times 4","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"False — 9 ÷ 4 = 2.25, so just over twice as much","correct":true,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns2-7","chapter":"Fat ≠ bad","title":"Fat ≠ bad","html":"<p>Fat is another essential macronutrient. Your course expects you to know several functions — cell membranes, hormone-related biology, concentrated energy, structural/protective roles, and transport/absorption of fat-soluble vitamins.</p>  <p>AVitamin A</p> <p>DVitamin D</p> <p>EVitamin E</p> <p>KVitamin K</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> ADEK LOVES FAT</p> <h3>Saturated vs. unsaturated</h3> <p>The course specifically tests that <b>unsaturated fats are more prone to oxidation</b> than saturated fats. More double bonds → more vulnerable points → greater oxidation susceptibility.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns2-7-x","title":"Explore: Fat ≠ bad","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"Saturated","text":"🔗🔗🔗Carbon chains saturated with hydrogen — no double bonds, generally more chemically stable, helps stabilize cell membranes."},{"label":"Unsaturated","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns2-8","chapter":"ALA, LA, and the balance","title":"ALA, LA, and the balance","html":"<p>Both belong to the polyunsaturated-fat family. Alive tests a preferred ratio of <b>2:1 to 4:1</b> (omega-6 : omega-3) — put this in student brain for now; we'll evidence-check whether a precise ratio remains the most useful modern approach further down.</p> <p> ALA (alpha-linolenic acid) → Omega-3 LA (linoleic acid) → Omega-6 </p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> Don't confuse the two — ALA and omega-3 go together; LA and omega-6 go together.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns2-9","chapter":"Cholesterol isn't simply \"bad fat\"","title":"Cholesterol isn't simply \"bad fat\"","html":"<p>Your course expects you to know that cholesterol is present throughout the body, is a precursor for bile acids, and is a precursor for sex hormones. The test treats <b>A + B</b> as that combination. HDL is taught as \"good\" cholesterol — useful exam shorthand, though modern cardiovascular risk assessment is more nuanced.</p> <h3>Fat digestion</h3> <p>Oil and water don't mix — so fat needs special handling. Alive specifically tests pancreatic lipase and identifies the small intestine as the main site of fat digestion.</p> <p>🛢️Fat</p> <p>→</p> <p>🧪Bile emulsifies</p> <p>→</p> <p>✂️Pancreatic lipase breaks down</p> <p>→</p> <p>🩸Absorbed</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> BILE BREAKS UP · LIPASE BREAKS DOWN</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🧱 <b>Fat + cell membranes:</b> membranes are largely built from a lipid bilayer — fat isn't just \"stored calories,\" it's part of the physical structure of your cells. 🩸 <b>HDL/LDL nuance:</b> Alive teaches \"HDL = good cholesterol\" as exam shorthand. For practice, it's more accurate to think of HDL and LDL as <i>lipoprotein particles</i> transporting cholesterol and other lipids, rather than two different \"kinds\" of cholesterol. Also remember: <b>blood cholesterol ≠ dietary cholesterol</b> — related, but not interchangeable.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Cholesterol isn't simply \"bad fat\"","items":[{"label":"Present in almost all cells","detail":"Cholesterol isn't confined to one tissue — it's a component throughout the body."},{"label":"Precursor for bile acids","detail":"Needed to make the bile acids used in fat digestion."},{"label":"Precursor for sex hormones","detail":"The starting material for steroid sex hormones."}]}}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Protein building blocks?","back":"Amino acids."},{"front":"What element distinguishes protein from carbs and fats?","back":"Nitrogen."},{"front":"Essential amino acid means?","back":"Must be obtained through the diet."},{"front":"Which amino acid is the precursor to serotonin?","back":"Tryptophan."},{"front":"What does glutamic acid convert into (inhibitory neurotransmitter)?","back":"GABA."},{"front":"Protein digestion begins significantly where?","back":"The stomach (HCl + pepsin)."},{"front":"Major end products of protein digestion?","back":"Amino acids."},{"front":"What is the protein RDA Alive tests?","back":"0.8 g/kg body weight/day."},{"front":"Calories per gram of protein?","back":"4 kcal/g."},{"front":"Calories per gram of fat?","back":"9 kcal/g."},{"front":"Which vitamins are fat-soluble?","back":"A, D, E, and K."},{"front":"Essential omega-3 fatty acid?","back":"ALA (alpha-linolenic acid)."},{"front":"Essential omega-6 fatty acid?","back":"LA (linoleic acid)."},{"front":"Alive's tested omega-6:omega-3 ratio?","back":"2:1 to 4:1."},{"front":"Main fat-digestion location?","back":"Small intestine."},{"front":"What does bile do to fat?","back":"Emulsifies it (increases surface area)."},{"front":"What does pancreatic lipase do?","back":"Enzymatically digests fat."},{"front":"HDL shorthand in this course?","back":"\"Good\" cholesterol."},{"front":"What is cholesterol a precursor for?","back":"Bile acids and steroid/sex hormones."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding protein is FALSE?","o":["Proteins are complex molecules consisting of combinations of 22 amino acids","Protein molecules are composed of only carbon, oxygen, and hydrogen","Protein makes up about 20% of our body weight","Protein is needed for a healthy immune system"],"a":1,"e":"Protein also contains nitrogen — this is the key chemical distinction from carbohydrates and fats — so “only carbon, oxygen, and hydrogen” is false."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is true when it comes to amino acids?","o":["They are in our bodies not only to provide structure but also to establish communication between cells and entire organ systems","They could be equally as important as proteins when it comes to nourishment","Amino acids with certain functions are called neurotransmitters","All of the statements are true"],"a":3,"e":"Amino acids provide structure and cell-to-cell/organ-system communication, are foundational to nourishment, and some function directly as neurotransmitters."},{"q":"When taking an amino acid supplement, unless stated otherwise, it is advised to:","o":["Limit the use to 6 to 8 weeks with a 2 to 3 week break","Limit the use to 4 to 6 weeks with a 2 to 3 week break","Limit the use to 2 to 4 weeks with a 1 to 2 week break","None of these are sound recommendations"],"a":1,"e":"General guidance for amino-acid supplementation is cycling on for 4-6 weeks with a 2-3 week break to avoid imbalance."},{"q":"To avoid possible amino acid imbalance when supplementing ensure to:","o":["Rotate usage as noted in question 3","Take a basic amino acid mixture that contains all amino acids","Only take the essential amino acids","A and B","A and C","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Rotating usage and taking a balanced mixture covering all amino acids helps prevent imbalance from single-amino-acid supplementation."},{"q":"Which amino acid is the precursor to serotonin?","o":["Threonine","Tryptophan","Leucine","Alanine"],"a":1,"e":"Tryptophan is the dietary precursor in the pathway leading to serotonin."},{"q":"Which amino acid deficiency has been linked to hypoglycemia?","o":["Alanine","Tryptophan","Glutamic acid","Tyrosine"],"a":0,"e":"Alanine plays a key role in the glucose-alanine cycle, supplying the liver with material for gluconeogenesis; a deficiency has been linked to hypoglycemia."},{"q":"Which of the following is true of Glutamic acid?","o":["It is readily converted into the inhibitory neurotransmitter GABA which helps with relaxation and anxiety","It is readily converted into glutamine","It helps transport potassium into the spinal fluid","It has been used to treat fatigue, parkinsonism, and schizophrenia","All of the above are true of glutamic acid"],"a":4,"e":"Glutamic acid converts to both GABA and glutamine, plays a role in potassium transport into spinal fluid, and has a history of therapeutic use for these conditions in nutrition literature."},{"q":"The most commonly seen amino acid deficiencies in full vegetarians and vegans are:","o":["Lysine, methionine, and tryptophan","Lysine, taurine, and tryptophan","Lysine, methionine, and leucine","Lysine, methionine, and taurine"],"a":3,"e":"Lysine, methionine, and taurine are commonly limited in strict vegetarian/vegan diets since they're concentrated in animal foods."},{"q":"Foods that can be combined to create a complete protein include:","o":["Brown rice and sunflower seeds","Beans and corn","Peas and wheat","A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All three pairings are complementary plant-protein combinations named in the course material."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding protein metabolism is FALSE?","o":["Hydrochloric acid, pepsin, and proteases split peptide bonds between amino acid chains turning them into long-chain polypeptides","The pancreatic enzyme trypsin turns long-chain polypeptides into shorter-chain polypeptides","Amino peptidases and dipeptidases turn polypeptides, tripeptides, and dipeptides into single amino acids","The amino acids are transported through the wall of the intestines into the bloodstream and then via the portal vein to the liver","All of the above statements are correct"],"a":4,"e":"Each step (A–D) accurately describes a stage of protein digestion — so none of them are false, making ‘All of the above are correct’ the right choice for this FALSE-statement question."},{"q":"Functions of protein include:","o":["Growth and maintenance of body tissues","Energy for the body","Maintaining fluid and salt balance by helping keep the correct amount of water in the cells","Maintaining an acid-alkaline balance by acting as buffers","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Alive explicitly tests growth/maintenance, energy, fluid/salt balance, and acid-alkaline buffering as protein functions."},{"q":"The recommended dietary allowance for protein per kilogram of body weight is:","o":["0.5 gram","0.8 gram","1.0 gram","1.2 grams"],"a":1,"e":"The tested RDA figure is 0.8 g protein/kg body weight/day."},{"q":"Which nutrients rely on fat for transport and absorption?","o":["Vitamins A, D, E, and K","Vitamins A, C, E, and K","Calcium, B vitamins, and magnesium","EFAs","All of the above"],"a":0,"e":"Vitamins A, D, E, and K are the fat-soluble vitamins that require dietary fat for transport and absorption — remembered as ‘ADEK loves fat.’"},{"q":"Which of the following statements concerning fat is FALSE?","o":["Lipids are found primarily in meats, dairy, nuts, and seeds","Fat packs more than three times the amount of calories as protein or carbs","Fats are an essential component of the cell membrane","Fats help our glands make hormones","Fat keeps our cells strong to protect against invasion by microorganisms or damage by chemicals"],"a":1,"e":"Fat provides about 9 kcal/g versus roughly 4 kcal/g for protein and carbohydrates — about 2.25x, not ‘more than three times’ — making this statement the false one."},{"q":"Why are saturated fats structurally helpful to cells?","o":["They help stabilize cell membranes","They are not very susceptible to damage","They allow the cells to stay in dynamic communication with their surroundings","A and B","B and C"],"a":3,"e":"Saturated fats' chemical stability helps stabilize cell membranes and resist damage — dynamic communication with surroundings is more associated with the fluidity unsaturated fats provide."},{"q":"The best ratio of Omega 6 & Omega 3 is?","o":["2:1 to 4:1","10:1 to 20:1","1:2 to 1:4","2:1 to 10:1"],"a":0,"e":"Alive tests a preferred omega-6:omega-3 ratio of 2:1 to 4:1."},{"q":"It is important to maintain a healthy balance of Omega 3s and 6s because:","o":["The ratio has been linked to risk of noninsulin-dependent diabetes","Healthy genetic processes depend on the balance","Nearly all chemical communication through the body depends at least in part on the balance","Ratios leaning too much towards the Omega 6s are linked to inflammatory illnesses","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"The course ties the omega-6:omega-3 balance to diabetes risk, genetic processes, cellular communication, and inflammatory illness risk."},{"q":"Omega 3 fatty acids are also know as:","o":["Alpha-linolenic acid (ALA)","Linoleic acid (LA)","Monounsaturated fat","A and C"],"a":0,"e":"ALA (alpha-linolenic acid) is the omega-3 fatty acid. LA (linoleic acid) is omega-6, and omega-3s are polyunsaturated, not monounsaturated — so only A is correct."},{"q":"Cholesterol:","o":["Is the precursor of the bile acids and sex hormones","Is present in almost all cells","Can come from plants or animals","A and B","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Cholesterol is a precursor for bile acids/sex hormones and present in almost all cells (A and B) — but cholesterol itself comes only from animal sources, not plants, so ‘All of the above’ is incorrect."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding the relationship of dietary fat and disease is FALSE?","o":["High intake of saturated animal fat is related to increased cancer risk","Increased fat intake is linked to atherosclerosis","Increased consumption of alpha-linolenic acid foods are known to reduce inflammation","Polyunsaturated fats should be used for cooking","None of the statements are false"],"a":3,"e":"Polyunsaturated fats are more prone to oxidation, especially under heat, so they are generally not recommended for cooking — making this statement the false one."},{"q":"Many vegetable proteins contain adequate levels of several of the essential amino acids.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Plant proteins often provide several essential amino acids adequately, even when one or two are relatively lower (making them 'incomplete' rather than absent)."},{"q":"Pancreatic lipase breaks down the fat globules into smaller groups of molecules.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Pancreatic lipase breaks down emulsified fat droplets so the products can be absorbed."},{"q":"Excess protein can be turned into fat and stored in the body as potential fuel or stored as glycogen in the liver.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Excess protein can be deaminated and its carbon skeleton converted into fat or glucose/glycogen depending on the amino acid."},{"q":"Consuming fats causes excessive weight gain and should be avoided.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Fat is an essential macronutrient — the issue is total dietary pattern, not fat consumption itself."},{"q":"To remain healthy, our bodies need both saturated and unsaturated fatty acids.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Both categories of fat serve necessary physiological roles."},{"q":"Unsaturated fats are more delicate than saturated fats and are prone to oxidation.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"More double bonds means more vulnerable points, making unsaturated fats more prone to oxidation."},{"q":"HDL is considered “good” cholesterol.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"HDL is taught as 'good' cholesterol in this course — useful shorthand, though modern cardiovascular risk assessment is more nuanced."},{"q":"Cholesterol in food is the greatest cause of cardiovascular disease.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Dietary cholesterol is not considered the primary driver of cardiovascular disease in current understanding."},{"q":"The small intestine is the main place of digestion of fats.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Bile emulsification and pancreatic lipase activity both occur in the small intestine, the main site of fat digestion."},{"q":"High-fat diets have been linked to the drastic rise in colon cancer. This is thought to be due to the fats in the diet causing a release of bile acids from the gallbladder and liver into the intestine to help emulsify the fats.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This reflects an older bile-acid hypothesis linking high fat intake, increased bile acid release, and colon cancer risk taught in the course material."}],"perStepChapter":false},{"n":3,"title":"Vitamins","kicker":"Lesson 3 of your course, with your official Lesson 3 Test.","steps":[{"id":"ns3-0","chapter":"Organic micronutrients, not fuel","title":"Organic micronutrients, not fuel","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> Vitamins are organic micronutrients needed in small quantities for normal physiological function — essential for growth, vitality, and health, and important as <b>coenzymes</b>. <b>Vitamins do not provide energy.</b> Alive tests this directly: \"vitamins are sources of energy\" is the false statement.</p> <p>Carbohydrate ≈ 4 kcal/g, protein ≈ 4 kcal/g, fat ≈ 9 kcal/g. Vitamins: <b>0 kcal.</b> Many B vitamins become components of coenzymes involved in reactions that let the body metabolize carbs, fats, and proteins — that's why \"B vitamins give you energy\" is a mix-up between providing energy and participating in energy metabolism.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> MACROS = FUEL · VITAMINS = HELPERS</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns3-1","chapter":"Two big vitamin families","title":"Two big vitamin families","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 Don't overgeneralize: water-soluble does NOT mean \"impossible to overdose,\" and fat-soluble does NOT mean \"automatically toxic.\" Dose matters either way.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns3-1-x","title":"Explore: Two big vitamin families","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"🥑 Fat-soluble","text":"A · D · E · K (\"ADEK\")Absorption travels with dietary lipid handling. Can be stored in tissues to varying degrees — greater potential for accumulation and toxicity for some."},{"label":"💧 Water-soluble","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns3-2","chapter":"Small intestine = absorption powerhouse","title":"Small intestine = absorption powerhouse","html":"<p>Fat-soluble vitamins follow fat's path: <b>fat → bile → micelles → intestinal absorption → transport.</b> So anything affecting fat digestion, bile, pancreatic function, or intestinal absorption can affect fat-soluble vitamin status.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns3-2-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Alive tests: \"Vitamin A is absorbed primarily from the large intestine.\" True or false?","options":[{"label":"True","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"False — the small intestine is the major nutrient-absorption site","correct":true,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns3-3","chapter":"Vision + epithelia + immunity","title":"Vision + epithelia + immunity","html":"<p>Deficiency can impair vision (especially dark adaptation), epithelial integrity, and immune function. <b>Function ≠ supplement indication</b> — \"vitamin A supports immunity\" doesn't mean \"take lots of vitamin A for immunity.\"</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns3-3-x","title":"Explore: Vision + epithelia + immunity","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"Preformed A","text":"🥚🐟 Liver, eggs, dairyRetinoids from animal-derived foods/supplements. High amounts can be toxic — including brain swelling/pressure with headaches."},{"label":"Provitamin A","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns3-4","chapter":"The hormone-like vitamin","title":"The hormone-like vitamin","html":"<p>☀️Skin</p> <p>→</p> <p>🏭Liver</p> <p>→</p> <p>🫘Kidney</p> <p>→</p> <p>🎯Target tissues</p> <p>One major role: regulating calcium and phosphorus for bone mineralization — vitamin D works best with adequate calcium/phosphorus intake. Sources: sunlight/skin synthesis, fatty fish, fortified foods, egg yolk, supplements where appropriate.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 Severe deficiency impairs bone mineralization — <b>rickets</b> in children, <b>osteomalacia</b> in adults. Excess supplemental intake (it's fat-soluble) can cause toxicity, including elevated-calcium problems. More ≠ better.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns3-5","chapter":"Antioxidant anchor","title":"Antioxidant anchor","html":"<p>Vitamin E helps protect lipids/cell membranes from oxidative damage. Alive states deficiency is uncommon (false that it's common) in generally healthy people, though it can occur in malabsorption/genetic contexts.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 💊 High-dose caution: Alive flags large doses in relation to high blood pressure, anticoagulant therapy, and vitamin K deficiency. Bigger principle: high-dose supplements can interact with medications and physiology — especially bleeding/clotting.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns3-6","chapter":"K = KLOT","title":"K = KLOT","html":"<p>Vitamin K activates proteins involved in normal blood clotting, and also participates in bone-biology proteins. Leafy greens are particularly important dietary sources.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 💊 Especially relevant with certain anticoagulant medications — medication-specific dietary consistency and medical guidance can matter. Don't independently alter anticoagulant medication or prescribe therapeutic vitamin K changes; coordinate/refer appropriately.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns3-7","chapter":"The metabolic team, part 1","title":"The metabolic team, part 1","html":"","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"The metabolic team, part 1","items":[{"label":"B1 · Thiamin","detail":"Carbohydrate metabolism + nervous system. Sources: whole/enriched grains, pork, legumes, seeds/nuts. Severe deficiency: beriberi, with neurological/cardiovascular consequences."},{"label":"B2 · Riboflavin","detail":"FAD/FMN coenzymes, oxidation-reduction reactions. Sources: dairy, eggs, meat, almonds, fortified grains. Deficiency can affect mouth/lips/tongue/skin — but one sign ≠ one deficiency."},{"label":"B3 · Niacin","detail":"NAD/NADP coenzymes. Can be made from tryptophan (Unit 2 connection). Severe deficiency: pellagra — the 3 D's: dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia. Pharmacological high doses ≠ ordinary nutritional intake."}]}},{"id":"ns3-8","chapter":"The metabolic team, part 2","title":"The metabolic team, part 2","html":"","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"The metabolic team, part 2","items":[{"label":"B5 · Pantothenic acid","detail":"Part of Coenzyme A (CoA) — central to metabolism. Alive's \"anti-stress vitamin\" / adrenal-support nickname. Don't turn this into \"you're stressed, therefore B5-deficient.\""},{"label":"B6 · Pyridoxine family","detail":"Amino acids + neurotransmitter metabolism. Alive: needed more with increased estrogen (birth control) — B6 + B2. High supplemental B6 can cause neurological toxicity — water-soluble ≠ harmless."},{"label":"B7 · Biotin","detail":"Carboxylase enzymes — fatty acids, amino acids, glucose pathways. Needed for fatty-acid synthesis. Alive links it to weight-reduction/blood-sugar claims — don't market it as a weight-loss/diabetes treatment. High-dose biotin can interfere with some lab tests."}]}},{"id":"ns3-8-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which vitamin supports the adrenal glands and is nicknamed the \"anti-stress vitamin\" in this course?","options":[{"label":"Niacin (B3)","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"Pantothenic acid (B5)","correct":true,"feedback":""},{"label":"Biotin (B7)","correct":false,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns3-9","chapter":"One of the most important comparisons in the unit","title":"One of the most important comparisons in the unit","html":"<p><b>B9/Folate:</b> DNA + cell division + blood — crucial in one-carbon metabolism, especially important when cells divide rapidly. Sources: spinach, kale, beet greens, chard, liver/kidney/brewer's yeast, lima beans, garbanzo beans, sweet potatoes.</p> <p><b>B12/Cobalamin:</b> blood + DNA + nervous system. Reliable dietary sources are animal-derived foods and fortified foods. Absorption path: food-B12 → carrier proteins → intrinsic factor (from stomach parietal cells) → absorbed in the terminal ileum. B12 status isn't only about intake — it can involve absorption too.</p> <p>🩸Both → megaloblastic anemia</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🌱 Alive: \"Vitamin B12 can be easily obtained from a vegan diet\" is <b>FALSE.</b> Your automatic vegan-client question: \"what is your reliable B12 source?\" — not \"do you eat healthy?\"</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns3-9-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"True or false: like B12, folate deficiency leads to progressive, irreversible neurological damage.","options":[{"label":"True","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"False — only B12 deficiency carries that classic neurological risk","correct":true,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns3-10","chapter":"Collagen + antioxidant + iron","title":"Collagen + antioxidant + iron","html":"<p>Sources: peppers, kiwi, strawberries, citrus, broccoli, potatoes, tomatoes — not just oranges.</p> <p>🫘Non-heme iron</p> <p>+</p> <p>🍊Vitamin C</p> <p>→</p> <p>⬆️Better absorption</p> <p>Alive's multi-part question treats all of these as correct: lower vitamin C levels in smokers, readily absorbed from the intestines, aids metabolism of tyrosine/folic acid/tryptophan, and can be depleted during stress — plus deficiency associations with smokers with poor diets and IBD.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 Severe deficiency: <b>scurvy</b> — collagen failure affecting gums, skin, blood vessels, wound healing. Don't interpret \"I'm stressed\" as \"therefore I need high-dose vitamin C.\"</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns3-11","chapter":"The supplement trap","title":"The supplement trap","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> ALIVE'S ANTIOXIDANT TRIO: A + C + E</p> <p>When reactive-species production and antioxidant defences become imbalanced, <b>oxidative stress</b> can occur. Antioxidant systems donate/accept electrons to help limit damaging chain reactions.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 The flawed logic: oxidative stress damages cells → antioxidants counter oxidation → therefore huge antioxidant doses = better health. Wrong — reactive species also have normal signalling roles, and concentrated supplement doses don't behave like antioxidant-rich foods. Antioxidant function ≠ megadose indication.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns3-12","chapter":"PABA, Vitamin P, bioflavonoids","title":"PABA, Vitamin P, bioflavonoids","html":"","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"PABA, Vitamin P, bioflavonoids","items":[{"label":"PABA","detail":"Para-aminobenzoic acid — part of the folic-acid molecule, used in sunscreen per Alive. Not treated as an essential human vitamin in modern classification."},{"label":"\"Vitamin P\"","detail":"Alive's \"universal vitalizing energy source\" tied to digestion, nervous system, endorphins, hormones — historical/course terminology generally associated with bioflavonoids, not a recognized essential vitamin."},{"label":"Bioflavonoids","detail":"Alive: \"bioflavonoids provide synergy in utilization of vitamin D\" (True/False item) — a course-specific statement, not a core modern vitamin D concept."}]}},{"id":"ns3-13","chapter":"Four concepts to actually memorize","title":"Four concepts to actually memorize","html":"","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Four concepts to actually memorize","items":[{"label":"EAR","detail":"Estimated Average Requirement — meets the needs of about half of healthy individuals in a group. Used in population assessment and to help establish an RDA."},{"label":"RDA","detail":"Recommended Dietary Allowance — meets the needs of nearly all healthy individuals. This is Alive's tested \"individual intake target.\""},{"label":"AI","detail":"Adequate Intake — used when there isn't enough evidence to establish an EAR/RDA."},{"label":"UL","detail":"Tolerable Upper Intake Level — highest average daily intake unlikely to pose risk for most people. NOT \"the recommended amount\" — it's an upper safety reference."}]}},{"id":"ns3-14","chapter":"More factors than \"not eating enough\"","title":"More factors than \"not eating enough\"","html":"<p>IntakeDigestionAbsorptionUtilizationRequirementLossesMedicationsMedical conditions</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🩺 \"I'm tired\" could relate to nutrition — but doesn't prove iron, B12, folate, or vitamin D deficiency. Symptom → assess → dietary pattern → risk factors → medical context → appropriate testing/referral → then recommendation. Not \"fatigue → B12 shot.\"</p> <p>Toxicity — TRUE in the course framework: occurs more commonly with fat-soluble vitamins (ADEK have greater storage/accumulation potential). But water-soluble ≠ zero toxicity:</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"More factors than \"not eating enough\"","items":[{"label":"A","detail":"Preformed A toxicity."},{"label":"D","detail":"Hypercalcemia-related toxicity at excessive doses."},{"label":"E","detail":"High-dose bleeding/interaction risk."},{"label":"B6","detail":"Neuropathy with excessive supplemental exposure."}]}},{"id":"ns3-15","chapter":"The 10-point supplement decision model","title":"The 10-point supplement decision model","html":"<p><p>1</p><p>Why? What problem/goal are we addressing?</p> <p>2</p><p>Food? What's their actual intake?</p> <p>3</p><p>Risk? Is deficiency/insufficiency plausible?</p> <p>4</p><p>Evidence? Does supplementation make sense?</p> <p>5</p><p>Dose? Nutritional or high therapeutic dose?</p> <p>6</p><p>Form? Tablet, capsule, liquid, powder?</p> <p>7</p><p>Duplication? Multivitamin + B complex + individual vitamins?</p> <p>8</p><p>Medications? Possible interaction?</p> <p>9</p><p>Medical conditions? Could they change safety/absorption?</p> <p>10</p><p>Monitor/refer? Do we need labs or another professional?</p> </p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🔥 Alive says checking blood levels before starting a supplement program is good practice, and flags <b>very large time-release vitamins</b> as an incorrect recommendation. It also says active people, people under stress, people dieting, and people in life transitions (childhood/pregnancy) \"may need\" supplements — all true circumstances that can change needs, but none of them automatically means \"supplement required.\" Assess first.</p>","visual":null}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Do vitamins provide calories/energy?","back":"No."},{"front":"Fat-soluble vitamins?","back":"A, D, E, K."},{"front":"Water-soluble vitamins?","back":"B vitamins + C."},{"front":"Hormone-like vitamin?","back":"D."},{"front":"Alive antioxidant trio?","back":"A + C + E."},{"front":"Vitamin A anchor?","back":"Vision + epithelial/immune function."},{"front":"Vitamin D partner minerals?","back":"Calcium + phosphorus."},{"front":"Vitamin E anchor?","back":"Antioxidant."},{"front":"Vitamin K anchor?","back":"Clotting."},{"front":"B1?","back":"Thiamin."},{"front":"B2?","back":"Riboflavin."},{"front":"B3?","back":"Niacin."},{"front":"B5?","back":"Pantothenic acid."},{"front":"B6?","back":"Pyridoxine family."},{"front":"B7?","back":"Biotin."},{"front":"B9?","back":"Folate."},{"front":"B12?","back":"Cobalamin."},{"front":"Alive \"anti-stress vitamin\"?","back":"B5."},{"front":"Biotin major exam association?","back":"Fatty-acid synthesis."},{"front":"Folate deficiency?","back":"Megaloblastic anemia."},{"front":"B12 deficiency?","back":"Megaloblastic anemia + neurological risk."},{"front":"Reliable B12 in vegan diet automatically?","back":"No — requires attention to fortified sources/supplementation."},{"front":"Vitamin C + plant iron?","back":"Enhances non-heme iron absorption."},{"front":"RDA?","back":"Individual intake goal for nearly all healthy people in group."},{"front":"AI?","back":"Used when RDA can't be established."},{"front":"EAR?","back":"Requirement estimate for ~50% of group."},{"front":"UL?","back":"Upper intake safety reference."},{"front":"PABA?","back":"Alive B-vitamin-related/sunscreen association."},{"front":"Vitamin P?","back":"Historical/course bioflavonoid terminology."},{"front":"Where is most vitamin absorption?","back":"Small intestine."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of the following statements about vitamins is FALSE?","o":["Vitamins are sources of energy","Vitamins function principally as coenzymes","Vitamins are essential for growth, vitality, and health","A variety of nutritional disorders and health problems can result from vitamin deficiencies","None of the above are incorrect"],"a":0,"e":"Vitamins do not provide calories/energy — carbohydrates, protein, and fat do. Vitamins work as helpers/coenzymes instead."},{"q":"Which vitamins have antioxidant properties?","o":["Vitamins A, E, and C","Vitamins A, C, and D","Vitamins A, E, and K","Vitamins C, E, and D"],"a":0,"e":"A, C, and E are the antioxidant trio explicitly tested by Alive."},{"q":"Functionally, which vitamin is more like a hormone than a vitamin in that it is produced in one part of the body and released into the blood to affect other tissues?","o":["A","D","E","C"],"a":1,"e":"Vitamin D is produced via UVB exposure on skin and processed into an active hormone-like signalling molecule."},{"q":"Deficiency of which of the following vitamins can cause megaloblastic anemia?","o":["A","Inositol","B12","Folic Acid","Both B12 and Folic Acid"],"a":4,"e":"Both B12 and folate deficiency can cause megaloblastic anemia — though only B12 deficiency also causes neurological damage."},{"q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["The primary function of vitamin E is as an antioxidant","Vitamin D works best with adequate calcium and phosphorus intake","Vitamin K is necessary for normal blood clotting","B vitamins are not stored very well in the body","Like B12, folic acid deficiency can lead to progressive and irreversible neurological damage"],"a":4,"e":"This is the statement Alive specifically treats as false — folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia, but unlike B12 deficiency, it is not linked to the same progressive, irreversible neurological damage."},{"q":"Which of the following recommendations would be considered incorrect?","o":["It is good to get one's blood levels checked for deficiencies prior to implementing a supplement program","Take Calcium and magnesium before bed","Take water soluble vitamins with food","Take very large time-release vitamins","None of the recommendations are incorrect"],"a":3,"e":"Very large, time-release vitamin doses are generally discouraged — the other recommendations (testing first, calcium/magnesium timing, water-soluble vitamins with food) are considered sound."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding Vitamin C is correct?","o":["Vitamin C levels are lower in smokers than in non-smokers","It is readily absorbed from the intestines","It aids the metabolism of tyrosine, folic acid, and tryptophan","Vitamin C can be depleted in the body during times of stress","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All four statements about vitamin C are accurate per the course material."},{"q":"Which B vitamin that is part of the folic acid molecule has been used in sunscreen?","o":["Pangamic acid","Para-aminobenzoic acid","Inositol","Biotin"],"a":1,"e":"PABA (para-aminobenzoic acid) is structurally part of the folic acid molecule and has historically been used in sunscreen formulations."},{"q":"Smokers with poor diets and people with inflammatory bowel disease more often have a deficiency in which vitamin?","o":["A","D","B12","C","K"],"a":3,"e":"The course specifically ties vitamin C deficiency to smokers with poor diets and people with inflammatory bowel disease."},{"q":"For which of the following are large doses of vitamin E NOT recommended?","o":["People with high blood pressure","People undergoing anticoagulant therapy","People with a vitamin K deficiency","A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"The course cautions against large-dose vitamin E for people with high blood pressure, those on anticoagulant therapy, and those with vitamin K deficiency."},{"q":"Which vitamin supports the adrenal glands and has come to be known as the anti-stress vitamin?","o":["Pantothenic acid","Niacin","Thiamin","Choline"],"a":0,"e":"Pantothenic acid (B5) is nicknamed the 'anti-stress vitamin' and associated with adrenal support in this course."},{"q":"During times of increased estrogen levels in the body (such as while taking birth control pills), the body requires more of which vitamin?","o":["B12 and B2","B6 and B2","C and B5","B1 and D"],"a":1,"e":"Increased estrogen (e.g. from oral contraceptives) is commonly taught to increase the need for B6 and B2."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about natural vitamins is FALSE?","o":["Vitamins are considered natural when they are extracted exclusively from food sources and contain the specific mix of enzymes, catalysts, or minerals to aid the body’s use of the vitamins","The amounts of individual nutrients in purely natural vitamins are usually not very high","There is no legal definition of \"natural\" and products bearing this label may or may not meet the criteria of “natural”","Natural vitamins are made from organic sources"],"a":3,"e":"“Natural” and “organic” are different regulatory concepts — natural vitamins aren't necessarily made from certified organic sources, making this statement false."},{"q":"What vitamin, considered the universal vitalizing energy source is crucial in digestion, functioning of the nervous system and enhancement of endorphins and hormones?","o":["A","C","D","P","L"],"a":3,"e":"This references an older, non-standard 'vitamin' naming scheme (Vitamin P) not covered in the course write-up — verify this one against the official answer key."},{"q":"Which of the following are sources of folic acid?","o":["Spinach, kale, beet greens, and chard","Liver, kidney, and brewer's yeast","Lima beans, garbanzo beans, and sweet potatoes","A and C","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Leafy greens, organ meats/brewer's yeast, and legumes are all recognized folate food sources."},{"q":"Which vitamin has commonly been used to help normalize fat metabolism in weight-reduction programs and to help reduce blood sugar in diabetic patients?","o":["Bioflavonoids","Pantothenic acid","Biotin","Folic acid"],"a":2,"e":"Biotin is involved in fatty acid synthesis/fat metabolism and has been studied in relation to blood sugar regulation in diabetic patients."},{"q":"Dietary guidelines that are intended to be used primarily as a goal for daily intake by individuals are the:","o":["AI recommendations","EARs","RDAs","UL"],"a":2,"e":"RDA (Recommended Dietary Allowance) is designed as the daily intake goal for nearly all healthy individuals."},{"q":"The practice of using large doses of vitamins and minerals in conjunction with therapy for psychiatric conditions, such as depression and schizophrenia, is called:","o":["Megavitamin therapy","Orthomolecular medicine","Chelation therapy","Orthomolecular psychiatry","None of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Orthomolecular psychiatry is the specific term for megadose vitamin/mineral approaches alongside therapy for psychiatric conditions."},{"q":"Who may need to supplement with vitamins?","o":["Active people","People going through times of stress","People who are dieting","People going through life transitions, such as childhood or pregnancy","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All of these groups are noted as populations who may benefit from supplementation."},{"q":"Biotin is needed for the synthesis of fatty acids.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Biotin is a required cofactor for fatty acid synthesis."},{"q":"Vitamin A is absorbed primarily from the large intestine.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Vitamin A, being fat-soluble, is absorbed primarily in the small intestine, not the large intestine."},{"q":"Injections of B12 in doses of 500-1000 mcg can be used to treat a variety of low-energy and mental symptoms.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This is course-tested content — treat it as exam knowledge rather than an individualized clinical recommendation."},{"q":"Vitamin E deficiency is common.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Vitamin E deficiency is uncommon in typical diets."},{"q":"Folic acid deficiency symptoms are similar to that of a B12 deficiency.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Both produce megaloblastic anemia, so the anemia-related symptoms overlap — even though the neurological effects differ."},{"q":"Too much preformed vitamin A can result in slight swelling of the brain and resulting pressure headaches.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This is the specific toxicity mechanism the course tests for excess preformed vitamin A."},{"q":"Bioflavonoids provide synergy in the utilization of vitamin D.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Bioflavonoids are classically paired with vitamin C utilization/absorption, not vitamin D."},{"q":"Toxic levels of vitamins occur more commonly in fat-soluble vitamins.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Fat-soluble vitamins (ADEK) can accumulate in the body, making toxicity more of a concern than with water-soluble vitamins."},{"q":"For many children or elders, more easily digested and absorbed supplements, such as a liquid or powdered vitamin/mineral combination, may be advantageous.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Liquid or powdered forms can be easier to digest and absorb for populations with digestive limitations."},{"q":"Vitamin B12 can be easily obtained from a vegan diet.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"B12 is concentrated in animal foods, so vegan diets require specific attention to this nutrient — it is not easily obtained without fortified foods or supplementation."},{"q":"Vitamins are micronutrients.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Vitamins are classified as micronutrients, needed in small amounts relative to macronutrients."}],"perStepChapter":false},{"n":4,"title":"Minerals","kicker":"Lesson 4 of your course, with your official Lesson 4 Test.","steps":[{"id":"ns4-0","chapter":"Inorganic, but essential","title":"Inorganic, but essential","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> <b>Minerals do not provide energy</b> — but they help the body produce and use it. No magnesium calories, no calcium calories, no iron calories, but without them metabolism doesn't function normally. Alive tests mineral involvement in energy production, fluid regulation, and acid-base balance.</p> <p>An <b>essential</b> mineral is one the body needs but cannot make itself — the same idea as essential amino acids or essential fatty acids. Essential ≠ \"extra important\"; it means the body needs an external (dietary) source.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🍽️ <b>Food first:</b> Alive emphasizes a variety of wholesome foods (with a preference for organic/local produce and grains) as the healthiest approach to optimal mineral levels. Food first, supplements when justified — because minerals interact, and more calcium, zinc, or iron isn't always better.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns4-1","chapter":"Grouped by amount, not importance","title":"Grouped by amount, not importance","html":"<p>Minerals are grouped by how much the body needs — <b>trace ≠ unimportant.</b> You need only tiny amounts of iodine, but inadequate iodine can profoundly affect thyroid function.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns4-1-x","title":"Explore: Grouped by amount, not importance","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"🪨 Major minerals","text":"Ca · P · Mg · Na · K · Cl · SRequired in relatively larger quantities."},{"label":"🔬 Trace minerals","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns4-2","chapter":"Structure + signal","title":"Structure + signal","html":"<p>🔥 <b>Calcium is the most abundant mineral in the human body</b> — Alive asks this directly. About 99% is stored in bones and teeth, but the remaining fraction is enormously important: muscle contraction, nerve signalling, blood clotting, and cellular signalling.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> CALCIUM = STRUCTURE + SIGNAL</p> <p>☀️Vitamin D</p> <p>→</p> <p>🦴Calcium absorption</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 📊 <b>Alive's tested figure:</b> the average adult absorbs <b>20–30%</b> of dietary calcium. Practitioner brain: this isn't a fixed percentage — it varies with age, vitamin D status, physiological need, dose, and food matrix.</p> <p><b>Phosphorus</b> works closely with calcium in bones/teeth, and also participates in energy metabolism, cell structures, phospholipid membranes, muscle contraction, heartbeat, and nerve conduction.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🦴 <b>Bone health is not just calcium:</b> it's calcium + vitamin D + protein + physical loading + hormonal/life-stage factors + overall nutrition. Especially relevant for women 40+.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns4-3","chapter":"The metabolic manager","title":"The metabolic manager","html":"<p>Magnesium is a cofactor in <b>hundreds of enzymatic reactions</b> — ATP/energy metabolism, muscle function, nerve function, bone, glucose metabolism, and cardiovascular physiology.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> Mg = MANY ENZYMES</p> <p>Food sources: seeds, nuts, legumes, whole grains, leafy greens — whole plant foods → mineral density.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> ⚖️ Magnesium interacts with calcium, phosphorus, potassium, and sodium chloride — minerals interact, which matters a lot once supplements enter the picture. Alive calls magnesium a \"natural tranquilizer\"/\"anti-stress mineral\" — know the term for the exam, but don't teach it to clients as established physiology. Possible early deficiency symptoms include fatigue, appetite loss, irritability, insomnia, tremors, and twitching — but nonspecific symptom ≠ mineral deficiency; assess first.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns4-4","chapter":"The electrolyte team","title":"The electrolyte team","html":"<p>One of the most important diagrams in this unit:</p> <p> Na⁺ + Cl⁻ — outside the cell K⁺ — inside the cell </p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> Na OUT · K IN · Cl FOLLOWS Na</p> <p>Potassium is specifically linked with glucose/glycogen metabolism. Chloride travels primarily with sodium and water, helps generate osmotic pressure, and is eliminated through the kidneys and lost with sodium in sweat.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🟡 Know that for Alive — but don't reduce hypertension to a single mineral deficiency. Blood pressure involves sodium intake, potassium-rich foods, overall dietary pattern, body composition, activity, alcohol, and medical factors together.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns4-4-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Alive's course concept for a mineral deficiency that \"may contribute to hypertension\" is:","options":[{"label":"Iron","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"Calcium","correct":true,"feedback":""},{"label":"Zinc","correct":false,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns4-5","chapter":"Iron = oxygen delivery","title":"Iron = oxygen delivery","html":"<p>Iron is a key component of <b>hemoglobin</b>, which carries oxygen in red blood cells to tissues.</p> <p>🫘Non-heme iron</p> <p>+</p> <p>🍊Vitamin C</p> <p>→</p> <p>⬆️Better absorption</p> <p>Factors that can negatively affect iron absorption: coffee, calcium, antacids, low copper.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 <b>Iron deficiency ≠ all anemia:</b> Alive's statement \"iron deficiency is the sole cause of anemia\" is <b>false</b> — there are many possible causes. When a client says \"I'm tired, I think I'm iron deficient,\" the answer isn't \"take iron\" — it's assess → labs/medical evaluation when appropriate → intervene within scope. And excess iron can also be harmful: deficiency ≠ bad, more ≠ better — the goal is adequacy.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns4-5-x","title":"Explore: Iron = oxygen delivery","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"Heme iron","text":"🥩Found in animal-derived foods — generally more bioavailable."},{"label":"Non-heme iron","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns4-6","chapter":"Repair, immunity, and thyroid enzymes","title":"Repair, immunity, and thyroid enzymes","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> ⚠️ <b>Zinc ↔ copper:</b> high supplemental zinc over time can interfere with copper status — \"zinc helps immunity\" does not mean \"take huge doses of zinc.\" Alive pairs low zinc and selenium with impaired immune function — true as a concept, but \"take selenium and zinc to boost your immune system\" overstates it.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Repair, immunity, and thyroid enzymes","items":[{"label":"Zinc","detail":"DNA/protein synthesis, immune function, wound healing, taste/smell, growth. Alive links zinc to T-lymphocyte production and white-blood-cell function, and notes zinc is best absorbed from animal foods — plant zinc can be less bioavailable due to phytates."},{"label":"Selenium","detail":"Incorporated into selenoproteins — antioxidant systems and thyroid hormone metabolism. Brazil nuts are the classic food association, but concentration varies enormously, so \"eat a handful daily\" isn't a safe universal rule."}]}},{"id":"ns4-7","chapter":"Iodine → thyroid hormones","title":"Iodine → thyroid hormones","html":"<p>The thyroid needs iodine to synthesize thyroid hormones — Alive directly tests this relationship. Iodine deficiency can lead to <b>goiter</b> and impaired thyroid hormone production.</p> <p>🧂 Iodized salt🐟 Seafood🌊 Seaweed</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> ⚠️ <b>The seaweed trap:</b> seaweed can contain very large and variable amounts of iodine, so \"thyroid problem → take kelp\" is not a safe universal recommendation. Both inadequate <i>and</i> excessive iodine can disrupt thyroid function in susceptible people.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> IODINE = THYROID, NOT \"MORE = BETTER\"</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns4-8","chapter":"Simplifying the rest of the list","title":"Simplifying the rest of the list","html":" <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 💊 <b>Lithium:</b> Alive calls lithium a trace mineral and asks which one is used mainly to treat \"manic-depressive disorder\" — the course answer is lithium. 🚨 Pharmaceutical lithium for bipolar disorder is a prescription medication and medical/psychiatric treatment — not something you'd address nutritionally with lithium supplements.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Simplifying the rest of the list","items":[{"label":"Manganese","detail":"Enzyme cofactor. Alive tests nuts and whole grains as sources."},{"label":"Copper","detail":"Enzymes, iron metabolism, connective tissue, nervous-system biology. Remember zinc ↔ copper: excess zinc supplementation can reduce copper status."},{"label":"Molybdenum","detail":"Cofactor for a small number of enzymes. Alive gives a true/false item involving molybdenum and nitrosamines — exam-trap territory more than core clinical concept."},{"label":"Chromium","detail":"Associated with carbohydrate/glucose metabolism in the course — but supplement marketing has gone further than the evidence. Know the material; don't treat it as a diabetes/weight-loss treatment."},{"label":"Silicon","detail":"Associated with nails and connective tissue. Its clinical significance is less established than major minerals like calcium or essential trace minerals like iodine."},{"label":"Boron","detail":"May influence bone/mineral metabolism. Alive asks whether boron upsets calcium balance and increases osteoporosis risk — exam-trap territory, not a major practitioner intervention."}]}},{"id":"ns4-9","chapter":"Minerals don't just \"eat → absorb 100%\"","title":"Minerals don't just \"eat → absorb 100%\"","html":"<p><p>1</p><p>🍽️ Food → digestion</p> <p>2</p><p>⚗️ Mineral released</p> <p>3</p><p>🌀 Intestine → absorption</p> <p>4</p><p>🩸 Blood → storage/use</p> </p> <p>Absorption is influenced by food form, nutrient status, gut function, enhancers, inhibitors, other minerals, and dose.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🌾 <b>Refining connects back to Unit 1:</b> whole grain → refining → bran + germ removed → some fibre + vitamins + minerals lost. Alive asks which minerals are lost in the greatest amounts during wheat milling.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Minerals don't just \"eat → absorb 100%\"","items":[{"label":"Vitamin D + Calcium","detail":"Vitamin D supports calcium absorption."},{"label":"Vitamin C + Iron","detail":"Vitamin C supports non-heme iron absorption."},{"label":"Zinc ↔ Copper","detail":"High supplemental zinc can impair copper status."},{"label":"Calcium ↔ other minerals","detail":"Large calcium amounts can reduce absorption of magnesium, phosphorus, zinc, and manganese — dose and context matter, this doesn't mean \"never eat calcium with other minerals.\""}]}},{"id":"ns4-10","chapter":"A completely different category","title":"A completely different category","html":"<p>These aren't nutrients to optimize — think exposure → reduce/identify → medical/public-health management when needed.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"A completely different category","items":[{"label":"Lead","detail":"Particularly concerning for children. Alive discusses screening children with hyperactivity/learning problems, and notes calcium inhibiting lead absorption. Nutrition does not \"detox\" a significant lead exposure — priorities are identify exposure → remove/control source → appropriate testing → medical/public-health management."},{"label":"Aluminum","detail":"Alive's course association is with brain-aging disorders such as Alzheimer's. Know the course answer — but this causal conclusion is not established; association/course wording ≠ established causation."},{"label":"Mercury / Thimerosal","detail":"Alive states thimerosal is \"an ethyl form of mercury used in many vaccinations.\" Thimerosal is an ethylmercury-containing preservative, but the \"many vaccinations\" framing is outdated/overbroad and shouldn't be used to imply routine vaccination is a mercury-toxicity problem. Know the course wording; don't use this as health counselling about vaccines."}]}},{"id":"ns4-11","chapter":"Functional families, not 20 random substances","title":"Functional families, not 20 random substances","html":"<p>🦴Structure: Ca + P</p> <p>⚡Electricity: Na, K, Cl, Mg, Ca</p> <p>🩸Oxygen: Fe</p> <p>🦋Thyroid: I + Se</p> <p>🛡️Repair/immunity: Zn</p> <p>⚙️Enzyme helpers: Mg, Zn, Se, Cu, Mn, Mo</p>","visual":null}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Most abundant body mineral?","back":"Calcium."},{"front":"Vitamin needed for calcium absorption?","back":"Vitamin D."},{"front":"Alive's average calcium absorption figure?","back":"20–30%."},{"front":"Major intracellular electrolyte?","back":"Potassium."},{"front":"Major extracellular cation?","back":"Sodium."},{"front":"Electrolyte travelling with sodium/water?","back":"Chloride."},{"front":"Mineral strongly associated with oxygen transport?","back":"Iron."},{"front":"Better-absorbed dietary iron form?","back":"Heme iron."},{"front":"Vitamin enhancing non-heme iron absorption?","back":"Vitamin C."},{"front":"Mineral required for thyroid hormones?","back":"Iodine."},{"front":"Mineral associated with wound healing/immune function?","back":"Zinc."},{"front":"Mineral involved in antioxidant and thyroid enzymes?","back":"Selenium."},{"front":"Magnesium's broad role?","back":"Enzyme cofactor + nerve/muscle function."},{"front":"Food groups rich in magnesium?","back":"Nuts, seeds, legumes, whole grains, greens."},{"front":"Zinc absorption generally higher from?","back":"Animal foods."},{"front":"Long-term excessive zinc can interfere with?","back":"Copper."},{"front":"Trace mineral Alive associates with connective tissue/nails?","back":"Silicon."},{"front":"Alive's psychiatric trace-mineral answer?","back":"Lithium."},{"front":"Mineral Alive says inhibits lead absorption?","back":"Calcium."},{"front":"Metal Alive associates with Alzheimer's?","back":"Aluminum — course association, not established causation."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of the following statements are true regarding minerals?","o":["They are hard to liberate from food during digestion therefore low amounts are absorbed","Deficiencies of minerals may be more common than vitamin deficiencies","They assist the body in energy production","Some minerals help regulate fluid and acid base balance in our bodies","All of the statements are true"],"a":4,"e":"Minerals assist in energy production and fluid/acid-base regulation, and the course also teaches that they can be hard to liberate from food and that mineral deficiencies may be more common than vitamin deficiencies."},{"q":"A mineral is considered essential if:","o":["Deficiency symptoms are seen when it is lacking in the diet","It can be produced in sufficient quantities in our body","It is not easily absorbed","It is a necessary component of another essential nutrient","A and D"],"a":4,"e":"Essentiality criteria: deficiency symptoms appear when it's absent from the diet, and/or it's a necessary component of another essential nutrient — not that the body can make it itself (that would make it non-essential)."},{"q":"Large amounts of which mineral can reduce absorption of magnesium, phosphorus, zinc, and manganese?","o":["Copper","Calcium","Iron","Cobalt"],"a":1,"e":"Large amounts of calcium can reduce absorption of magnesium, phosphorus, zinc, and manganese — a key mineral-interaction concept."},{"q":"What is the healthiest approach for ensuring optimum mineral levels?","o":["Taking mineral supplements","Eating a variety of wholesome foods","Eating organic, local produce and grains","B and C","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"The course frames food variety plus organic/local produce and grains — not supplements — as the healthiest approach, i.e. food first."},{"q":"A deficiency of which mineral may contribute to hypertension?","o":["Calcium","Sodium","Zinc","Chromium"],"a":0,"e":"Calcium is the course concept linked to hypertension risk when deficient."},{"q":"To function optimally, magnesium must be balanced in the body with what other minerals?","o":["Calcium, phosphorus, potassium, and sodium chloride","Calcium, phosphorus, potassium, and chromium","Calcium, selenium, potassium, and sodium chloride","Calcium, selenium, potassium, and chromium"],"a":0,"e":"Magnesium's tested balance partners are calcium, phosphorus, potassium, and sodium chloride."},{"q":"Low levels of which two minerals may hinder the immune system and make us prone to infection?","o":["Zinc and selenium","Sodium and potassium","Calcium and magnesium","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"Zinc and selenium are paired with immune function in this course."},{"q":"Which electrolyte mineral travels primarily with sodium and water and helps generate the osmotic pressure of body fluids?","o":["Calcium","Magnesium","Chloride","Copper"],"a":2,"e":"Chloride travels with sodium and water and helps generate osmotic pressure."},{"q":"Which of the following minerals are lost in the highest amounts in wheat milling?","o":["Magnesium, zinc, and chromium","Magnesium, manganese, and sodium","Manganese, chromium, and magnesium","Manganese, potassium, and iron"],"a":2,"e":"Manganese, chromium, and magnesium are commonly cited as showing the steepest percentage losses during wheat refining/milling."},{"q":"Which is the most abundant mineral in the human body?","o":["Magnesium","Phosphorus","Calcium","Sodium","A and C"],"a":2,"e":"Calcium alone is the most abundant mineral in the body — about 99% is in bones and teeth."},{"q":"Which vitamin is the most essential to calcium absorption?","o":["A","C","D","B6"],"a":2,"e":"Vitamin D is most directly tied to calcium absorption."},{"q":"An average adult will use/absorb what amount of calcium obtained by diet alone?","o":["20-30%","30-50%","50-70%","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"20-30% is the course's tested average calcium absorption figure."},{"q":"Which of the following statements concerning minerals is FALSE?","o":["Magnesium is a natural tranquilizer and is considered the anti-stress mineral","Phosphorus aids muscle contraction including the regularity of the heartbeat, and is supportive of proper nerve conduction","Potassium is a part of glycogen and glucose metabolism, converting glucose to glycogen","A diet too high in chromium has been linked to adult onset diabetes","None of the above are false"],"a":3,"e":"The established concept runs the other way — low/inadequate chromium status, not excess chromium, is the one linked to impaired glucose tolerance and diabetes risk — making this statement the false one."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding chloride is correct?","o":["It is obtained primarily from salts","It is eliminated through the kidneys","It helps replace sodium chloride lost in perspiration on hot days or with exercise","A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Chloride is obtained primarily from salt, eliminated through the kidneys, and dietary chloride helps replace losses from sweat."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding magnesium are correct?","o":["Magnesium can help alleviate PMS","Early symptoms of a deficiency can include fatigue, anorexia, irritability, insomnia, and muscle tremors or twitching","Magnesium is given as part of a treatment for autism or hyperactivity in children, usually along with vitamin B6","A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"This is course-tested content across all three claims. The PMS and autism/hyperactivity claims in particular are exam content, not something to translate directly into clinical recommendations — see the evidence check for this unit."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about iodine is FALSE?","o":["Iodine deficiency can lead to goiter and hypothyroidism","Iodine is an essential nutrient for production of the body's thyroid hormones","Iodine requirements can be met by eating seafood, seaweed, and vegetables grown in iodine-rich soil","Iodine solutions are commonly used as antiseptics and can kill bacteria and fungi","Iodine is absorbed in the small intestine and about 30% goes to the thyroid gland"],"a":4,"e":"Iodine is absorbed very rapidly, largely in the stomach and upper GI tract rather than primarily the small intestine — this specific absorption-site detail is the most likely inaccuracy among these statements."},{"q":"Which mineral has been shown to increase T lymphocytes production and enhance other white blood cell functions, thereby improving low immunity levels?","o":["Iron","Chloride","Chromium","Zinc"],"a":3,"e":"Zinc is linked to T-lymphocyte production and white blood cell function."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about manganese is FALSE?","o":["It activates the enzymes necessary for the body to use biotin, thiamin, vitamin C, and choline","It is involved in many enzyme systems helping to catalyze many bio-chemical reactions","Large amounts of calcium and/or phosphorus interfere with manganese absorption","Nuts and whole grains are the best sources of manganese","Manganese along with calcium helps decrease copper levels"],"a":4,"e":"The zinc–copper interaction is the well-established mineral pairing (excess zinc reduces copper status) — manganese and calcium reducing copper isn't an established pairing, making this the false statement."},{"q":"Antacids, calcium, coffee, and low copper are a few of the factors that can negatively affect absorption of which mineral?","o":["Magnesium","Iron","Selenium","Zinc"],"a":1,"e":"These are the specific factors the course lists as negatively affecting iron absorption."},{"q":"High levels of which mineral has been associated with schizophrenia, learning disabilities, and senility?","o":["Selenium","Chromium","Copper","Iodine","Boron"],"a":2,"e":"High copper is the course's association with these psychiatric/neurocognitive conditions — course wording, not an established diagnostic link."},{"q":"Low levels of which mineral have been linked with higher cancer rates?","o":["Chromium","Selenium","Fluoride","Iron"],"a":1,"e":"Low selenium is the course's association with higher cancer rates — an association, not evidence that supplementation prevents cancer."},{"q":"Which mineral gives strength and stability to the nails and connective tissues?","o":["Zinc","Boron","Cadmium","Silicon"],"a":3,"e":"Silicon is associated with nails and connective tissue in this course."},{"q":"Which trace mineral is used mainly for treating manic-depressive disorder?","o":["Germanium","Vanadium","Lithium","Strontium"],"a":2,"e":"Lithium is the course's answer — note that pharmaceutical lithium for bipolar disorder is a prescription medication, not a nutritional supplement matter."},{"q":"To reduce lead toxicity, which mineral can help inhibit lead absorption?","o":["Calcium","Manganese","Selenium","Phosphorus"],"a":0,"e":"Calcium is the course's mineral for inhibiting lead absorption — supportive, not a substitute for exposure control and medical management."},{"q":"Which metal has been implicated in brain-aging disorders, such as Alzheimer's?","o":["Arsenic","Aluminum","Cadmium","Lead","Mercury"],"a":1,"e":"Aluminum is the course's association with Alzheimer's — course wording, not an established causal link."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about iron are FALSE?","o":["Heme iron is generally considered the best absorbed","Iron overload can disrupt immune function and metabolic events in the body","Iron deficiency is the sole cause of anemia","Vitamin C assists with iron absorption","Chelated iron is the best assimilated and easiest on the intestinal tract"],"a":2,"e":"The course explicitly marks \"iron deficiency is the sole cause of anemia\" as false — there are many possible causes of anemia."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about lead is correct?","o":["Children showing signs of hyperactivity or poor learning should be screened for lead","A high-calcium diet or supplemental calcium inhibits lead absorption","Vitamin C helps improve elimination of lead","A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All three claims appear in the course material. Remember: nutrition is supportive here, not a substitute for exposure control and medical management of significant lead exposure."},{"q":"Thimerosal is an ethyl form of mercury used in many vaccinations.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This is the course's stated wording — though \"many vaccinations\" is an outdated/overbroad framing that shouldn't be used for vaccine health counselling."},{"q":"Molybdenum can increase the production and actions of nitrosamines, which are known cancer-causing chemicals.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"The research this course concept draws from generally links LOW molybdenum status (not high molybdenum) to increased nitrosamine-related cancer risk — molybdenum-dependent enzymes are involved in nitrate/nitrite metabolism, so adequate molybdenum is protective rather than nitrosamine-promoting."},{"q":"Aspartate or citrate salts of calcium are highly absorbable forms of calcium.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Calcium citrate and calcium aspartate are commonly cited as well-absorbed (chelated) forms of calcium, compared with less-soluble forms like calcium carbonate."},{"q":"It is best not to take iron with vitamin E.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This is a commonly taught supplement-interaction caution — iron can oxidize vitamin E, reducing its effectiveness when taken together in high doses."},{"q":"Zinc is best absorbed from animal foods.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Zinc is best absorbed from animal foods; plant-based zinc sources contain phytates that can reduce bioavailability."},{"q":"Cooking with fluoridated water in aluminum cookware increases the aluminum in the water and the food.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Fluoride can increase the leaching of aluminum from cookware into food and water during cooking."},{"q":"Selenium appears to help stimulate antibody formation in response to vaccines, which is also enhanced by vitamin E.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Selenium and vitamin E have been studied together for supporting antibody/immune response — course-tested content."},{"q":"The best way to increase levels of silicon in the body is to eat less fibre.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Silicon is found in many high-fibre whole plant foods (whole grains, oats) — eating less fibre would generally reduce rather than increase silicon intake."},{"q":"Boron upsets calcium balance, increasing the risk of osteoporosis.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Current understanding generally suggests boron supports rather than disrupts calcium/bone metabolism, making this statement false — though the course frames it as a trap question worth knowing either way."}],"perStepChapter":false},{"n":5,"title":"Special Supplements","kicker":"Lesson 5 of your course, with your official Lesson 5 Test.","steps":[{"id":"ns5-0","chapter":"Not all the same type of substance","title":"Not all the same type of substance","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> MAGNESIUM | MELATONIN | OREGANO | PROBIOTIC | CoQ10 — a supplement shelf makes them all look equivalent. Physiologically and clinically, they're very different: a mineral, a hormone, an herb, a microorganism, and an enzyme cofactor.</p>  <p><p>1</p><p>What is it? Vitamin, mineral, herb, hormone, amino acid, fatty acid, microorganism, enzyme?</p> <p>2</p><p>What is the claim? Sleep, digestion, deficiency, pain, disease treatment?</p> <p>3</p><p>What's the evidence?</p> <p>4</p><p>What's the dose?</p> <p>5</p><p>What are the risks?</p> <p>6</p><p>Medication interactions?</p> <p>7</p><p>Is this within my scope?</p> </p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns5-1","chapter":"Lab activity ≠ clinical treatment","title":"Lab activity ≠ clinical treatment","html":"","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Lab activity ≠ clinical treatment","items":[{"label":"Bromelain","detail":"Protein-digesting (proteolytic) enzymes from pineapple. Alive associates it with anti-inflammatory use and chronic bronchitis/sinusitis — don't jump to \"bromelain treats bronchitis.\""},{"label":"Oregano","detail":"Contains carvacrol and thymol. Alive frames it as an \"antibiotic herb\" for Candida, compared with prescription antifungals. Lab antimicrobial activity ≠ clinically proven replacement for prescription treatment."},{"label":"Garlic","detail":"Alliin → allicin when crushed/chopped. Alive asks which herb works \"as effectively as prescription antibiotics\" — garlic is the answer, but garlic ≠ a replacement for prescribed antibiotics."},{"label":"Licorice","detail":"Alive lists it as cough suppressant, expectorant, anti-inflammatory, anti-arthritic, antibiotic, possibly anti-cancer. Contains glycyrrhizin — pharmacologically active, can affect blood pressure, potassium, and fluid balance. \"It's an herb\" ≠ \"it's harmless tea.\""},{"label":"Echinacea","detail":"Immune-related herbal use; Alive cautions limiting duration (3-4 weeks) due to possible liver irritation or changes to intestinal flora. Herbs can have side effects."},{"label":"Lysine + HSV-1","detail":"Alive's amino acid for oral herpes. Lysine is an amino acid, not an antibiotic medication — research exists on recurrent HSV, but it isn't equivalent to antiviral drug treatment."}]}},{"id":"ns5-2","chapter":"Handle the word \"detox\" carefully","title":"Handle the word \"detox\" carefully","html":"<p>The liver genuinely participates in metabolism and elimination of substances. But \"your liver is full of toxins, take this herb\" is a different, unsupported claim.</p> <p>🏭Liver produces bile</p> <p>→</p> <p>🟡Stored in gallbladder</p> <p>→</p> <p>🥑Emulsifies fat (Unit 2)</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🌿 <b>Milk thistle</b> is Alive's course association as \"top liver detoxifier\" (contains silymarin, studied in liver-related contexts) — know it for the exam, but don't diagnose \"liver toxicity\" or promise it \"cleanses\" the liver. 🟡 <b>Bile-flow herbs:</b> dandelion root + artichoke leaves + turmeric. 🚨 <b>Safety matters:</b> someone with gallstones, biliary obstruction, significant abdominal pain, or liver disease needs medical assessment, not bile-flow herbs.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns5-3","chapter":"Reconnecting with Unit 2","title":"Reconnecting with Unit 2","html":"<p>ALA = omega-3LA = omega-6EPA + DHA = marine omega-3s</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 💊 <b>Supplement stacking:</b> cod-liver oil + multivitamin + vitamin A + vitamin D can duplicate nutrients unknowingly. Always total the entire regimen.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Reconnecting with Unit 2","items":[{"label":"Wheat-germ oil","detail":"Alive associates it with vitamin E."},{"label":"Flax","detail":"Provides ALA (and some omega-6), but does NOT directly provide meaningful EPA/DHA the way marine oils do — conversion from ALA to EPA/DHA is limited."}]}},{"id":"ns5-3-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Alive's cod-liver oil question sets up a false answer by claiming it contains vitamins A and E. Which two vitamins does cod-liver oil actually contain?","options":[{"label":"A and E","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"A and D","correct":true,"feedback":""},{"label":"D and K","correct":false,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns5-4","chapter":"Where nutrition support meets mental-health treatment","title":"Where nutrition support meets mental-health treatment","html":"","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Where nutrition support meets mental-health treatment","items":[{"label":"Melatonin","detail":"A hormone (not a vitamin), produced by the pineal gland, regulating circadian rhythm. Alive links it to sleep, seasonal affective disorder, and jet lag. Melatonin = body clock, not simply \"sleeping pill.\""},{"label":"5-HTP","detail":"Tryptophan → 5-HTP → serotonin. Precursor ≠ automatically safe — matters especially with serotonergic medications (antidepressants)."},{"label":"Kava","detail":"Works through the nervous system for anxiety/pain/relaxation. Natural ≠ risk-free — liver-related safety concerns and medication interactions apply."},{"label":"Valerian","detail":"Associated with sleep + relaxation. Research exists, but that's not the same as \"cures insomnia.\""},{"label":"Chamomile","detail":"A calming herbal tea — a very different exposure from a concentrated extract."}]}},{"id":"ns5-5","chapter":"Amylase → carbs, Protease → protein, Lipase → lipids","title":"Amylase → carbs, Protease → protein, Lipase → lipids","html":"<p>🍞Amylase → carbs</p> <p>🥩Protease → protein</p> <p>🥑Lipase → lipids</p> <p>Your body normally produces digestive enzymes — \"I'm bloated\" ≠ \"I need digestive enzymes.\" Bloating can involve meal size, fermentable carbs, constipation, food intolerance, GI disorders, swallowed air, medications, and more.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 Alive asks whether digestive enzymes are good advice when the stomach lining is inflamed — the broader principle is pain/inflammation → don't blindly supplement, investigate first. 🥛 <b>Yogurt + lactose:</b> bacterial fermentation reduces some lactose, which can make certain yogurts better tolerated for people with lactose malabsorption (Unit 1 connection).</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns5-6","chapter":"Strain-specific, not one-size-fits-all","title":"Strain-specific, not one-size-fits-all","html":"<p>Alive names <i>Lactobacillus acidophilus</i>, <i>Bifidobacterium bifidum</i>, and the historically-named <i>Streptococcus faecalis</i> as the \"three most important\" colon bacteria — older terminology; microbial taxonomy and probiotic science have moved well past a \"big three\" model.</p> <p>Genus</p> <p>→</p> <p>Species</p> <p>→</p> <p>Strain</p> <p>→</p> <p>Dose</p> <p>→</p> <p>Outcome</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🥣 Fermented food ≠ automatically probiotic — they overlap conceptually, but not every fermented food qualifies as a clinically studied probiotic. 🐝 <b>Propolis</b>: Alive asks whether it inhibits some E. coli strains — inhibiting bacteria in a lab ≠ treating a human E. coli infection.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns5-7","chapter":"Redox biology + mitochondria","title":"Redox biology + mitochondria","html":"<p>Alive describes antioxidants as shuffling energy/electrons to prevent cellular and tissue damage — simplistic wording, but the redox/electron concept is useful.</p> <p>🔬Cell</p> <p>→</p> <p>⚙️Mitochondria</p> <p>→</p> <p>🔋CoQ10</p> <p>→</p> <p>⚡Electron transport chain → ATP</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🔥 Alive recommends CoQ10 for people taking statin drugs — know that for the test. 🟡 Statins can reduce circulating CoQ10, but that doesn't mean every statin user automatically requires supplementation.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns5-8","chapter":"Ginkgo, ginseng, kelp, adrenals","title":"Ginkgo, ginseng, kelp, adrenals","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🔥⚠️ Alive states that \"symptoms from low blood sugar are likely more related to the adrenal glands than the pancreas.\" Blood-glucose regulation involves multiple hormones and organs (pancreas, liver, adrenal hormones) — it isn't appropriate to attribute nonspecific \"low blood sugar symptoms\" primarily to adrenal dysfunction without assessment.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Ginkgo, ginseng, kelp, adrenals","items":[{"label":"Ginkgo","detail":"Alive's circulatory herb — appears to increase cerebral blood flow. Don't translate to \"improves everyone's memory,\" and remember herbal products can interact with medications."},{"label":"Ginseng","detail":"Alive's \"energy-boosting herb\" — adverse effects over 500mg can include increased blood pressure, diarrhea, skin eruptions, insomnia. Stimulating herbs can have adverse effects."},{"label":"Kelp","detail":"Alive's \"green food\" taken for iodine to improve thyroid function. Remember Unit 4: too little iodine is a problem, but too much can also be a problem — thyroid symptoms → kelp is not an appropriate automatic recommendation."},{"label":"Adrenal glands","detail":"Help with stress, mineral balances, inflammation — produce cortisol and aldosterone (which influences Na/K/water balance, connecting back to Unit 4)."}]}},{"id":"ns5-9","chapter":"The 8-point check + traffic light","title":"The 8-point check + traffic light","html":"<p><p>1</p><p>Why? What problem are we solving?</p> <p>2</p><p>Need? Food inadequacy, confirmed deficiency, evidence-supported goal?</p> <p>3</p><p>Dose? How much?</p> <p>4</p><p>Duration? For how long?</p> <p>5</p><p>Duplication? Already in another product?</p> <p>6</p><p>Medications? Could they interact?</p> <p>7</p><p>Medical conditions? Kidney, liver, thyroid, pregnancy, GI disease?</p> <p>8</p><p>Scope? Nutrition support, or trying to treat a medical condition?</p> </p> <p><p>Lower complexityFood-first education, general nutrient adequacy, reviewing dietary sources, label literacy, identifying duplication.</p> <p>Needs more careConcentrated herbal extracts, high-dose nutrients, sleep supplements, multiple supplements, known medications, specific symptom management.</p> <p>Refer / collaborateTreating infection, depression, bipolar disorder, thyroid disease, significant liver/GI/cardiovascular disease, or replacing prescribed medication.</p> </p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns5-10","chapter":"It's not magic — it's shared machinery","title":"It's not magic — it's shared machinery","html":" <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🩺 This is why \"it's just an herb\" isn't a safety argument — the question is always which mechanism, which medication, and what dose. This is also why the supplement-safety checklist in 5J always asks about medications specifically, not just \"any side effects?\"</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"It's not magic — it's shared machinery","items":[{"label":"Liver enzyme competition","detail":"Many drugs are broken down by a family of liver enzymes (CYP450). Some herbs (St. John's Wort is a classic example) can speed up or slow down those same enzymes, changing how fast a medication is cleared — making it too weak or too strong."},{"label":"Additive effects","detail":"Two substances that both thin the blood (say, an anticoagulant medication and high-dose vitamin E or fish oil) can add together, even though neither alone would be a problem at their usual dose."},{"label":"Protein-binding competition","detail":"Some drugs travel through blood attached to carrier proteins. A compound competing for that same binding site can temporarily raise the amount of \"free\" active drug in circulation."},{"label":"Opposing physiology","detail":"A supplement that pushes a system one direction (e.g., licorice's effect on blood pressure/potassium) can work against a medication designed to push it the other way."}]}},{"id":"ns5-11","chapter":"Beyond \"contains turmeric\"","title":"Beyond \"contains turmeric\"","html":"","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Beyond \"contains turmeric\"","items":[{"label":"Standardization","detail":"Is the active compound standardized (e.g., \"standardized to 95% curcuminoids\") or is it just \"turmeric root powder\" with an unknown active-compound percentage?"},{"label":"Dose vs. studied dose","detail":"Does the label's dose match what was actually used in the research behind the claim, or is it far below a \"therapeutic range\"?"},{"label":"Third-party testing","detail":"Has an independent lab verified the label matches what's actually in the product? Supplements aren't regulated the same way medications are."},{"label":"Proprietary blends","detail":"A \"proprietary blend\" listing a total weight for several ingredients combined can hide how little of any one compound is actually present."}]}}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Bromelain comes from?","back":"Pineapple."},{"front":"Alive antimicrobial herb for Candida?","back":"Oregano."},{"front":"Alive herb associated with bacterial killing?","back":"Garlic."},{"front":"Amino acid Alive associates with HSV-1?","back":"Lysine."},{"front":"Alive \"liver detoxifier\"?","back":"Milk thistle."},{"front":"Herbs Alive associates with bile flow?","back":"Dandelion root + artichoke leaves + turmeric."},{"front":"Circulatory/cerebral-flow herb?","back":"Ginkgo."},{"front":"Statin-related antioxidant supplement in Alive?","back":"CoQ10."},{"front":"Mood/sleep hormone?","back":"Melatonin."},{"front":"Serotonin precursor supplement?","back":"5-HTP."},{"front":"Alive relaxation/anxiety herb?","back":"Kava."},{"front":"Carbohydrate enzyme?","back":"Amylase."},{"front":"Protein enzymes?","back":"Proteases."},{"front":"Fat enzyme?","back":"Lipase."},{"front":"Which gland is linked to cortisol/aldosterone?","back":"Adrenals."},{"front":"Alive energy herb with >500 mg adverse-effect warning?","back":"Ginseng."},{"front":"Green food Alive associates with thyroid/iodine?","back":"Kelp."},{"front":"Flax omega-3?","back":"ALA."},{"front":"Marine omega-3s?","back":"EPA + DHA."},{"front":"Cod-liver-oil vitamins to remember?","back":"A + D."},{"front":"Name one mechanism by which herbs interact with medications?","back":"Competing for the same liver (CYP450) enzymes that clear the drug."},{"front":"Why can vitamin E + an anticoagulant be risky together?","back":"Additive effects on bleeding/clotting."},{"front":"What does \"standardized\" mean on a supplement label?","back":"The active compound is guaranteed at a specific percentage/amount, not just present."},{"front":"What can hide low active-ingredient amounts on a label?","back":"A \"proprietary blend\" listing combined weight instead of individual amounts."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Chronic bronchitis or sinusitis may benefit from which of the following anti-inflammatory supplements?","o":["Aloe vera","Magnesium salicylate","Flavonoids","Bromelain"],"a":3,"e":"Bromelain is Alive's anti-inflammatory association with chronic bronchitis/sinusitis."},{"q":"Which of the following antibiotic herbs has been shown to be as effective, if not more effective, in treating Candida yeast as prescription anti-fungal drugs?","o":["Grapefruit","Turmeric","Goldenseal","Oregano"],"a":3,"e":"Oregano is Alive's antimicrobial/antifungal herb association for Candida."},{"q":"Which of the following herbs works more effectively in a more alkaline environment in the body?","o":["Grapefruit","Tea tree oil","Ginger","Goldenseal"],"a":3,"e":"Goldenseal's active compounds are commonly described as working more effectively in a more alkaline environment."},{"q":"Which herb appears to work as effectively as prescription antibiotics in killing bacteria?","o":["Ginger","St. John's Wort","Garlic","Turmeric"],"a":2,"e":"Garlic is Alive's antibacterial herb association — not a substitute for prescribed antibiotics in practice."},{"q":"Which amino acid works as an antibiotic/antiviral to treat oral herpes (HSV-1)?","o":["Glutamine","Lysine","Tyrosine","Tryptophan"],"a":1,"e":"Lysine is Alive's amino acid association for oral HSV-1."},{"q":"Which anti-inflammatory herb is also useful as a cough suppressant, expectorant, anti-arthritic, antibiotic, and possibly anti-cancer herb?","o":["Licorice","Echinacea","Ginger","Aloe vera","Garlic"],"a":0,"e":"Licorice is given this broad list of therapeutic associations in the course — note it also contains glycyrrhizin, which can affect blood pressure, potassium, and fluid balance with substantial/prolonged use."},{"q":"Which herb should not be used for longer than 3 to 4 weeks to avoid possible effects as liver irritation or changes in intestinal flora?","o":["Ginger","Garlic","Echinacea","Turmeric"],"a":2,"e":"Echinacea carries this duration caution in the course material."},{"q":"Which antioxidant supplement is recommended for someone taking statin drugs for high cholesterol?","o":["Selenium","Carotenoids","Vitamin C","CoQ 10"],"a":3,"e":"CoQ10 is Alive's recommended antioxidant for statin users, since statins can reduce circulating CoQ10."},{"q":"Which herb is at the top of the list of liver detoxifiers?","o":["Milk thistle","Nettle and burdock","Gentian and ginger","Goldenseal and garlic"],"a":0,"e":"Milk thistle (containing silymarin) is Alive's top liver-detoxifier association."},{"q":"Which herbs help increase bile flow, which is often helpful in detoxifying?","o":["Dandelion root, artichoke leaves, and garlic","Dandelion root, artichoke leaves, and turmeric","Artichoke leaves, turmeric, and licorice","Artichoke leaves, nettle, and ginger"],"a":1,"e":"Dandelion root, artichoke leaves, and turmeric are the course's tested bile-flow combination."},{"q":"Which circulatory herb appears to increase cerebral blood flow and thus help oxygenation?","o":["Garlic","Bilberry","Cayenne","Gingko","Alfalfa"],"a":3,"e":"Ginkgo is Alive's circulatory/cerebral blood flow herb association."},{"q":"Which of the following statements concerning oil supplements is FALSE?","o":["People with high blood fats, low HDL, or increased risk of cardiovascular disease can supplement 500 to 1000 of EPA and DHA twice daily to improve these conditions","Wheat germ oil is high in vitamin E","Flax is a balanced oil containing the building blocks for both Omega 6 and Omega 3 fatty acids","Cod liver oil contains vitamin A and E and someone supplementing with it should pay close attention to the total intake of these vitamins from other sources"],"a":3,"e":"Cod liver oil actually contains vitamins A and D, not A and E — this is the false statement, deliberately set up as a trap."},{"q":"Which mood-balancing nutrient or hormone is used to help treat insomnia, seasonal affective disorder, and jet lag?","o":["Serotonin","Melatonin","Pregnenalone","Kava","L-theanine"],"a":1,"e":"Melatonin is the hormone associated with circadian rhythm, sleep, SAD, and jet lag."},{"q":"Which supplement has value in treating sleep disorders and depression and is the precursor to serotonin?","o":["5-HTP","Kava","Melatonin","L-theanine"],"a":0,"e":"5-HTP is the serotonin precursor in this pathway (tryptophan → 5-HTP → serotonin)."},{"q":"Which herb works through the nervous system to lessen anxiety, reduce pain, and promote relaxation?","o":["Ginkgo","Chamomile","Kava","Valerian root"],"a":2,"e":"Kava is described this way in the course — though safety concerns including liver-related issues and medication interactions apply."},{"q":"Which digestive enzyme is found in the pancreas?","o":["Lipase","Protease","Amylase","All of the above","None of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Pancreatic secretions include lipase (fat), proteases (protein), and amylase (carbohydrate)."},{"q":"Which gland helps us deal with stress, mineral balances, and inflammation?","o":["Thymus","Thyroid","Pancreas","Adrenal"],"a":3,"e":"The adrenal glands produce cortisol and aldosterone, involved in stress response and mineral/fluid balance."},{"q":"Which herb can help with colic in infants and gas in adults?","o":["Goldenseal","Ginger","Peppermint","Marshmallow"],"a":2,"e":"Peppermint is commonly associated with easing gas and colic as a carminative herb."},{"q":"Which of the following energy-boosting herbs can cause increased blood pressure, diarrhea, skin eruptions, or insomnia when taken in amounts over 500 mg?","o":["Ginseng","Ashwaganda","Ginkgo","Codonopsis"],"a":0,"e":"Ginseng carries this specific over-500mg adverse-effect warning in the course material."},{"q":"Which of the following green foods are taken primarily for its iodine content by people who want to improve thyroid function?","o":["Wheatgrass","Spirulina","Algae","Kelp"],"a":3,"e":"Kelp is the course's iodine/thyroid-function green food association — though thyroid symptoms don't automatically mean iodine deficiency."},{"q":"Most people can correct low stomach acid by eating a balanced diet of wholesome foods and by reducing their daily stress level.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This reflects the course's food-first, lifestyle-oriented framing for digestive support."},{"q":"Propolis is known to inhibit the growth of some strains of E. coli bacteria.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This is the course's stated lab-research association — inhibiting bacteria in research isn't the same as treating a human E. coli infection."},{"q":"Symptoms that come from low blood sugar are likely more related to the adrenal glands than to the pancreas.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This is the course's stated position — an oversimplification, since blood-glucose regulation involves the pancreas, liver, and adrenal hormones together."},{"q":"Yogurt can be used by people with lactose intolerance, as the bacteria change or ferment the lactose sugar, to produce lactic acid.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Bacterial fermentation reduces some lactose, which can make certain yogurts better tolerated."},{"q":"It is good advice to take digestive enzymes when there is inflammation of the stomach lining.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Inflammation/pain shouldn't be blindly treated with a supplement — it should be investigated first."},{"q":"There are many lactobacillus and other bacteria that can inhabit the human colon, but the three that seem to be most important are Lactobacillus acidophilus, Bifidobacterium bifidum, and Streptococcus faecalis.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This is the course's stated (older) model — modern microbiome science recognizes far more complexity, with probiotic effects being strain- and outcome-specific."},{"q":"Mood-balancing supplements often involve hormones or their precursors.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Melatonin (a hormone) and 5-HTP (a serotonin precursor) are both examples of this category."},{"q":"Aloe vera is a natural antibiotic, antibacterial, and antifungal, and has been shown to inhibit the fungus Candida albicans.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's general pattern of framing herbs with strong antimicrobial claims."},{"q":"Antioxidants shuffle energy, or electrons, to keep the energy flowing in a way that prevents damage to the cells and tissue.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This is the course's simplified redox/electron description of antioxidant function."},{"q":"Glutamine is the preferred fuel of cells lining the large intestine.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Glutamine is classically described as the preferred fuel for cells lining the small intestine; cells lining the large intestine (colon) preferentially use short-chain fatty acids like butyrate from fibre fermentation."}],"perStepChapter":false},{"n":6,"title":"Foods and Phytonutrients","kicker":"Lesson 6 of your course, with your official Lesson 6 Test.","steps":[{"id":"ns6-0","chapter":"Food ≠ a pile of supplements","title":"Food ≠ a pile of supplements","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> Broccoli contains fibre, vitamin C, vitamin K, folate, potassium, carotenoids, glucosinolates, water, and many other compounds — together, a <b>food matrix</b>. A vitamin C tablet gives you one concentrated compound. That matrix matters for digestion, absorption, satiety, nutrient interactions, and overall dietary quality.</p> <p>Practitioner mindset — don't only ask what nutrient is missing. Also ask:</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> \"WHAT FOODS ARE MISSING FROM THEIR PATTERN?\"</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns6-1","chapter":"Soluble, insoluble, fermentable","title":"Soluble, insoluble, fermentable","html":"<p>Alive starts Lesson 6 by testing fibre, associating high-fibre plant-food patterns with a lower likelihood of several chronic diseases. Fibre consists primarily of plant carbohydrate components that aren't fully digested by human enzymes.</p> <p>🌾Fibre</p> <p>→</p> <p>🦠Colon microbes</p> <p>→</p> <p>⚗️Fermentation</p> <p>→</p> <p>🔥Short-chain fatty acids (butyrate)</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 Going from 15 g/day to 40 g/day overnight can cause bloating, gas, and discomfort. Increase gradually + adequate fluid + individualize.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns6-1-x","title":"Explore: Soluble, insoluble, fermentable","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"💧 Soluble","text":"Oats · Barley · Legumes · PsylliumDissolves/forms gels in water — can slow digestion and support cholesterol/glycemic management; highly fermentable."},{"label":"🌾 Insoluble","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns6-2","chapter":"Plant compounds, not vitamins","title":"Plant compounds, not vitamins","html":"<p>\"Phyto\" means <b>plant</b>. Phytonutrients (phytochemicals) are biologically active plant compounds contributing to colour, flavour, and aroma. They are <b>not</b> vitamins or minerals, but phytonutrient-rich foods are strongly associated with healthy dietary patterns.</p>  <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> EAT THE RAINBOW → PHYTOCHEMICAL DIVERSITY</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Plant compounds, not vitamins","items":[{"label":"Carotenoids","detail":"Beta-carotene (carrots, pumpkin, sweet potato), lycopene (tomatoes), lutein/zeaxanthin (leafy greens). In Alive's framework, grouped under terpenoids."},{"label":"Flavonoids","detail":"Large polyphenol family — berries, apples, tea, onions, citrus. Plant colour + bioactive compounds."},{"label":"Sulfur compounds","detail":"Cruciferous vegetables (broccoli, cabbage, kale, Brussels sprouts, cauliflower). Alive specifically tests sulfur compounds and indole-3-carbinol."},{"label":"Terpenoids","detail":"Alive associates terpenoids with carotenoids and antioxidant/immune/inflammatory claims."},{"label":"Alkaloids + Glycosides","detail":"Both simply recognized as phytonutrient categories for the exam."}]}},{"id":"ns6-3","chapter":"More than \"sugar\"","title":"More than \"sugar\"","html":"<p>Fruit = water + carbohydrate + fibre + vitamins/minerals + phytonutrients.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🍇 <b>Dried fruit</b> is more energy-dense per bite once water is removed — portions are easy to underestimate. Alive frames excess dried fruit as contributing to constipation and suggests rehydrating it. 🍋 <b>Lemon water:</b> Alive's course association is that lemon in water can stimulate gastric juices and digestion — know it for the exam, but your GI tract doesn't need a lemon-water ritual to function. 🫐 <b>Berries:</b> Alive's highest-iron berry among its choices is <b>blackberries</b> — a fun exam fact, not a major iron-deficiency treatment.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns6-3-x","title":"Explore: More than \"sugar\"","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"🍊 Whole fruit","text":"🍊Water, carbohydrate, vitamin C, fibre, and intact food structure."},{"label":"🧃 Juice","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns6-4","chapter":"Nutrient density + fibre + phytonutrients","title":"Nutrient density + fibre + phytonutrients","html":"<p>Leafy greens (spinach, kale, chard, collards) can provide folate, vitamin K, carotenoids, vitamin C, magnesium, potassium, non-heme iron, and fibre.</p> <p>🥬Spinach (~2mg iron/cup raw)</p> <p>+</p> <p>🍋Vitamin C</p> <p>→</p> <p>⬆️Better non-heme absorption</p> <p>Remember: plant iron is non-heme — pairing spinach + red pepper + lemon dressing makes far more nutritional sense than memorizing \"spinach = iron\" alone.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns6-4-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Alive's raw vegetable containing nearly 2 mg of iron per cup is:","options":[{"label":"Kale","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"Spinach","correct":true,"feedback":""},{"label":"Broccoli","correct":false,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns6-5","chapter":"Glucosinolates","title":"Glucosinolates","html":"<p>Broccoli, cauliflower, cabbage, kale, Brussels sprouts, and bok choy contain <b>glucosinolates</b>. When chopped or chewed, these can transform into other biologically active compounds. Alive associates crucifers with \"anticancer effects\" and sulfur-derived compounds.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🟢 <b>Practitioner translation:</b> cruciferous vegetables are excellent components of a healthy dietary pattern. 🔴 <b>Don't translate that to:</b> \"broccoli prevents or treats cancer.\" Food-pattern evidence ≠ disease-treatment claim.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns6-6","chapter":"Shiitake, nori, and the metal-binding claim","title":"Shiitake, nori, and the metal-binding claim","html":"","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Shiitake, nori, and the metal-binding claim","items":[{"label":"Shiitake mushrooms","detail":"Alive's association: contains all eight essential amino acids. Useful food, but mushrooms aren't major protein staples compared with beans, lentils, soy, eggs, fish, or meat."},{"label":"Sea vegetables","detail":"Nori, wakame, kombu, kelp — contribute minerals, iodine, and various polysaccharides. Alive claims they contain a fibre molecule that binds and eliminates minerals/metals — do not turn this into a heavy-metal detox protocol. Remember: seaweed can also mean too much iodine."}]}},{"id":"ns6-7","chapter":"\"Living foods\" — with a safety note","title":"\"Living foods\" — with a safety note","html":"<p>Sprouting = seed + water + germination → young plant. Alive strongly emphasizes sprouts as \"living foods,\" high in nutrients and active enzymes. Germination does change enzyme activity, nutrient composition, phytate levels, and digestibility — but <b>\"living food\" ≠ automatically superior food.</b></p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 <b>Sprout safety:</b> raw sprouts can carry foodborne pathogens, since the warm, moist conditions needed for sprouting also favour microbial growth. Food-safety guidance matters for higher-risk clients.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns6-8","chapter":"The complete-protein pseudocereal","title":"The complete-protein pseudocereal","html":"<p>Alive's answer for \"which grain is a complete protein containing all essential amino acids\" is <b>quinoa</b> (technically a pseudocereal, used nutritionally like a grain).</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> YOU DON'T NEED EVERY MEAL TO BE \"COMPLETE\"</p> <p>A varied diet across the day provides complementary amino acids (grain + legume, from Unit 2). Whole grains retain bran + germ + endosperm — more fibre, vitamins, minerals, and bioactive compounds than refined grains. Alive also calls whole-grain rice and whole wheat sources of selenium, though soil selenium content varies.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns6-9","chapter":"Nutrient-dense AND energy-dense","title":"Nutrient-dense AND energy-dense","html":"","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Nutrient-dense AND energy-dense","items":[{"label":"Pumpkin seeds","detail":"Magnesium, zinc, iron, protein, unsaturated fats. Alive's association: zinc + prostate-health claims. Zinc source? Yes. Prostate-disease treatment? Don't make that leap."},{"label":"Sunflower seeds","detail":"Alive's exam fact: ½ cup shelled ≈ 400 calories — a reminder that nuts/seeds are nutrient-dense AND energy-dense, useful when building portions."}]}},{"id":"ns6-9-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Alive's answer to \"are raw and roasted nuts nutritionally identical?\" is:","options":[{"label":"True — heat doesn't change anything","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"False — heat can alter some nutrients and compounds","correct":true,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns6-10","chapter":"Meat is not required daily","title":"Meat is not required daily","html":"<p>Alive identifies \"meat must be consumed daily to meet protein requirements\" as the <b>unsound</b> recommendation. Protein can come from legumes, tofu/tempeh, soy, nuts/seeds, grains, eggs, dairy/alternatives, fish, and meat.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🌱 <b>Vegetarian diets:</b> Alive associates them with lower cholesterol/triglycerides and less cardiovascular disease. Better modern framing: well-planned plant-rich patterns can be highly nutritious and cardio-supportive — but vegetarian ≠ automatically healthy (fries, cookies, and soda can all technically fit). Dietary quality matters.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Meat is not required daily","items":[{"label":"Processed meat","detail":"Alive recommends limiting cured meats due to high sodium/fat and nitrate-related concerns. Frequency + overall pattern matter."},{"label":"Eggs + cholesterol","detail":"Blood cholesterol ≠ simply dietary cholesterol — response varies between people and depends on the overall dietary pattern, including saturated fat."}]}},{"id":"ns6-11","chapter":"Fat + oxygen = oxidation","title":"Fat + oxygen = oxidation","html":"<p>Alive's practical tip: buy flour fresh and store it in the <b>fridge/freezer</b> to reduce oxidation, rancidity, and infestation. Whole-grain flours contain the germ and its oils — this connects straight back to Unit 2: unsaturated fats can oxidize, and now you're seeing it play out in a real food.</p> <p>🥑Unit 2: fat oxidation</p> <p>→</p> <p>🌾Unit 6: whole-grain flour storage</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns6-12","chapter":"What to ask when looking at a client's diet","title":"What to ask when looking at a client's diet","html":"","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"What to ask when looking at a client's diet","items":[{"label":"Protein","detail":"What's the anchor?"},{"label":"High-fibre carbohydrate","detail":"What's the energy/fibre source?"},{"label":"Plants","detail":"Where are the vegetables/fruit?"},{"label":"Fat","detail":"What's the fat source?"},{"label":"Variety","detail":"Are we repeating the same foods every day?"}]}},{"id":"ns6-13","chapter":"Fibre → microbes → butyrate → colonocyte fuel","title":"Fibre → microbes → butyrate → colonocyte fuel","html":" <p><p>1</p><p>🌾 Fibre that resists human digestive enzymes reaches the colon largely intact.</p> <p>2</p><p>🦠 Colon bacteria ferment it, extracting energy for themselves in the process.</p> <p>3</p><p>⚗️ Fermentation byproducts include short-chain fatty acids — acetate, propionate, and butyrate.</p> <p>4</p><p>🔋 Butyrate is preferentially used as fuel by the cells lining the colon itself (colonocytes) — different from small-intestine cells, which rely more on glutamine.</p> <p>5</p><p>🩺 Adequate colonocyte fuel supports the gut lining's structure and function over time.</p> </p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🔗 This is the mechanism behind why fibre variety (not just fibre quantity) matters — different fibre types feed different bacterial populations, which produce different short-chain fatty acid profiles.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns6-14","chapter":"Beyond the grain + legume rule of thumb","title":"Beyond the grain + legume rule of thumb","html":"<p>Section 6J covered quinoa as a complete protein and the grain + legume complementary pairing. The modern practitioner concept underneath \"complete protein\":</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🩺 Practitioner takeaway: don't reduce plant protein adequacy to a single \"complete vs. incomplete\" label for one food. Look at the whole day's protein variety and total intake — the same conclusion as the grain + legume rule, arrived at with more nuance.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Beyond the grain + legume rule of thumb","items":[{"label":"Amino acid profile","detail":"A protein is \"complete\" if it supplies all nine essential amino acids in reasonable proportions relative to human needs — not just \"contains all of them in trace amounts.\""},{"label":"Digestibility matters too","detail":"Modern protein-quality scoring (concepts like PDCAAS/DIAAS) accounts for how well a protein is actually digested and absorbed, not just its amino acid content on paper — which is why food form and preparation can matter."}]}}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does \"phyto\" mean?","back":"Plant."},{"front":"What are phytonutrients?","back":"Bioactive compounds found in plants."},{"front":"What creates much of plant colour/taste/aroma?","back":"Phytonutrients."},{"front":"Carotenoids belong to which Alive category?","back":"Terpenoids."},{"front":"Indole-3-carbinol association?","back":"Sulfur compounds/cruciferous vegetables."},{"front":"Alkaloids and glycosides are?","back":"Phytonutrient categories."},{"front":"Alive's edible flowers?","back":"Chrysanthemum, borage, marigold."},{"front":"Alive berry with highest iron among its choices?","back":"Blackberry."},{"front":"Raw leafy green Alive says has ~2 mg iron/cup?","back":"Spinach."},{"front":"Mushroom Alive associates with eight essential amino acids?","back":"Shiitake."},{"front":"Sea vegetables → Alive association?","back":"Metal-binding fibre."},{"front":"Complete-protein grain/pseudograin?","back":"Quinoa."},{"front":"Seed >30% protein in Alive?","back":"Pumpkin seed."},{"front":"Pumpkin seeds especially associated with?","back":"Zinc."},{"front":"½ cup sunflower seeds ≈ ?","back":"400 kcal in Alive."},{"front":"Does meat have to be eaten daily for protein?","back":"No."},{"front":"Whole grain components?","back":"Bran + germ + endosperm."},{"front":"Best flour storage according to Alive?","back":"Fresh, refrigerated/frozen."},{"front":"Raw and roasted nuts nutritionally identical?","back":"No."},{"front":"Which short-chain fatty acid is the preferred fuel for colon (large intestine) cells?","back":"Butyrate."},{"front":"What do colon cells use for fuel vs. small intestine cells?","back":"Colon cells prefer butyrate; small intestine cells rely more on glutamine."},{"front":"What two things does modern protein-quality scoring account for?","back":"Amino acid profile AND digestibility."},{"front":"Why does fibre variety matter, not just fibre quantity?","back":"Different fibre types feed different gut bacteria, producing different short-chain fatty acid profiles."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding fibre is FALSE?","o":["Without fibre, it is impossible for digestion to take place in a balanced way","Fibre is found exclusively in plant foods","Diabetes, heart disease, high blood pressure, obesity, and several types of cancer are least likely to occur when a person's diet is high in high-fibre plant foods","None of the statements are false"],"a":0,"e":"Digestion can still occur without dietary fibre — this overstated claim is the false one, unlike the other statements which reflect the course's core fibre teaching."},{"q":"Which of the following statements concerning phytonutrients is true?","o":["Colour, taste, and aromas of food stem from phytonutrients","Beta-carotene and carotenoids are phytonutrients that help protect us from the development of cancer and heart disease","Indole-3-carbinol is released from phytonutrient sulfur compound","Phytonutrients are essential to the body's nourishment","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All four statements reflect this course's phytonutrient framing."},{"q":"Which grouping of phytonutrients is derived from amino acids that commonly have anti-inflammatory, anti-cancer, immunosupportive, and antibiotic properties?","o":["Flavonoids","Tannins","Terpenoids","Sulfur compounds"],"a":3,"e":"Sulfur compounds (like glucosinolates) are derived from amino acids such as cysteine and methionine."},{"q":"Which grouping of phytonutrients are derived from a vitamin B5-containing compound and usually contains oxygen; includes all carotenoids; and includes substances with antioxidant, immunosupportive, anti-inflammatory, and cancer-preventative properties?","o":["Tannins","Flavonoids","Terpenoids","Phynylpropaoids"],"a":2,"e":"Terpenoids is the category that includes all carotenoids in this course's framework."},{"q":"Alkaloids and glycosides are types of:","o":["Fruits","Sprouts","Phytonutrients","Fibre"],"a":2,"e":"Both are recognized as phytonutrient categories."},{"q":"Chrysanthemum petals, borage, and marigolds are all:","o":["Edible flowers","Non-edible flowers","Tropical fruits","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"These are the course's example edible flowers."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding fruit is FALSE?","o":["Next to fresh, dried fruit is best from a nutritional standpoint","Fruits are low in fat and high in fiber","Fruit can ferment easily when sitting in the stomach juices for a long period","Acidic fruits become more alkaline when broken down in the body","None of the statements are false"],"a":4,"e":"All four statements reflect course teaching, including the \"dried fruit is next-best to fresh\" and \"acidic fruit becomes alkaline\" framings — know these as course wording rather than settled physiology."},{"q":"Which of the following fruits contain good levels of potassium?","o":["Plums","Peaches","Grapes","Pears","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All of these fruits are reasonable potassium sources."},{"q":"Which fruit can be added to water to help stimulate gastric juices and help digestion?","o":["Oranges","Limes","Lemons","Grapes"],"a":2,"e":"Lemon is the course's tested fruit for this digestion association."},{"q":"Which of the following berries has the highest level of iron?","o":["Blackberries","Raspberries","Cranberries","Strawberries"],"a":0,"e":"Blackberries are the course's highest-iron berry among these choices."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about vegetables is FALSE?","o":["Chlorophyll is produced as a result of the sun's effects on plants, and it is known to have a revitalizing and refreshing effect when used in humans","Leafy greens are the richest in nutrients of all vegetables","Dried vegetables lose the most vitamins and minerals","The cruciferous vegetables have anticancer effects","None of the above are false"],"a":2,"e":"This absolute claim about dried vegetables losing the most nutrients isn't well-supported compared with other processing methods — the other statements match course-taught content (including the chlorophyll claim, which is course wording rather than established physiology)."},{"q":"Which of the following, when raw, has nearly 2mg of iron in one cup?","o":["Kale","Collards","Chard","Spinach"],"a":3,"e":"Spinach is the course's ~2mg-iron-per-cup raw vegetable."},{"q":"Which of the following mushrooms have 8 essential amino acids and are therefore quite nutritious?","o":["Button","Oyster","Boletus","Shiitake"],"a":3,"e":"Shiitake is the course's complete-amino-acid mushroom association."},{"q":"Which vegetables contain a fibre molecule that binds minerals and can attract various metals within the digestive tract, eliminating them from the body?","o":["Nightshade vegetables","Sea vegetables","Leafy greens","Fungi"],"a":1,"e":"Sea vegetables are the course's metal-binding fibre association — don't turn this into a heavy-metal detox claim."},{"q":"The two most commonly low amino acids in vegetable proteins are:","o":["Tryptophan and methionine","Tryptophan and lysine","Lysine and threonine","Lysine and arginine"],"a":1,"e":"Tryptophan and lysine are commonly cited together as limiting amino acids across plant protein sources in this course's framework."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding sprouts is FALSE?","o":["When a seed is sprouted into the first beginnings of the new plant, much of its stored nutrient potential bursts forth into the seedling and are therefore very high in nutrients","Sprouts are living foods that contain active enzymes that help digestion","Sprouting at home is simple and only requires water, the seed, grain, or beans, and a large glass jar or tray filled with soil","Sprouts are considered the most vitally alive and nourishing foods we can eat","None of the statements are false"],"a":2,"e":"Standard home sprouting uses just water and a jar or tray (with drainage) — not soil — making this the inaccurate statement."},{"q":"Which of the following grains is considered a complete protein because it contains all essential amino acids?","o":["Oats","Quinoa","Amaranth","Barley"],"a":1,"e":"Quinoa (technically a pseudocereal) is the course's complete-protein answer."},{"q":"Which seed is more than 30% protein?","o":["Sesame","Pumpkin","Sunflower","None of the above"],"a":1,"e":"Pumpkin seed is the course's >30%-protein seed."},{"q":"Which of the following statements concerning seeds is FALSE?","o":["Sesame seeds contain vitamins A and E, and most of the B vitamins including B12 and folic acid","One cup of raw sunflower seeds contains more than 1300 mg of potassium and only 4 mg of sodium","Pumpkin seeds are high in iron and are best known for their concentration of zinc and their use in the treatment and prevention of male prostate problems","An excessive intake of seeds can throw off the balance of phosphorus and calcium in the body","A and D"],"a":4,"e":"B12 is not found in plant foods like sesame seeds (making A false), and this option pairs it with D as the intended combined false-statement answer."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a sound recommendation regarding meat?","o":["When eating meats, try leaner cuts","To meet protein requirements, meat must be consumed daily","Avoid cured meats, such as bacon, ham, lunch meat, sausage, and franks because of the high fat, high sodium, and cancer-causing chemicals, such as nitrates","Add more fish to the diet in place of red meat dishes to help cut cholesterol and fats and to protect from cardiovascular diseases"],"a":1,"e":"Meat is not required daily to meet protein needs — this is the unsound recommendation."},{"q":"Bee pollen is used more for physical energy and athletic performance than for disease or symptom treatment.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Bee pollen is generally marketed and discussed in the context of energy/athletic performance rather than as a disease treatment."},{"q":"Eating too much dried fruit can lead to constipation, therefore it is helpful to rehydrate these fruits in water prior to eating to prevent constipation.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's dried-fruit and constipation framing directly."},{"q":"Whole-grain rice and whole wheat are usually good sources of hard-to-find selenium.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"The course names whole-grain rice and whole wheat as selenium sources, though soil selenium content varies."},{"q":"When buying flours, it is best to get them fresh and store them in the freezer or fridge to prevent oxidation and rancidity and/or infestation with bugs.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's flour-storage guidance directly."},{"q":"The nutrient content is the same in roasted as it is in raw nuts.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Heat can alter some nutrients and compounds, so roasted and raw nuts aren't nutritionally identical."},{"q":"Half a cup of shelled sunflower seeds contains approximately 400 calories.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's specific calorie fact for sunflower seeds."},{"q":"Aspartame is a chemical sweetener that is best avoided, as many people can become agitated and have their moods and nervous system affected with use. It can also cause effects similar to those of MSG.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This is the course's stated position — treat it as exam content rather than an established fact for the general population."},{"q":"Research suggests that consumption of eggs when not associated with a high-fat diet raises serum cholesterol.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This reflects the course's framing of the egg/cholesterol question — modern understanding is more nuanced, with individual cholesterol response varying based on overall dietary pattern."},{"q":"Vegetarians usually have much lower levels of cholesterol and triglycerides than meat-eaters and also have less atherosclerosis and heart disease.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's vegetarian-diet cardiovascular association — well-planned plant-rich patterns are associated with these benefits, though \"vegetarian\" alone doesn't guarantee diet quality."},{"q":"Cayenne peppers help digestion, stimulate circulation, and are thought to cleanse the blood.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This is the course's stated position — \"cleanses the blood\" isn't an appropriate clinical claim, but it reflects what Alive teaches."}],"perStepChapter":false},{"n":7,"title":"Diets and Dietary Habits","kicker":"Lesson 7 of your course, with your official Lesson 7 Test.","steps":[{"id":"ns7-0","chapter":"Food is one decision. Diet is repeated decisions.","title":"Food is one decision. Diet is repeated decisions.","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> It's not one nutrient separating healthy from unhealthy eaters — it's the <b>pattern</b>: foods, amounts, frequency, variety, habits, culture, preferences, and relationship with eating, repeated over time.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> FOOD = ONE DECISION · DIET = REPEATED DECISIONS</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Food is one decision. Diet is repeated decisions.","items":[{"label":"Person A","detail":"Vegetables, fruit, legumes, whole grains, fish, nuts, unsaturated fats."},{"label":"Person B","detail":"Sugary drinks, fried foods, sweets, processed meats, refined grains, few vegetables."}]}},{"id":"ns7-1","chapter":"Vegan ≠ automatically healthy","title":"Vegan ≠ automatically healthy","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🌾🫘 Grain + legume complementary proteins <b>don't have to be eaten at the same meal</b> — adequate protein and amino-acid variety across the day is what matters.</p> <p>Compare two vegan patterns:</p> <p>Both vegan. \"My client is vegan\" alone tells you nothing about quality. For a vegan client, pay particular attention to: B12, iron, zinc, calcium, iodine, vitamin D, omega-3, protein.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Vegan ≠ automatically healthy","items":[{"label":"Vegan pattern A","detail":"Lentils, tofu, vegetables, berries, whole grains, seeds."},{"label":"Vegan pattern B","detail":"Fries, vegan cookies, vegan frozen pizza, soda."}]}},{"id":"ns7-1-x","title":"Explore: Vegan ≠ automatically healthy","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"🌱 Vegan","text":"🌱Avoids all animal-derived foods — meat, fish, eggs, dairy. Alive calls it the strictest/purest form of vegetarianism."},{"label":"🥚 Lacto-ovo","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns7-2","chapter":"Grains + vegetables + soy","title":"Grains + vegetables + soy","html":"<p>Traditional macrobiotic patterns emphasize whole grains, vegetables, and legumes/soy, with restrictions varying by version. Alive's test specifically notes that <b>fruit juice is avoided</b> among the listed choices.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> WHAT'S BEEN REMOVED? → WHAT NUTRIENTS ARE AFFECTED? → IS IT NECESSARY?</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns7-3","chapter":"Omnivorous or vegetarian, properly balanced","title":"Omnivorous or vegetarian, properly balanced","html":"<p>Alive's answer for a diet that can be omnivorous <b>or</b> vegetarian and still provide all needed nutrients is the <b>natural foods diet</b>. The useful modern idea underneath: minimally processed, nutrient-dense foods can form many different healthy patterns — you don't have to be vegan, Paleo, or Mediterranean to eat well.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns7-4","chapter":"Hunter-gatherer, high fibre, no refined grains","title":"Hunter-gatherer, high fibre, no refined grains","html":"<p>Alive describes the Paleolithic pattern as hunter-gatherer, high fibre, relatively high protein, without refined grains.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> The modern \"Paleo diet\" isn't literally a reconstruction of one universal ancestral diet — human diets historically varied enormously by geography, climate, food availability, and local plants/animals. \"Our ancestors ate this way\" is usually too simplistic.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns7-4-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which statement about Paleo is FALSE according to Alive?","options":[{"label":"Hunter-gatherer","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"Refined grains were kept to 10% of total calories","correct":true,"feedback":""},{"label":"Relatively high protein","correct":false,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns7-5","chapter":"\"Processed\" needs nuance","title":"\"Processed\" needs nuance","html":"<p>Alive connects the Standard American and industrialized diets with tooth decay, cardiovascular disease, cancer, and diabetes — a pattern high in refined grains, added sugars, sodium, processed meats, and energy-dense highly processed foods, often lower in fibre, fruit, vegetables, whole grains, and legumes.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 <b>Processed ≠ automatically unhealthy.</b> Pasteurized milk, canned beans, frozen vegetables, canned salmon, and whole-grain bread are all \"processed.\" Ask instead: what did processing do to the food? Improve safety? Preserve it? Add excessive sodium/sugar? Remove fibre? Make a nutritious food convenient?</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns7-6","chapter":"Improve the diet, don't replace the culture","title":"Improve the diet, don't replace the culture","html":"<p>Alive's association: <b>Ayurveda → Indian</b> dietary tradition. But the bigger lesson: food isn't merely nutrients — it's family, culture, religion, home, economics, celebration, tradition, and identity.</p>  <p>If a client grew up eating rice + beans, telling them to \"replace everything with quinoa, kale, and salmon\" isn't necessarily a better plan — it may be a plan they won't want to live with.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> IMPROVE THE CLIENT'S DIET. DON'T REPLACE THEIR CULTURE.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Improve the diet, don't replace the culture","items":[{"label":"Indian","detail":"Practices an Ayurvedic approach — the idea that the same forces governing health or illness in a diet are the same forces that govern the entire universe."},{"label":"Japanese","detail":"Similar to Chinese eating patterns — rice, cooked and pickled vegetables, fish, and modest animal products, with much more seafood than other Asian countries (Japan being an island nation). Raw fish (sashimi) is characteristic; soy shows up as tofu and other beans like adzuki, and miso as a common salty soup base. Dairy is minimal, fresh raw vegetables are eaten rarely, and most food is cooked, pickled, or smoked — likely a historical response to spoilage and contamination concerns. Japan's diet has also become more Westernized, bringing industrialized-diet concerns along with it."},{"label":"Mexican","detail":"Refined foods — cookies, candies — have become more common, especially among young children, and cooking oils/lard are a concern related to atherosclerosis. The course suggests less refined food and more whole grains/vegetables for fibre, with plentiful fruit. Because food contamination concerns are common, most food is well cooked — though eating more fresh fruits and vegetables for their cooling effect would likely be healthier for the hot climate."}]}},{"id":"ns7-7","chapter":"No universal perfect schedule","title":"No universal perfect schedule","html":"<p>Alive associates overeating, late-night eating, and \"too many different foods at once\" with weakened digestion and easier weight gain, and presents \"what we eat\" as the most important food habit. Its preferred meal principle: <b>eat the most before you do the most.</b></p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 There is no universal perfect meal schedule. Some people thrive on 3 meals, others need snacks, shift work, early exercise, medications affecting appetite, or culturally later dinners. Assess: when do you get hungry? When do you need energy? When can you actually eat?</p> <p>Meal composition matters too — compare:</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"No universal perfect schedule","items":[{"label":"Meal A","detail":"Plain white toast + jam. Mostly rapidly-digested carbohydrate."},{"label":"Meal B","detail":"Whole-grain toast + eggs + berries + nut/seed component. Carbohydrate + protein + fat + fibre together — changes digestion, satiety, and glucose response."}]}},{"id":"ns7-8","chapter":"Hunger isn't the only reason humans eat","title":"Hunger isn't the only reason humans eat","html":"<p>We eat because of pleasure, comfort, celebration, stress, fatigue, social situations, habit, environment, culture, sensory cues, availability — and yes, physiological hunger.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 Don't learn \"emotional eating is bad.\" Eating for pleasure or comfort is sometimes normal. The real question: is this pattern causing distress or working against the client's goals? Alive notes that using sweets as rewards can contribute to lifelong problems with our relationship with food, and that emotions strongly influence eating behaviour.</p> <p><b>Behaviour has a function</b> — understand it before trying to remove it. \"Every night at 9pm I lose control around sweets\" deserves questions about daily fueling, protein at meals, restrictive dieting, stress, habit/cue, sleep, and food rules — not an immediate \"you need discipline.\"</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns7-9","chapter":"A major Alive vs. evidence section","title":"A major Alive vs. evidence section","html":"<p>Alive teaches: fruit should be eaten alone; proteins shouldn't be mixed with starches; meat needs an acidic medium; starch needs an alkaline environment; fruit with other foods may \"ferment\" in the body.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🔴 Know this is in the course. Do not build your practice around this theory. Your digestive system is specifically designed to process <b>mixed meals</b> — a bean naturally contains carbohydrate + protein + fibre together; milk contains carbohydrate + protein + fat; nuts contain protein + fat + carbohydrate/fibre. Multiple digestive enzymes (proteases, amylase, lipase) operate throughout the process on the same meal.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> MIXED MEALS ARE NORMAL HUMAN PHYSIOLOGY</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns7-10","chapter":"Blood pH is tightly regulated","title":"Blood pH is tightly regulated","html":"<p>Alive's answer for the food generating the most alkaline \"ash\" is <b>lemon</b>, and states most fruits/vegetables generate alkaline ash. It frames acid-alkaline balance as the body's response to food, not simply the food's pH.</p> <p>🫁Lungs regulate CO₂</p> <p>+</p> <p>🫘Kidneys regulate acid/base</p> <p>=</p> <p>⚖️Tightly controlled blood pH</p> <p>Food can influence urinary acid load/pH — that doesn't mean \"eating lemons alkalizes your blood.\" Plant-rich diets are healthy for many real reasons; don't sell them as blood-alkalizing.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns7-11","chapter":"The 4-4-9 rule + practitioner label check","title":"The 4-4-9 rule + practitioner label check","html":"<p>🍞Carb ≈ 4 kcal/g</p> <p>🥩Protein ≈ 4 kcal/g</p> <p>🥑Fat ≈ 9 kcal/g</p> <p>Example: 25g carbohydrate × 4 = 100 kcal from carbohydrate. (Alcohol is ~7 kcal/g, though not one of the three core macronutrients.) Alive also tests that <b>ingredients are listed from most to least by weight.</b></p> ","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"The 4-4-9 rule + practitioner label check","items":[{"label":"Serving size","detail":"Everything else depends on it."},{"label":"Protein","detail":"Relevant to meal/snack goals."},{"label":"Fibre","detail":"Especially for carbohydrate foods."},{"label":"Sodium","detail":"Important when comparing packaged foods."},{"label":"Saturated fat","detail":"Useful in overall cardiovascular assessment."},{"label":"Sugars","detail":"Interpret in context, including added/free sugars where labeled."},{"label":"Ingredients","detail":"What is actually in it?"}]}},{"id":"ns7-12","chapter":"Don't turn food into morality","title":"Don't turn food into morality","html":"<p>Alive asks which recommendation is NOT appropriate among: model healthy eating, organize the food environment, provide healthy snacks, and encourage water <i>and juice</i> throughout the day. The problematic option is the recommendation for plenty of juice.</p> <p>Children learn from what they see, what's available, repeated exposure, and family environment — not just \"eat your vegetables.\"</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 Avoid creating \"good food\" vs. \"bad food,\" and avoid constantly using sweets as reward — it can unintentionally teach \"broccoli is the unpleasant thing I endure to earn the valuable cookie.\"</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns7-13","chapter":"Clean → Separate → Cook → Chill","title":"Clean → Separate → Cook → Chill","html":"<p><p>🧼</p><p><b>Clean</b> — hands, surfaces, utensils.</p> <p>🔪</p><p><b>Separate</b> — raw meat from ready-to-eat foods.</p> <p>🔥</p><p><b>Cook</b> — use appropriate safe temperatures.</p> <p>❄️</p><p><b>Chill</b> — refrigerate appropriately.</p> </p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 <b>Cooked ≠ can't be recontaminated.</b> Alive's \"once food is cooked, contamination is unlikely\" statement is <b>false</b> — imagine raw chicken cooked safely, then placed back on the same contaminated raw-chicken cutting board. Cooking worked; then you recontaminated it. 🥫 <b>Bulging can? Don't use it</b> — this isn't a \"smell it and see\" situation. Foodborne illness needs food + moisture + temperature + time — which is why storage and handling matter so much.</p>","visual":null}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Strictest vegetarian pattern in Alive?","back":"Vegan."},{"front":"Lacto = ?","back":"Dairy."},{"front":"Ovo = ?","back":"Egg."},{"front":"Lacto-ovo vegetarian eats animal flesh?","back":"No."},{"front":"Alive macrobiotic avoidance question?","back":"Fruit juice."},{"front":"Alive balanced omnivorous/vegetarian diet?","back":"Natural foods diet."},{"front":"Paleo = ?","back":"Hunter-gatherer framework."},{"front":"Refined grains in Paleolithic diet?","back":"No."},{"front":"Ayurvedic eating → ?","back":"Indian tradition."},{"front":"Alive's most important food habit?","back":"What we eat."},{"front":"Alive daily-meal principle?","back":"Eat the most before you do the most."},{"front":"Alive alkaline-ash food among choices?","back":"Lemon."},{"front":"Do rice + beans need to be eaten at the same meal?","back":"No."},{"front":"Carbohydrate kcal/g?","back":"4."},{"front":"Protein kcal/g?","back":"4."},{"front":"Fat kcal/g?","back":"9."},{"front":"Ingredient list order?","back":"Most → least by weight."},{"front":"Can cooked food become contaminated?","back":"Yes."},{"front":"Bulging cans?","back":"Discard/do not consume."},{"front":"Four food-safety anchors?","back":"Clean → Separate → Cook → Chill."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding diets is FALSE?","o":["Cancer and cardiovascular disease, both nutritionally related, are the two biggest killers of the adult population","Sweets, such as ice cream or milk and cookies, after dinner or before bed, and treats as rewards may create lifelong problems with our relationship with food","When we markedly change our diets, we should pay close attention to how the body handles new foods as digestive problems, and other sensitivities and allergies may occur.","There are little differences between the diets of rural and urban families."],"a":3,"e":"Rural and urban dietary patterns have historically differed substantially in food access, variety, and composition — making this the false statement, unlike the other three which reflect the course's core teaching."},{"q":"Which of the following is the strictest, purest form of vegetarianism?","o":["Macrobiotic","Vegan","Lacto-ovo vegetarian","Vegetarian"],"a":1,"e":"Vegan is the strictest/purest form of vegetarianism — no animal-derived foods at all."},{"q":"Which of the following foods are avoided in the macrobiotic diet?","o":["Fruit juices","Cooked vegetables","Soy products","Cooked grains"],"a":0,"e":"Fruit juice is the course's tested macrobiotic avoidance — macrobiotic patterns emphasize whole grains, vegetables, and soy."},{"q":"Which of the following foods do not form a complete protein?","o":["Grains and legumes","Vegetables and legumes","Seeds or nuts and legumes","Vegetables and milk or eggs"],"a":1,"e":"Grain+legume and seed/nut+legume are the classic complementary-protein pairings; vegetables+legumes isn't one of the recognized complementary combinations in this framework."},{"q":"Which of the following diets does not include animal flesh, but does use the by-products of chickens and cows, such as eggs and milk products?","o":["Vegan","Macrobiotic","Vegetarian","Lacto-ovo vegetarian","None of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Lacto (dairy) + ovo (eggs) — no animal flesh, but includes animal by-products."},{"q":"If properly balanced, which diet can be either omnivorous or vegetarian while providing all of the nutrients we need for the body to function optimally?","o":["Paleolithic","Natural foods","Raw foods","Industrialized"],"a":1,"e":"The natural foods diet is the course's answer for a pattern that can be omnivorous or vegetarian while still meeting nutrient needs."},{"q":"Which of the following diets consists of small meals or snacks every 2 to 3 hours and focuses on the concept that food is our fuel?","o":["Raw food","Paleolithic","Warrior","North American","Industrialized"],"a":2,"e":"Confirmed directly from the course text: the \"Warrior's\" diet is described as small meals or snacks eaten every 2 to 3 hours throughout the day, treating food as fuel."},{"q":"Tooth decay, cardiovascular disease, cancer, and diabetes are all problems associated with which of the following diets?","o":["Macrobiotic diet","Mediterranean diet","Standard American diet","Industrialized diet","C and D"],"a":4,"e":"Both the Standard American diet and the industrialized diet are linked to these chronic disease associations in the course material."},{"q":"Which cultural diet practices an Ayurvedic approach to eating, which believes the forces that make a diet healthy or unhealthy are the same forces that govern the entire universe?","o":["Thai","Kosher","Mexican","Indian","Asian"],"a":3,"e":"Ayurveda is the Indian dietary tradition tested here."},{"q":"Which of the following statements concerning the Paleolithic diet is FALSE?","o":["Their longevity was similar to ours, but with much less chronic degenerative disease.","Protein intake was high, being 19 to 35% of total calories, and always included meats","It was a hunter-gatherer diet","Fibre intake was very high","Refined grains were kept to 10% of total calories"],"a":4,"e":"Refined grains were not part of Paleolithic diets at all — this is the deliberate exam trap, since a nonzero percentage contradicts the hunter-gatherer framing."},{"q":"Which habits can weaken digestive functions and make it much easier to gain weight?","o":["Overeating","Eating late at night","Eating too many different foods at the same time","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"The course groups overeating, late-night eating, and eating too many different foods together as digestion-weakening habits."},{"q":"What is the most important food habit?","o":["Where we eat","When we eat","Why we eat","What we eat"],"a":3,"e":"\"What we eat\" is presented as the most important food habit in the course."},{"q":"Which principle is the best approach to daily meal patterns?","o":["Eat a big breakfast to “break the fast”","Finish eating for the day by 9 pm","Eat the most before you do the most","Eat the largest meal mid-day"],"a":2,"e":"\"Eat the most before you do the most\" is the course's tested meal-timing principle."},{"q":"Which of the following foods generate the most alkaline ash?","o":["Grains","Nuts","Milk","Lemons"],"a":3,"e":"Lemons are the course's tested most-alkaline-ash food."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about food combining is FALSE?","o":["Fruits are best eaten alone","Meats require an acidic digestive medium and starches require an alkaline environment, so when eaten together, it slows digestion and makes it less efficient","Proteins should not be mixed with starches","If eaten with other foods, fruits and simple sugars can ferment in the body allowing gas to go through the intestines","None of the statements are false"],"a":4,"e":"All four food-combining claims (A-D) reflect the course's own food-combining framework — none are false within the course's system, even though this whole framework needs an evidence-context caveat for practice."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about food labeling is correct?","o":["High potassium means 700mg or more per serving","Good source of fibre indicates 2.5 to 4.9 g per serving","To calculate the calories from carbohydrates, multiply the total grams of carbohydrates by 4 calories","The basic objective of reading food labels is to find out if the product is nutritious or should be avoided","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All four statements about food labeling are accurate per the course material."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a recommendation for nourishing our children?","o":["The most effective way to get kids to eat healthfully is to set a good example","Organize your refrigerator and pantry in a way that allows the young ones to get the items you want them to have","Children should be encouraged to drink plenty of water and juice throughout the day","Offer children healthy snacks at least a couple of times per day","All of the above are good recommendations"],"a":2,"e":"Encouraging plenty of juice (alongside water) is the unsound recommendation — the other options reflect sound practice."},{"q":"Which spice can be contaminated with salmonella and should be stored in the refrigerator?","o":["Paprika","Garlic","Red pepper","Turmeric","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"Paprika has documented salmonella-contamination history and is the course's tested spice for refrigerated storage."},{"q":"Which of the following is recommended to avoid the spread of infections through food?","o":["Avoid buying overripe produce","Not using bulging cans","Using meats and poultry within 1 or 2 days","B and C","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All of these practices are recommended food-safety measures."},{"q":"About one-third of deaths caused by food poisoning can be directly traced to which three organisms?","o":["Salmonella, listeria, and toxoplasma","Shigella, campylobacter, and E. coli","Salmonella, E. coli, and blastocystis","Giardia, cryptosporidium, and hepatitis A"],"a":0,"e":"Salmonella, Listeria, and Toxoplasma are the three organisms most commonly cited as responsible for the majority of foodborne-illness deaths in public health literature."},{"q":"Which of the following foods generate alkaline ash in the body?","o":["Meat and fish","milk products","Cranberries","most fruits and vegetables"],"a":3,"e":"Most fruits and vegetables generate alkaline ash per the course's framework."},{"q":"To create a complete protein, rice and beans must be eaten together, at the same meal.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Complementary proteins don't need to be eaten in the same meal — adequate variety across the day is what matters."},{"q":"When the industrial or refined foods diet was introduced to different tribal cultures throughout the world, a general degradation of their health followed, usually within a single generation.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This is the course's stated position on dietary transitions and tribal health — a broad historical claim worth knowing for the exam, though it needs more context before generalizing clinically."},{"q":"Extra salt and too few of the other minerals are affecting the body's water balance, thereby affecting the kidneys, blood pressure, and eventually the entire cardiovascular system.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This reflects the course's framing connecting excess sodium and mineral imbalance to fluid balance, kidney function, blood pressure, and cardiovascular health."},{"q":"It is a good practice to drink liquids with our meals.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Confirmed directly from the course text: drinking liquids with meals is described as not really good practice, since extra fluids can dilute digestive juices — drinking water before or after meals, or a small amount (less than 1 cup) with meals, is preferred."},{"q":"Our emotions strongly influence our eating behaviour, so if we want to maintain a more balanced diet, and thus a more balanced life, we need to learn to deal with our emotional states in ways other than with foods.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Emotions do strongly influence eating behavior, and developing non-food coping strategies is a reasonable extension of that principle."},{"q":"Acid-alkaline balance refers to the body's response to the food consumed, not to the food's pH level in isolation.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's explicit framing of acid-alkaline balance as a bodily response rather than simply the food's own pH."},{"q":"Contents in foods are listed on food labels in order by quantity. The predominant ingredient in the product is listed first and the smallest last.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Ingredients are listed from greatest to least amount by weight."},{"q":"The main concern regarding food contamination is contamination by food handling.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Food handling practices are emphasized as the central concern in the course's food-safety framework."},{"q":"Once food is cooked, contamination is unlikely.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Cooked food can absolutely be recontaminated afterward — for example, by placing it back on a contaminated raw-meat cutting board."},{"q":"It is best to wait for about an hour after meals before exercising.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated guidance on timing exercise relative to meals."}],"perStepChapter":false},{"n":8,"title":"Environmental Nutrition","kicker":"Lesson 8 of your course, with your official Lesson 8 Test.","steps":[{"id":"ns8-0","chapter":"What did the processing actually do?","title":"What did the processing actually do?","html":"<p>Milling → refining → artificial processing can reduce nutritional value — the same concept as Unit 4's wheat-milling mineral losses (bran + germ + endosperm → milling removes portions → fibre, vitamins, minerals, phytonutrients may be reduced).</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 Processing ≠ automatically bad. Pasteurizing milk improves safety, freezing preserves food, canning increases convenience/storage, cooking tomatoes alters availability of certain compounds. Ask \"what did the processing do?\" not \"was it processed?\"</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns8-1","chapter":"Hazard ≠ risk","title":"Hazard ≠ risk","html":"<p>Risk depends on repeated exposure, multiple chemical exposures, similar chemicals from multiple sources, and individual susceptibility. The toxicology concept underneath: <b>dose + route + duration + hazard + susceptibility</b> all matter — detectable ≠ harmful dose.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns8-1-x","title":"Explore: Hazard ≠ risk","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"Hazard","text":"⚠️Can this thing cause harm under some circumstances?"},{"label":"Risk","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns8-2","chapter":"Chemicals that interfere with hormone systems","title":"Chemicals that interfere with hormone systems","html":"<p>Endocrine disruptors are chemicals capable of interfering with hormonal systems — potential mechanisms include interfering with hormone receptors, synthesis, transport, metabolism, or signalling. The course's \"lengthening the estrogen cycle\" description is more specific than what I'd carry into practice.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🩺 Avoid: \"your hormones are toxic because of chemicals.\" Better: \"some environmental chemicals can interfere with endocrine signalling; exposure and health effects depend on the particular substance and dose.\"</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns8-3","chapter":"Exposure routes beyond food","title":"Exposure routes beyond food","html":"<p>👃 Inhalation🍽️ Ingestion✋ Dermal/contact</p> <p><b>Air pollution examples:</b> formaldehyde, methane, ozone, propane, cadmium, carbon monoxide. A person's health exists inside food + water + air + environment + behaviour + biology — nutrition is one part.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Exposure routes beyond food","items":[{"label":"Indoor pollution","detail":"Carpets, synthetic drapes, gas heaters — and dust as a potential carrier of contaminants."},{"label":"Household products","detail":"Course associates dry-cleaning spot remover with ethanol, ammonia, perchloroethylene. Know the associations for the exam — don't memorize as universal ingredient lists, since formulations vary."}]}},{"id":"ns8-4","chapter":"Know each association — without chemical phobia","title":"Know each association — without chemical phobia","html":" <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> EVERYTHING IS MADE OF CHEMICALS — ASK: WHICH SUBSTANCE, HOW MUCH, WHAT EXPOSURE, WHAT EVIDENCE?</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Know each association — without chemical phobia","items":[{"label":"Sulfites","detail":"Course associates with reactions, particularly in susceptible people including some with asthma."},{"label":"Nitrites","detail":"Course associates with behavioural effects and cancer claims."},{"label":"Gelatin + pectin + annatto","detail":"Course's \"relatively safe\" group."},{"label":"BHA/BHT","detail":"Antioxidant additive in packaged foods, associated with liver/kidney irritation."},{"label":"Carrageenan","detail":"Seaweed-derived stabilizer used in dairy-type products and puddings."},{"label":"Paraben","detail":"Used in foods/cosmetics."},{"label":"Smoke flavouring","detail":"Connected with PAHs."},{"label":"MSG","detail":"Associated with headache, tingling, numbness, chest pain in some people."}]}},{"id":"ns8-5","chapter":"Course position vs. evidence","title":"Course position vs. evidence","html":"<p>The course groups aspartame and MSG as \"excitotoxins\" and frames sucralose as not a good sugar-substitute recommendation.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🔴 Don't automatically translate that into \"aspartame damages the nervous system\" or \"sucralose is toxic\" — those are stronger claims than the course assessment establishes. Sweetener decisions should weigh dose/intake, overall diet, GI tolerance, client preference, reason for use, and current safety guidance.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns8-6","chapter":"A technology, not a single toxin","title":"A technology, not a single toxin","html":"<p>Alive's course position: organic food helps consumers avoid GM foods, and recommends avoiding GM foods.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🔴 Don't teach \"GMO = dangerous\" as a blanket scientific conclusion. Genetic modification describes a technology/process, not a single nutrient or toxin — individual foods/traits need evaluation on their actual characteristics.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns8-7","chapter":"Irradiated ≠ radioactive","title":"Irradiated ≠ radioactive","html":"<p>Food irradiation uses ionizing radiation to reduce microorganisms/pests and extend shelf life. The food isn't turned into a radioactive object simply because irradiation was used.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> X-RAY + GAMMA = IONIZING</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns8-7-x","title":"Explore: Irradiated ≠ radioactive","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"☢️ Ionizing","text":"X-rays · Gamma raysHas enough energy to ionize atoms/molecules."},{"label":"📡 Non-ionizing","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns8-7-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Alive states: \"Non-ionizing radiation includes X-rays, gamma rays and nuclear radiation.\" True or false?","options":[{"label":"True","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"False","correct":true,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns8-8","chapter":"Relaxing ≠ clearing electricity","title":"Relaxing ≠ clearing electricity","html":"<p>Alive says standing barefoot on the earth can \"ground\" the body and \"clear body electricity.\"</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🔴 Know the concept appears in the course. Do not present this as established medical/nutrition therapy. Walking outside barefoot may be relaxing — that's different from claiming it clears harmful electricity from the body.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns8-9","chapter":"Don't make clients afraid of produce","title":"Don't make clients afraid of produce","html":"<p>Imported foods, especially some tropical fruits, may be treated during shipping. Produce waxes' protectiveness/safety is also tested.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🩺 Practical advice: eat plenty of fruits/vegetables, wash produce appropriately, follow food-safety practices, choose organic if desired/affordable — but don't create \"conventional vegetables are toxic,\" which could discourage eating foods we actually want clients to eat.</p>","visual":null}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Hazard means?","back":"Can this thing cause harm under some circumstances?"},{"front":"Risk means?","back":"How likely is harm at the exposure someone actually receives?"},{"front":"Endocrine disruptors interfere with?","back":"Hormonal signalling systems."},{"front":"Indoor pollution sources Alive lists?","back":"Carpets, synthetic drapes, gas heaters."},{"front":"What can carry contaminants indoors?","back":"Dust."},{"front":"Alive's \"relatively safe\" additive group?","back":"Gelatin, pectin, annatto."},{"front":"Carrageenan is derived from?","back":"Seaweed."},{"front":"Smoke flavouring is connected with?","back":"PAHs."},{"front":"Aspartame + MSG are grouped by Alive as?","back":"Excitotoxins."},{"front":"Is GMO automatically dangerous?","back":"No — it's a technology/process, not a single toxin."},{"front":"Irradiated food is radioactive?","back":"False — irradiated ≠ radioactive."},{"front":"X-rays and gamma rays are?","back":"Ionizing radiation."},{"front":"Non-ionizing radiation examples?","back":"Radiofrequency, visible light."},{"front":"\"Grounding\" claim in Alive?","back":"Standing barefoot \"clears body electricity\" — not established therapy."},{"front":"Imported tropical foods may be?","back":"Treated during shipping."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a food process that results in loss of nutritive value?","o":["Milling of grains","Refining","Synthesizing","Artificial processes","All of the above are processes that result in loss of nutritive value"],"a":2,"e":"Synthesizing typically adds compounds rather than removing them — unlike milling, refining, and artificial processing, which all tend to strip nutritive value."},{"q":"Many factors influence the dangers of chemicals in our lives. Which of the following is NOT a factor?","o":["Repeated exposure to small amounts of chemicals","Interaction of various chemicals","Repeated exposure to the same or similar chemicals found in different products or areas","Our individual immune system capacity","All of the above are factors"],"a":4,"e":"All four listed items are genuine factors influencing chemical risk — repeated exposure, chemical interactions, cumulative exposure across products, and individual susceptibility."},{"q":"Which type of chemical lengthens the estrogen cycle, prolonging the body's exposure to estrogen and significantly increasing the possibility of cancer?","o":["Nerve toxins","Reproductive toxins","Endocrine disrupters","Carcinogens"],"a":2,"e":"Endocrine disrupters are the course's tested chemical category for this estrogen-cycle effect."},{"q":"Which of the following is a source of indoor pollution?","o":["Carpets","Synthetic drapes","Gas heaters","A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Carpets, synthetic drapes, and gas heaters are all named indoor pollution sources."},{"q":"Which indoor pollutant can carry toxic materials, such as mites, molds, bacteria, asbestos, lead, and carbon monoxide?","o":["Paints","Dust","Smoke","Heating systems"],"a":1,"e":"Dust is the course's tested carrier of these toxic materials indoors."},{"q":"Which product commonly used in the home contains ethanol, ammonia, and perchloroethylene, and can disrupt activity in our nervous and endocrine systems?","o":["Furniture polish","Dishwasher detergent","Carpet shampoo and upholstery cleaner","Dry cleaning spot remover"],"a":3,"e":"Dry cleaning spot remover is the course's tested product containing this specific chemical combination."},{"q":"What type of plastic is commonly found in adhesives, containers, beach balls, pacifiers, raincoats, and boots that can release vinyl chloride and cause cancer, liver disease, and birth defects?","o":["Plasticizers","Polyvinyl chloride","Polyurethane foam","Fluorocarbon plastic"],"a":1,"e":"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is the plastic associated with vinyl chloride release and these health concerns."},{"q":"Which of the following are symptoms of chemical toxicity?","o":["Headache, skin rashes, wheezing, and depression","Fatigue, nausea, loss of sex drive, and confusion","Behaviour changes, mood swings, and coughing","A and C","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All three symptom groupings are listed as chemical toxicity symptoms in the course material."},{"q":"Which of the following products may contain hydrogen peroxide, ammonia, formaldehyde, and artificial colour, and can damage our nervous and immune systems and disrupt our liver metabolism?","o":["Nail polish remover","Air freshener","Toothpaste","Mouthwash"],"a":3,"e":"Mouthwash is the course's tested product for this specific chemical combination."},{"q":"Formaldehyde, methane, ozone, propane, cadmium, and carbon monoxide are all examples of:","o":["Common air pollutants","Common water pollutants","Common home pesticides","Common home cleaning supplies"],"a":0,"e":"These are all named as common air pollutants."},{"q":"Which of the following additives to avoid can cause headaches, nausea, diarrhea, irritated membranes, and allergic reactions, particularly in people with asthma?","o":["Nitrites","Artificial colors","Sulfites","MSG"],"a":2,"e":"Sulfites are specifically tied to these reactions, particularly in people with asthma."},{"q":"Which of the following additives can cause hyperactivity and behavioural changes in children and has also been shown in research studies to cause cancer?","o":["Preservatives","Nitrites","Sugar","Refined flour"],"a":1,"e":"Nitrites are the course's tested additive for this combined behavioural and cancer-risk association."},{"q":"Which of the following are relatively safe additives?","o":["Lecithin, olestra, and vanillin","Annatto, casein, and sacchrin","Gelatin, pectin, and annatto","Sorbitol, minerals, and aspartame"],"a":2,"e":"Gelatin, pectin, and annatto are grouped as the course's relatively safe additives."},{"q":"Which antioxidant food additive can be irritating to the liver and kidneys, and is commonly found in prepackaged foods, such as enriched rice, breakfast cereals, dog and cat foods, chewing gum, and cake mixes?","o":["Carob","Bromines","BHA","BHT"],"a":3,"e":"BHT is the course's tested antioxidant additive for this specific list of foods and organ concerns."},{"q":"Which food additive is derived from seaweed and is used as a natural stabilizer in foods such as ice cream, cheese spread, chocolate milk, and puddings?","o":["Cornstarch","Carrageenan","DES","Calcium proprionate"],"a":1,"e":"Carrageenan is the seaweed-derived stabilizer used in these foods."},{"q":"Which of the following food additives is found in jams, jellies, baked goods, and sugar substitutes and is also widely used in the cosmetics industry?","o":["Paraben","Palmitic acid","Polysorbate","Silicate"],"a":0,"e":"Paraben is used across both these food categories and cosmetics."},{"q":"Which additive that helps flavour foods and can reduce bacterial contamination has been found to contain polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs), all of which are classified as carcinogens?","o":["Sodium nitrate","Smoke flavoring","Sulfites","Soy sauce"],"a":1,"e":"Smoke flavoring is the course's tested additive connected to PAHs."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about food irradiation is FALSE?","o":["Food is irradiated using electromagnetic radiation in the range of 10^19 to 10^22 Hz","Radiation therapy has been shown to deplete B vitamins and fat soluble vitamins, such as A and E","Doses of radiation used for food are similar to that used for an average chest X-ray","Food irradiation has been shown to alter cell structures and intracellular activities","Spices, wheat, potatoes, and meat products are some examples of the foods the FDA has approved for food irradiation"],"a":2,"e":"Food irradiation doses are vastly higher than an average chest X-ray dose — this comparison dramatically understates the actual radiation dose used, making it the false statement."},{"q":"Which of following statements regarding electromagnetic radiation is correct?","o":["EMR can be a danger to our health","EMR can be divided into two categories: ionizing and nonionizing radiation","EMR can rip electrons from atoms and molecules and directly affect cell division and cell structure","EMR can affect the pineal gland, which produces serotonin and melatonin","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All four statements about electromagnetic radiation are accurate per the course material."},{"q":"Which food additive is commonly used in food processing, restaurants, and home cooking, and can cause some people to experience headaches, tingling, numbness, and chest pains?","o":["Neotame","MSG","HVP","DES"],"a":1,"e":"MSG is the course's tested additive for these specific symptoms."},{"q":"MSG and aspartame are termed excitotoxins and fall under the nerve toxins category. Even if they do not cause nervous system damage, their chemical effect can overstimulate mental activity.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated \"excitotoxin\" framing for MSG and aspartame."},{"q":"Some estimates suggest that pollution is 5 to 10 times more damaging indoors than it is outdoors.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This is the course's stated indoor-vs-outdoor pollution comparison."},{"q":"Many imported foods, particularly tropical fruits, are sprayed or fumigated to keep bugs away during shipping.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated concern about imported produce treatment during shipping."},{"q":"The artificial sweetener sucralose is a good recommendation for a sugar substitute.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"The course does not recommend sucralose as a good sugar substitute."},{"q":"Even though sulfites may not be cancer-causing themselves, they may enhance the cancer-causing potential of other substances, and many people seem to react to sulfites, especially those sprayed on foods or added in restaurants.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated position on sulfites and cancer-potentiating effects."},{"q":"Consumers can avoid genetically modified foods by choosing to buy organic.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated position that organic purchasing helps avoid GM foods."},{"q":"A good recommendation is to avoid GM foods.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated recommendation to avoid GM foods."},{"q":"Non-ionizing radiation includes X-rays, gamma rays, and nuclear radiation. Exposing the body to these ions at certain levels can dramatically affect the atomic structure.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"X-rays, gamma rays, and nuclear radiation are actually ionizing radiation, not non-ionizing — making this statement false."},{"q":"A way to balance electromagnetism and electropollution is to put our bare feet on the earth (grass or dirt) and stand for a few minutes to help ground ourselves and clear body electricity.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated \"grounding\" recommendation."},{"q":"Waxes applied to produce protect the food and are safe to consume.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated position that approved produce waxes are safe to consume."}],"perStepChapter":false},{"n":9,"title":"Building the Ideal Diet","kicker":"Lesson 9 of your course, with your official Lesson 9 Test.","steps":[{"id":"ns9-0","chapter":"Alive's core food-group grouping","title":"Alive's core food-group grouping","html":"<p>🍎<h4>Fruits</h4></p> <p>🥦<h4>Vegetables</h4></p> <p>🌾<h4>Whole grains + legumes</h4></p> <p>🥩<h4>Proteins + fats/oils</h4></p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns9-1","chapter":"Variety → coverage","title":"Variety → coverage","html":"<p>Different foods provide different vitamins, minerals, amino acids, fatty acids, fibres, and phytonutrients — connecting straight back to Units 1-6.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> VARIETY → COVERAGE</p> <p>Think of nutrition as a portfolio. One \"superfood\" eaten daily with almost nothing else isn't nutritional excellence. <b>Balance</b> means nutrients/food groups exist in reasonable relationships; <b>moderation</b> means amount matters too.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> MODERATION ≠ RESTRICTION</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns9-2","chapter":"Split the claims","title":"Split the claims","html":"<p>Alive claims rotation may reduce food allergies/sensitivities from repeatedly eating the same foods.</p> <p><p>Excellent principleVariety in the diet.</p> <p>Don't present as establishedRotating foods to prevent allergies — you don't need to tell a healthy client \"you ate oats yesterday, so you can't eat oats today or you'll become sensitive.\"</p> </p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns9-3","chapter":"Alive's seasonal framework","title":"Alive's seasonal framework","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🟢 Seasonal/local foods can be affordable, fresh, culturally meaningful, varied, and enjoyable — not because \"winter food energetically heats your organs.\" 🚨 Local ≠ automatically more nutritious. Don't turn a preference into a universal health rule.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Alive's seasonal framework","items":[{"label":"Spring","detail":"Greens + fresh produce. Also the season Alive names for reevaluation/cleansing/fasting."},{"label":"Summer","detail":"More succulent fruits/vegetables, lighter foods."},{"label":"Autumn","detail":"Root vegetables + squash + cooked foods."},{"label":"Winter","detail":"Whole grains + richer foods + course-specific seaweed/dairy/poultry ideas."}]}},{"id":"ns9-4","chapter":"The same myths return","title":"The same myths return","html":"<p>Alive lists eggs, cheese, wheat, refined flour, honey, and meat as \"acidic\" foods, and claims acid imbalance may contribute to mucus congestion. It also continues food-combining rules, claiming improper combinations cause gas/bloating/indigestion.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🔴 Don't use either theory to explain client symptoms. Blood pH is tightly regulated (Unit 7), and normal digestion handles mixed meals. If someone consistently experiences bloating, assess the actual problem instead of blaming food combinations.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns9-4-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Alive tests: \"Human blood normally has a pH of 8.\" True or false?","options":[{"label":"True","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"False — normal arterial blood pH is about 7.35–7.45","correct":true,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns9-5","chapter":"Personalize","title":"Personalize","html":"<p>Alive says individuals determine dietary needs by considering body, nature, and state of health. Strip away the older wording and this becomes a modern principle: <b>personalize.</b></p> <p>AgeLife stageActivityCulturePreferencesBudgetScheduleHealth statusMedicationsGI toleranceRestrictionsGoals</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> NOT \"THE PERFECT DIET\" — THE RIGHT NEXT CHANGE FOR THIS PERSON</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns9-6","chapter":"Intake ≠ status","title":"Intake ≠ status","html":"<p>Even excellent intake depends on healthy digestion/absorption, stress, and additional needs to actually become nutritional status.</p> <p>🍽️Food</p> <p>→</p> <p>🌀Digest</p> <p>→</p> <p>🩸Absorb</p> <p>→</p> <p>🚚Transport</p> <p>→</p> <p>⚡Use</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🫘 Example: a client eats lentils — does that tell you their iron status? No. Consider amount, frequency, vitamin C, inhibitors/enhancers, overall intake, blood loss, absorption, and medical factors.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> INTAKE ≠ STATUS</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns9-7","chapter":"Possibility of a gap ≠ proof of a deficiency","title":"Possibility of a gap ≠ proof of a deficiency","html":"<p>Alive claims diminishing soil nutrients + processing make obtaining all nutrients from food difficult. Don't translate that into \"everyone needs supplements because modern food is nutritionally empty.\"</p> <p><p>1</p><p>Food first</p> <p>2</p><p>Assess</p> <p>3</p><p>Identify gap/risk</p> <p>4</p><p>Supplement when appropriate</p> </p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 💊 Multinutrient formulas may contain herbs below a \"therapeutic range.\" Read the actual dose — don't assume a label saying \"contains turmeric\" means a meaningful amount, for vitamins, minerals, herbs, or probiotics.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns9-8","chapter":"Practical storage & prep facts","title":"Practical storage & prep facts","html":"","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Practical storage & prep facts","items":[{"label":"No overnight soak needed","detail":"Lentils + split peas + adzuki beans, per Alive's grouping."},{"label":"Wash carefully","detail":"Quinoa + amaranth + millet, per Alive's recommendation."},{"label":"Fresh herbs","detail":"Store in a glass of water in the refrigerator."},{"label":"Nuts + seeds","detail":"Unsaturated fat is susceptible to oxidation (Unit 2) — cooler storage can help preserve them longer, contrary to a room-temperature airtight claim."}]}},{"id":"ns9-9","chapter":"Build-a-meal, not obsessive precision","title":"Build-a-meal, not obsessive precision","html":"<p>Meal planning is one of the most practical tools you'll use — but it doesn't need to be \"Monday: 137g chicken, ½ cup quinoa, 73g broccoli\" unless there's a genuine reason for that precision.</p> <p>🥦Plants</p> <p>🥩Protein</p> <p>🌾High-fibre carb</p> <p>🫒Fat</p> <p>💧Fluid</p> <p>Then personalize portions and composition for the individual.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns9-10","chapter":"Ayurveda, respectfully","title":"Ayurveda, respectfully","html":"<p>Alive says constitutional typing goes back more than 5,000 years to Indian Ayurveda.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🔴 Don't confuse Ayurvedic constitutional types with medical/nutrition diagnoses. Cultural/traditional systems can be discussed respectfully without representing them as equivalent to established biomedical assessment.</p>","visual":null}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Alive's New Basic Four?","back":"Fruits, vegetables, whole grains + legumes, proteins + fats/oils."},{"front":"Variety provides?","back":"Coverage of different nutrients."},{"front":"Moderation means?","back":"Amount matters — not deprivation."},{"front":"Does food rotation prevent allergies?","back":"Not established — course claim, not physiology."},{"front":"Season Alive names for cleansing/reevaluation?","back":"Spring."},{"front":"Is local food automatically more nutritious?","back":"No."},{"front":"Normal blood pH range?","back":"About 7.35–7.45."},{"front":"\"Blood pH of 8\" — true or false in Alive?","back":"False."},{"front":"Constitutional typing traces back to?","back":"Ayurveda (India), 5,000+ years."},{"front":"Beans that don't need overnight soaking?","back":"Lentils, split peas, adzuki beans."},{"front":"Grains needing careful washing?","back":"Quinoa, amaranth, millet."},{"front":"Best fresh herb storage?","back":"Glass of water in the refrigerator."},{"front":"Intake guarantees nutritional status?","back":"No — intake ≠ status."},{"front":"Is there one ideal diet for everyone?","back":"No."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The New Basic Four Food Group is made up of:","o":["Fruits, vegetables, whole grains and legumes, and proteins and fats/oils","Meats, dairy, grains, and fruits and vegetables","Fruits, vegetables, cereal grains, protein, and fats","Vegetables, fruits, whole grains, and protein"],"a":0,"e":"This exact four-part grouping is the course's tested \"New Basic Four.\""},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the 10 key components of a healthy diet?","o":["Natural foods","Balance","Food combining","Moderation","Low fat"],"a":4,"e":"\"Low fat\" is a specific numeric directive rather than one of the broad guiding principles (natural, balanced, moderate, well-combined, etc.) that make up the course's 10 key components."},{"q":"Which of the seasons is best for cleansing, fasting, and reevaluation of our health program?","o":["Spring","Summer","Fall","Winter"],"a":0,"e":"Spring is the course's tested season for cleansing, fasting, and reevaluation."},{"q":"Which of the following best describes the most important component of a healthy diet?","o":["Fruits, nuts, seeds, vegetables, whole grains, and legumes should constitute half of the diet","Eating foods available and grown locally","Eating a variety of foods to obtain a variety of nutrients","Eating a balanced diet and consuming moderate amounts of foods, without regular overeating"],"a":2,"e":"Variety — eating a range of foods to obtain a range of nutrients — is emphasized as the most important component throughout the course."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding seasonal eating is FALSE?","o":["Summer is a time that succulent fruits and vegetables are available and is a good time to shift away from cooked foods, while also reducing heavier cooked fats and protein","Autumn has an abundance of root vegetables and squash. The diet shifts to more cooked foods, whole grains, and richer protein and fat foods.","Winter consists of more complex carbohydrates found in whole grains, and an addition of some dairy and poultry, and is a good time to consume high mineral seaweeds","Spring is when the greens grow freely and fresh fruit and vegetables become more available","None of the statements are false"],"a":4,"e":"All four seasonal descriptions match the course's own seasonal-eating framework — none of them are false."},{"q":"Why are variety and food rotation important practices?","o":["Eating a variety of foods provides a variety of nutrients thus preventing any marked deficiencies","Rotating reduces the potential to become allergic or sensitive, which can result from repeatedly stimulating the body's immune and cellular systems with the same nutritional biochemistry","Eating a variety of foods each day helps to avoid overstressing the digestive system","A and B only","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All three reasons — nutrient variety, reduced allergy/sensitivity potential, and avoiding digestive overstress — are given as reasons for variety and rotation."},{"q":"Which nonessential amino acid, considered to be the least likely needed in terms of diet, may require supplementation to help the body detoxify the food additive sodium benzoate and aspirin?","o":["Glycine","Cystine","Alanine","Glutamine"],"a":0,"e":"Glycine is used in liver conjugation reactions that detoxify benzoate (forming hippuric acid) and is the course's tested amino acid for this role."},{"q":"Which of the following statements concerning acid-alkaline balance is FALSE?","o":["When foods are combusted, they are broken down into an alkaline or acid ash","A diet that is too acidic affects blood and tissues and the body will try to clear unwanted elements through enhanced elimination","Congestion of mucus in different body areas may often be caused in part by an acid-alkaline imbalance","In very cold climates, a higher percentage of richer acid-forming foods may be tolerated as these foods are higher in fats and burn hotter as body fuel.","Some examples of acidic foods include eggs, cheeses, wheat, refined flour, honey, and meat"],"a":3,"e":"This specific cold-climate/fat-burning claim doesn't hold up as well as the other statements, which are directly and repeatedly confirmed elsewhere in the course material."},{"q":"Which of the following is described as: Natural, seasonal, rotational, balanced, moderate, and well-combined?","o":["Constitutional typing","The indigenous diet","The ideal diet","New dietary guidelines"],"a":2,"e":"This six-word description is the course's definition of \"the ideal diet.\""},{"q":"What are some ways individuals can determine their own dietary needs?","o":["Listen to the body","Listen to nature","Be aware of own state of health","A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Listening to the body, listening to nature, and being aware of one's own health state are all given as ways to determine individual dietary needs."},{"q":"Even with an ideal diet, it is hard to maintain an entire balance of nutrients. Our utilization of the nutrients depends on which of the following factors?","o":["Healthy digestion and absorption","Stress levels","Extra needs","B and C","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Digestion/absorption, stress levels, and extra individual needs all affect nutrient utilization."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding healthy eating is FALSE?","o":["Indigestion, gas, bloating, and abdominal discomfort can all result from improper food combinations","After too large of a meal, much more blood than usual is sent to the digestive organs, and we are often sedated and unable to move well until digestion is complete many hours later","Common foods that may generate allergies include cows' milk, wheat, eggs, soybeans, corn, beef, coffee, chocolate, tomatoes, yeast, and shellfish","None of the responses are FALSE"],"a":3,"e":"All three statements reflect the course's own teaching — none of them are false."},{"q":"Which of the following are benefits of eating seasonally?","o":["It is economical","It gives us the cleanest foods, as fewer chemicals are needed to store or ship them","It provides the right type of fuel to protect us from the current climate","B and C","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Economy, cleanliness (fewer chemicals for storage/shipping), and climate-appropriate fuel are all listed as benefits of seasonal eating."},{"q":"Which of the following are the general guidelines for consumption of whole grains by lacto-ovo vegetarians?","o":["10 to 15%","20 to 25%","25 to 35%","None of the above"],"a":2,"e":"25 to 35% is the course's tested whole-grain guideline for lacto-ovo vegetarians."},{"q":"Which of the following are the general guidelines for consumption of meat, poultry, and fish by omnivores?","o":["5 to 10%","10 to 15%","15 to 20%","20 to 25%"],"a":1,"e":"10 to 15% is the course's tested meat/poultry/fish guideline for omnivores."},{"q":"Which season is the best season to reevaluate health programs or create new ones?","o":["Summer","Autumn","Winter","Spring"],"a":3,"e":"Spring is again the tested answer for reevaluating or creating new health programs."},{"q":"Which of the following beans do not require soaking overnight?","o":["Lentils, split peas, and adzuki beans","Lentils, split peas, and kidney beans","Lentils, black beans, and navy beans","All of the above"],"a":0,"e":"Lentils, split peas, and adzuki beans are the course's no-overnight-soak grouping."},{"q":"Eating seasonally – eating foods that are available and grown locally – keeps us attuned to Earth, its elements and the cycles of Nature.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's philosophical framing of seasonal/local eating."},{"q":"When following food combining, it is ok to consume more than one type of protein at the same meal as long as the proteins are not eaten with starches.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Under the food-combining framework, multiple proteins together is acceptable — the restriction is specifically about mixing protein with starch."},{"q":"With the diminishing nutrients in the soil and the high amount of food processing, it is not easy to obtain all of our nutrients from food.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated concern about soil depletion and food processing making complete nutrient intake harder."},{"q":"Human blood has a normal pH of 8.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Normal arterial blood pH is tightly regulated around 7.35-7.45, not 8."},{"q":"Many multinutrient formulas will throw in a few herbs, but typically the levels of herbs used are not enough to be in the naturally therapeutic range.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's caution about under-dosed herbs in multinutrient formulas."},{"q":"Soaking nuts and seeds overnight helps the body digest the fats because the fats in them become more available as fatty acids.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Soaking primarily reduces phytic acid and enzyme inhibitors and improves mineral availability — it doesn't meaningfully convert fats into free fatty acids, making this specific mechanism claim false."},{"q":"Fresh herbs are best kept in a glass of water in the refrigerator.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated fresh-herb storage recommendation."},{"q":"Planning meals in advance and then searching out appropriate foods is the best way to eat","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"This runs counter to the course's seasonal/local eating philosophy, which favors letting what's available guide meal planning rather than the reverse."},{"q":"Quinoa, amaranth, and millet require a more careful washing than other grains, and it is recommended to wash these grains several times prior to cooking.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated grain-washing recommendation for these three grains."},{"q":"The concept of constitutional typing goes back more than 5,000 years to the subcontinent of India with the tradition of ayurveda.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated origin of constitutional typing in Ayurveda."},{"q":"Nuts and seeds are best stored at room temperature in an air-tight container.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Nuts and seeds contain unsaturated fats susceptible to oxidation — cooler storage (fridge/freezer) preserves them longer than room-temperature storage."}],"perStepChapter":false},{"n":10,"title":"Nutrition through the life cycle","kicker":"Lesson 10 of your course. Test practice uses checked answers, because there is no official key for this test.","steps":[{"id":"ns10-0","chapter":"Lesson 10","title":"Nutrition through the life cycle","html":"<p>This lesson covers nutrition at each life stage: infancy, childhood, adolescence, adult men and women, pregnancy, breastfeeding, menopause and older age.</p><p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> The lesson notes for Lesson 10 are not in the study hub yet, so this lesson has test practice only.</p>"}],"flashcards":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of the following would NOT be considered a benefit of breastfeeding?","o":["Breast milk provides the right nourishment and has more available iron, vitamins A and C, niacin, potassium and the right amino acids for growth than any natural or formulated substitute","Breastfed babies have fewer allergies","Breastfeeding stimulates uterine contractions, reducing bleeding and bringing the uterus back to its normal size","Breast milk has lactase, which is the enzyme for digesting lactose, so the baby gets both the milk sugar (lactose) and the enzyme needed to digest it. This is important because the baby’s liver cannot make lactase during the first months of life.","Breast milk filters out pesticides so the baby is protected from contaminants"],"a":4,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"At what age do babies usually develop teeth, allowing finely chopped or pureed meats to be added to a baby's diet?","o":["6 to 7months","7 to 8 months","8 to 9 months","9 to 10 months"],"a":1,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key. Double-check this one against your Lesson 10 notes."},{"q":"What age group requires the most calories per pound of body weight?","o":["Babies","Toddlers","Preschoolers","Adolescents"],"a":0,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"Colic is a common problem during an infant’s first 6 months. Which of the following recommendations may help alleviate colic in a breastfed infant?","o":["Reducing the mother's sugar intake","Reducing the mother's salt intake","Supplementing the mother with B vitamins, calcium, magnesium, and potassium","A and C","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key. Double-check this one against your Lesson 10 notes."},{"q":"Premature feeding of an infant can lead to which of the following problems?","o":["Poor digestion","Immune problems","Tooth decay","A & B","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key. Double-check this one against your Lesson 10 notes."},{"q":"Which vitamins/minerals are important to not overuse in infants?","o":["A, D, calcium, phosphorus, and iron","A, D, magnesium, and iron","A, calcium, phosphorus, and magnesium","A, D, calcium, magnesium, and iron"],"a":0,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"Breastfed babies may have a slight deficiency in which vitamin?","o":["A","D","Iron","B vitamins"],"a":1,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"At what age does a baby’s diet shift as a result of growth slowing down?","o":["6 months","8 months","10 months","12 months"],"a":3,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding childhood nutrition is FALSE?","o":["Allowing children to help prepare meals and lunches will give them more pleasure in eating it","Likes and dislikes can often limit the diets of 5 to 13 year olds","Many parent overestimate their children’s needs and the amount of food required","Offering a sweet as a reward for eating vegetables is helpful with a picky eater","During the middle years, the average youngster gains between 5 and 8 lbs and grows about half an inch per year provided they have the nutrients they need."],"a":3,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"How many mg of calcium does a 4 to 8 year old child require?","o":["200","400","600","800"],"a":3,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"During adolescence, what is the rate of growth for boys and girls?","o":["Young men may gain 15 to 20 lbs and grow 4 to 5 inches in height per year, while girls may gain 13 to 18 lbs and grow 3 to 4 inches per year","Young men may gain 10 to15 lbs and grow 4 to 5 inches in height per year, while girls may gain 13 to 18 lbs and grow 3 to 4 inches per year","Young men may gain 15 to 20 lbs and grow 2 to 4 inches in height per year, while girls may gain 13 to 18 lbs and grow 3 to 4 inches per year","Young men may gain 15 to 20 lbs and grow 4 to 5 inches in height per year, while girls may gain 10 to 15 lbs and grow 3 to 4 inches per year"],"a":0,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"What is typical of the adolescent diet?","o":["Fried foods","Fast foods","Junk foods","Poor nutrition","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"Which of the following nutrients are needed in higher amounts during adolescence?","o":["Calcium","Vitamin D","Iron","A and C","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"What are probably the most influential factors governing longevity?","o":["Heredity","Nutrition","Exercise","A and B","B and C"],"a":3,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"Which of the following nutrients are important for sperm production?","o":["Vitamin A, C and E","Folic acid","EFAs","A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about adult male nutrition is correct?","o":["Much sexual dysfunction has to do with mental stresses and fears of intimacy","Extra zinc, magnesium, and vitamin B6 may be helpful to increase sex drive","Most active, productive men need a good supplement program to protect them from illness and deficiency","A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"Which of the following nutrients can help reduce sugar cravings while helping to support the adrenals?","o":["A, C, and E","Amino acids and chromium","B vitamins, particularly pantothenic acid","A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key. Double-check this one against your Lesson 10 notes."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding adult women and nutrition is FALSE?","o":["Women put on weight easily and have a difficult time losing it; even low calorie diets may not do the trick. Changing food quality may help more than calorie counting","Calcium, magnesium, and niacin may be helpful for menstrual cramps","During menopause, bioidentical hormones may be tolerated better and carry fewer risks than synthetic hormones","Women tend to do best on a moderate calorie diet that includes a good amount of protein and vegetables, some whole grains, as well as nuts, seeds, and a good amount of fruits"],"a":3,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key. Double-check this one against your Lesson 10 notes."},{"q":"Pregnant women need roughly how many extra calories per day than usual?","o":["200 to 300","350 to 450","400 to 500","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"Which of the following statements concerning lactation is correct?","o":["Nutritional requirements are much the same as during pregnancy, with even higher requirements for many nutrients and reduced needs for a few","High amounts of vitamin B6 can reduce milk production and should be avoided","Olive and coconut oil can be used on the nipples to aid nursing and keep the skin healthier","A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"Menopause represents a major transition in the lives of most women. Which of the following recommendations for menopause would be considered FALSE?","o":["During menopause, it is wise for women to get adequate sleep and even take naps if they feel tired because it can also be a time of lowered energy","A good diet along with supportive nutritional supplements and stress management may help to delay the onset of menopause and reduce the symptoms when they occur","Premenopausal women should regularly consume at least 1,200 to 1,500 mg of calcium per day. Squeezing lemon juice or adding apple cider vinegar to leafy greens helps to maximize calcium absorption","Vitamin D and E can often help with hot flashes and night sweats, as can cohosh and licorice root","L-tryptophan should be avoided as it can exacerbate fatigue"],"a":4,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"How much more protein is required during pregnancy?","o":["10%","25%","50%","Protein requirements are the same in pregnant and non-pregnant women"],"a":2,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"After menopause, calcium needs decrease.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"It is best to wait until an infant reaches 6 months of age before feeding him or her solid foods.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"Iron excess is much more common in men than iron deficiency.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"During the menstruating years, a woman’s need for iron to replace lost red blood cells is high.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"A woman’s pre-pregnancy condition has no effect on the baby's health as long as good habits are adopted during the pregnancy.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"Many old-age problems, such as insomnia, anorexia, fatigue, depression, diminishing eyesight and hearing, fragile bones, and fractures are a result of poor diet and nutritional deficiencies.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"It is best to avoid mega-doses of vitamin C during pregnancy.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."},{"q":"A good recommendation for an elder who wishes to avoid/treat constipation is a morning or evening drink made with 4 to 6 oz of prune juice, 2 to 4 oz of water, a quarter or half lemon, and 2 tablespoons of wheat or oat bran.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Checked answer, not from an official key."}]},{"n":11,"title":"Performance Enhancement","kicker":"Lesson 11 of your course, with your official Lesson 11 Test.","steps":[{"id":"ns11-0","chapter":"Protect → Adapt → Perform","title":"Protect → Adapt → Perform","html":"<p>Unit 9 asked how to build the ideal diet. Unit 11 asks: how can nutrition and lifestyle enhance the body's performance and resilience? \"Performance\" doesn't just mean sports — in Haas's framework it includes aging well, coping with stress, immune function, cardiovascular function, athletic performance, tissue repair, energy, and vitality.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> PROTECT THE CELL → MANAGE THE STRESS → SUPPORT IMMUNITY → PROTECT THE HEART → FUEL PERFORMANCE</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-1","chapter":"Two goals, and who it's for","title":"Two goals, and who it's for","html":"<p>Haas gives his Anti-Aging program two goals: <b>increase longevity</b> by reducing the potential for degenerative disease, and <b>improve vitality and tissue health</b> through nutrition. Aging varies considerably between individuals due to genetics and constitution, but lifestyle, diet, and exercise can influence how it manifests.</p> <p>This program is for more than \"elderly people\":</p> <p>People over 40People under stressPeople with demanding jobsPeople exposed to chemicalsPeople eating processed/red/cured meatsSmokers and alcohol drinkers</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-2","chapter":"Cardiovascular + nervous systems lead the list","title":"Cardiovascular + nervous systems lead the list","html":"<p>Haas identifies the most common aging-related problems as affecting the <b>cardiovascular</b> and <b>nervous</b> systems — atherosclerosis, reduced circulation, reduced oxygen/nutrient delivery, slower nerve conduction, reduced reflexes, memory/learning changes — plus changes in the musculoskeletal, gastrointestinal, genitourinary, endocrine, and immune systems.</p> <p>What accelerates aging, per Haas:</p> <p>SmokingExcess alcoholExcess dietary fatChemicals in foodPoor/deficient dietOvereatingStressPollutionInactivity</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> SMOKE · DRINK · OVER-EAT · UNDER-MOVE · OVER-STRESS</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-3","chapter":"Cross-linking and the aging clock","title":"Cross-linking and the aging clock","html":"<p>Haas lists several theories: stagnation/toxicity, aging clock, cross-linking of proteins, free radicals, DNA errors, brain changes, autoimmunity, and stress.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Cross-linking and the aging clock","items":[{"label":"Aging clock theory","detail":"Cells have an inherent, preset potential number of divisions. The number of divisions and the time between them contribute to lifespan."},{"label":"Cross-linking theory","detail":"Molecular changes in tissue proteins cause microfibres to be laid down against the normal direction of other tissue fibres. Result: elasticity decreases, stiffness increases, degeneration increases."}]}},{"id":"ns11-4","chapter":"The most accepted aging hypothesis in the book","title":"The most accepted aging hypothesis in the book","html":"<p>A free radical is an unstable, reactive molecule with a free electron — it reacts with other molecules and may damage cell membranes, fats, and tissue linings. Haas links free-radical generation to oxygen metabolism, chemicals, smoking, microbes, and unsaturated fats.</p> <p>🔥Free radical: \"I need an electron!\"</p> <p>↔</p> <p>🛡️Antioxidant: \"I'll neutralize you.\"</p> <p>Haas calls antioxidants \"free-radical scavengers.\" Major examples:</p> <p>Vitamin EVitamin CSeleniumBeta-carotene</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-5","chapter":"Eating lightly, and the mitochondrial coenzyme","title":"Eating lightly, and the mitochondrial coenzyme","html":"<p><p>1</p><p>Eat relatively lightly, avoid obesity</p> <p>2</p><p>Keep fat low (no more than ~25% of calories)</p> <p>3</p><p>Focus on complex carbohydrates + fibre-rich foods</p> <p>4</p><p>Moderate protein (~50-70 g/day, much from vegetable sources)</p> <p>5</p><p>Eat fewer chemicals; moderate salt, sugar, alcohol, nicotine, caffeine</p> <p>6</p><p>Drink plenty of water; periodic cleansing/detoxification</p> </p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> ⚙️ <b>CoQ10 (coenzyme Q10/ubiquinone)</b> supports cardiac muscle function and possibly specific immune functions. Memory chain: CoQ10 → mitochondria → ATP/energy → heart.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-6","chapter":"Fight or flight, one chain at a time","title":"Fight or flight, one chain at a time","html":"<p>Haas defines stress as our reaction to the external environment plus internal thoughts and feelings — fundamentally the body's fight-or-flight response. Some stress is necessary: mild stress can support motivation, activity, and productivity. The problem is stress that becomes too intense, too frequent, or too prolonged.</p> <p>Perceived danger</p> <p>→</p> <p>Brain + pituitary</p> <p>→</p> <p>ACTH</p> <p>→</p> <p>Adrenal glands</p> <p>→</p> <p>Epinephrine + norepinephrine + cortisol</p> <p>Result: heart rate ↑, blood pressure ↑, alertness ↑, blood flow toward muscles/brain, blood flow away from the digestive tract, blood glucose ↑, metabolism ↑. Adrenaline specifically stimulates the liver to release more glucose into the blood so cells have energy available.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-7","chapter":"Digestion is hit first","title":"Digestion is hit first","html":"<p>During stress, energy is diverted away from digestion — Haas considers the digestive tract the body area most easily influenced by stress, followed by the neurological system, circulatory system, and muscles.</p> <p>Haas's seven types of stress:</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> STRESS IS NOT JUST THE EVENT — IT IS HOW WE REACT TO IT</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Digestion is hit first","items":[{"label":"Physical","detail":"Exercise, hard labour, birth."},{"label":"Chemical","detail":"Pollution, pesticides, cleaning solvents, drugs, alcohol, caffeine, nicotine."},{"label":"Mental","detail":"Responsibility, long hours, perfectionism, anxiety, worry."},{"label":"Emotional","detail":"Anger, fear, frustration, sadness, betrayal, bereavement."},{"label":"Nutritional","detail":"Deficiencies, protein/fat excess or deficiency, food allergies."},{"label":"Traumatic","detail":"Infection, injury, burns, surgery, extreme temperatures."},{"label":"Psycho-spiritual","detail":"Relationships, finances, career, life goals, happiness."}]}},{"id":"ns11-7-x","title":"Explore: Digestion is hit first","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"Acute stress","text":"Stomach HCl ↑May contribute to indigestion, heartburn, gastritis, ulcer problems."},{"label":"Chronic stress","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns11-8","chapter":"A big exam area","title":"A big exam area","html":"<p>Haas lists vitamins A, E, C, and B vitamins, plus minerals zinc, selenium, calcium, magnesium, iron, potassium, sulfur, and molybdenum as depleted by stress. Stressed bodies also use more carbohydrate, protein, fat, and fatty acids.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🔥 Two especially important anti-stress nutrient groups: <b>B vitamins + vitamin C.</b> Within the B vitamins, Haas singles out <b>B5/pantothenic acid</b> as perhaps the most important \"anti-stress\" B vitamin, connecting B5 + folate + vitamin C with adrenal function. Major mineral roles go to potassium, calcium, and magnesium — magnesium is described as a \"tranquilizing mineral\" supporting nervous-system balance and heart function.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-9","chapter":"Not a supplement-only approach","title":"Not a supplement-only approach","html":"<p>Haas recommends: have fun, express feelings, sleep well, relax, exercise, develop supportive relationships, experience love/intimacy, and change unhelpful perceptions and attitudes.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> RELAX → EAT → REST</p> <p>He suggests small, wholesome meals for highly stressed people, emphasizes water, and says a lighter <b>detoxification-type diet may be useful at times of intense stress.</b></p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-10","chapter":"Friend vs. foe","title":"Friend vs. foe","html":"<p>Immunity protects against viruses, bacteria, yeasts/fungi, foreign proteins, and cancer cells. Haas divides it into innate/nonspecific and acquired/specific immunity.</p> <p>Physical barriers (skin, mucous membranes, mucus, cilia)</p> <p>→</p> <p>Immune cells (T cells, B cells, NK cells, macrophages, neutrophils)</p> <p>→</p> <p>Chemical defences (antibodies, complement, interferon, lysozymes)</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Friend vs. foe","items":[{"label":"T cells","detail":"Originate in bone marrow, mature in the thymus — which Haas calls the \"king\" of the immune system. T-helper cells stimulate immune activity; T-suppressor cells slow certain immune functions after a problem has been handled."},{"label":"B cells","detail":"Originate in bone marrow, become antibody-producing cells, and contribute to immune memory."}]}},{"id":"ns11-11","chapter":"A secondary circulatory system with no pump","title":"A secondary circulatory system with no pump","html":"<p>Haas calls the lymphatic system a <b>secondary circulatory system</b> — it collects foreign cells/proteins and eventually dumps them into the blood for breakdown/elimination. It has <b>no pump</b>, so Haas emphasizes muscle activity and exercise as what actually moves lymph.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🍽️ <b>Phagocytes</b> (phago = eat) are white blood cells that patrol the body, find foreign material, engulf it, and kill/dissolve it. Haas particularly discusses neutrophils and macrophages.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-12","chapter":"Immunity as a whole-lifestyle function","title":"Immunity as a whole-lifestyle function","html":"<p>Suppressors include aging, infection, surgery, radiation, chemotherapy, some drugs, poor sleep, travel, stress, depression/loneliness, overeating, high-fat diet, sugar, malnutrition, nutrient deficiencies, environmental chemicals, alcohol, and nicotine. Nutritionally, Haas particularly highlights <b>low protein, low vitamin A, and low zinc</b>.</p> <p>Supporters, per Haas's chart:</p> <p>Positive attitudesLoveLaughterRelaxation/meditationExercise/yogaWholesome foodsAdequate proteinLow-fat/low-sugar dietWaterFastingEssential fatty acids</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-13","chapter":"A classic exam trap","title":"A classic exam trap","html":"<p>One tested statement claims: \"When fighting an infection, reduce fever as quickly as possible and keep temperature below 100°F.\" That is <b>not</b> Haas's position in this program — the statement is false.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-14","chapter":"Beta-carotene, vitamin C, and protective foods","title":"Beta-carotene, vitamin C, and protective foods","html":"<p>Recognized risk factors include excess sunlight/radiation, dietary excesses, psychological influences, and undernutrition. The dietary pattern emphasizes reducing obesity and dietary fat while increasing protective whole-food intake.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🥦 <b>Cruciferous vegetables, onion, and garlic</b> are all included in relation to detoxifying enzymes/carcinogens — \"all of the above\" is the tested answer. Foods to avoid/reduce: pickled, smoked, and barbecued foods, and excessive protein. <b>Poultry</b> is given as the \"do NOT avoid\" choice.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Beta-carotene, vitamin C, and protective foods","items":[{"label":"Beta-carotene","detail":"Associated with deficiency among many smokers who develop lung cancer, and with protection of mucous-membrane cells. Memory: beta-carotene → barrier/membrane protection."},{"label":"Vitamin C","detail":"Reduces carcinogen production (specifically nitrosamine formation) and strengthens immune cells/cell membranes against viruses and toxic chemicals."}]}},{"id":"ns11-15","chapter":"Highly preventable through lifestyle change","title":"Highly preventable through lifestyle change","html":"<p>The big risk factors: <b>smoking, high cholesterol, high blood pressure</b> — all of the above is the tested answer.</p> <p>The book pays attention to total-cholesterol:HDL and LDL:HDL ratios. A statement claiming \"lower HDL while increasing LDL\" is <b>false</b> — that's backwards from the desired direction.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🩸 <b>Atherosclerosis:</b> damage → buildup → narrowing. Material accumulates within arterial walls — lipids, smooth muscle cells, fibrin, calcium — potentially beginning with microinjury to the vascular lining, with platelets and white blood cells contributing. Symptoms may not occur until substantial closure; around <b>70-80% closure</b> is more likely to produce angina.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-15-x","title":"Explore: Highly preventable through lifestyle change","teach":[],"act":{"type":"explore","prompt":"Open each one to compare.","panels":[{"label":"HDL","text":"Blood → LiverDescribed as protective. Higher HDL = desired direction."},{"label":"LDL","text":""}]}},{"id":"ns11-16","chapter":"Exercise + nutrition work together","title":"Exercise + nutrition work together","html":"<p>Athletes place additional demands on the body through movement, sweating, tissue stress, energy expenditure, and muscle work — activity up means nutrient/energy need up.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🏃 <b>Aerobic exercise:</b> continuous repetitive movement of large muscle groups for more than 10-15 minutes. Maximum heart-rate formula: <b>220 − age</b>, with a typical training range of <b>70-85% of maximum.</b></p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-17","chapter":"Not just protein","title":"Not just protein","html":"<p>Haas strongly rejects the idea that athletic nutrition should simply mean enormous protein intake. His model: carbohydrates → energy, protein → build/repair, fat → support/lubrication. The athlete diet centers on whole grains, pasta, legumes, potatoes, starchy vegetables, plus adequate protein, fruits, and relatively moderate fat.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 50–60% CARBOHYDRATE · 15–20% PROTEIN · 25–30% FAT</p> <p>Carbohydrate further breaks down as 10-20% simple, 40-50% complex. Protein maximum is approximately 25%. More than half of dietary fat should be unsaturated.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-18","chapter":"What exercise costs the body","title":"What exercise costs the body","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> SWEAT → WATER + ELECTROLYTES + B1</p> <p>Regular sweating particularly increases losses of water, B1, sodium, potassium, chloride, and magnesium. <b>Dehydration</b> is one of Haas's biggest athlete concerns: low fluid intake → tissue perfusion ↓ → oxygen/nutrient delivery ↓ → fatigue → performance ↓. Water should be the primary liquid.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"What exercise costs the body","items":[{"label":"Calcium + magnesium","detail":"The calcium-magnesium cellular exchange supports muscle contraction, muscle relaxation, nerve conduction, bone/cellular strength, and oxygen delivery to muscles."},{"label":"Iron","detail":"Especially important for female athletes — iron → hemoglobin → oxygen transport, plus iron is part of myoglobin in muscle. Iron → oxygen → energy/endurance."}]}},{"id":"ns11-19","chapter":"More metabolic activity → more need for protection","title":"More metabolic activity → more need for protection","html":"<p>Vigorous exercise increases free-radical production, so Haas emphasizes A/beta-carotene, C, E, and selenium for athletes — the same anti-aging antioxidant concept, applied to exercise stress.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 💪 <b>BCAAs (branched-chain amino acids): leucine, isoleucine, valine.</b> They make up about one-third of muscle tissue and can be useful for muscle building. Haas recommends taking them together, approximately <b>30-60 minutes before a workout.</b></p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns11-20","chapter":"The rest of the chapter","title":"The rest of the chapter","html":"","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"The rest of the chapter","items":[{"label":"Vitamin E + fertility","detail":"Associated with sperm motility; deficiency has been related to male infertility. (Zinc is kept separate — linked to libido, sperm count, and male sexual function.)"},{"label":"Glucosamine","detail":"Supports cartilage — has been shown to help joint pain and stiffness because it appears to support/build normal cartilage."},{"label":"Vegetarianism","detail":"If vegetarians eat a balanced diet, deficiencies are rare — but the added claim that \"supplementation is not recommended\" makes that full statement false in the book's framework. Separately: a primarily vegetarian diet is generally helpful in preventing cancer — true for this program."},{"label":"Environmental pollution + radiation","detail":"Revisits Unit 8's territory through the performance/protection lens — organic food, glutathione, salt-soda baths, miso, and kelp/iodine around radioactive iodine exposure. Note: Haas's own antioxidant discussion says the body's SOD and glutathione peroxidase are rapidly metabolized and not readily absorbed as oral supplements — worth memorizing the exact wording rather than extrapolating."}]}}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Haas's two anti-aging program goals?","back":"Increase longevity + improve vitality/tissue health."},{"front":"Systems most affected by aging?","back":"Cardiovascular + nervous systems."},{"front":"Cross-linking theory in one line?","back":"Abnormally linked protein fibres → stiffness, loss of elasticity."},{"front":"What is a free radical?","back":"An unstable, reactive molecule with a free electron."},{"front":"Haas's term for antioxidants?","back":"Free-radical scavengers."},{"front":"CoQ10's memory chain?","back":"CoQ10 → mitochondria → ATP/energy → heart."},{"front":"Stress physiology chain?","back":"Brain/pituitary → ACTH → adrenals → epinephrine/norepinephrine/cortisol."},{"front":"Body area most easily affected by stress?","back":"The digestive tract."},{"front":"Most important \"anti-stress\" B vitamin?","back":"B5 / pantothenic acid."},{"front":"Haas's \"tranquilizing mineral\"?","back":"Magnesium."},{"front":"Where do T cells mature?","back":"The thymus — Haas's \"king\" of the immune system."},{"front":"What do B cells become?","back":"Antibody-producing cells, contributing to immune memory."},{"front":"Haas's term for the lymphatic system?","back":"A secondary circulatory system with no pump."},{"front":"Three major nutritional immune suppressors Haas highlights?","back":"Low protein, low vitamin A, low zinc."},{"front":"Beta-carotene's cancer-prevention anchor?","back":"Barrier/mucous-membrane protection; linked to lung cancer risk in smokers when deficient."},{"front":"Vitamin C's cancer-prevention anchor?","back":"Reduces nitrosamine formation, protects cell membranes."},{"front":"Three CVD risk factors Haas emphasizes?","back":"Smoking, high cholesterol, high blood pressure."},{"front":"HDL vs. LDL direction?","back":"Higher HDL and lower LDL are the desired directions."},{"front":"Maximum heart rate formula?","back":"220 − age."},{"front":"Haas's athlete macronutrient ratio?","back":"50–60% carbohydrate / 15–20% protein / 25–30% fat."},{"front":"What do BCAAs stand for and when to take them?","back":"Leucine, isoleucine, valine — together, 30–60 minutes before a workout."},{"front":"Which mineral is especially important for female athletes?","back":"Iron (hemoglobin + myoglobin → oxygen transport)."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The anti-aging program can be employed by:","o":["Anyone over the age of 40","Those on diets of processed foods, red meats, and cured meats","People under stress","A and C","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"People over 40, those eating processed/red/cured meats, and people under stress are all named as candidates for the anti-aging program."},{"q":"The most common problems of aging affect which of the following?","o":["Cardiovascular system","Nervous system","Endocrine system","A and B"],"a":3,"e":"Cardiovascular and nervous systems are the most commonly affected systems in this program's aging framework."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT considered a factor in increasing the effects of aging?","o":["Excess alcohol consumption","Hanging on to depression, anger, and grief","Inactivity","Overreating","all are factors that increase the effects of aging"],"a":4,"e":"Alcohol, unresolved negative emotions, inactivity, and overeating are all named factors that accelerate aging."},{"q":"Which of the following is the aging theory that suggests that molecular changes occur in the protein molecules of body tissues, causing microfibres to be laid down against the normal direction of other tissue fibres, therefore creating loss of elasticity, stiffness, and degeneration?","o":["The free-radical theory","The aging clock theory","The telomerase inactivity theory","The cross-linking theory"],"a":3,"e":"This is the cross-linking theory of aging."},{"q":"Which of the following supplements may enhance specific immune functions and is critical for energy production by the mitochondria in our cells?","o":["L-cysteine","CoQ10","Hydrochloric acid","Selenium"],"a":1,"e":"CoQ10 supports mitochondrial energy production and may enhance certain immune functions."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about stress is FALSE?","o":["Stress may come to be seen as the primary contributing cause of most diseases","The brain and pituitary gland respond to stress by releasing the adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), which stimulates the adrenals to increase production of hormones epinephrine, norepinephrine, and cortisol","The adrenaline released during stress raises blood sugar as it stimulates the liver to produce and release more glucose into the blood","Stress experienced around the time of eating diverts the energy needed for efficient digestion. Eating smaller amounts of very high quality food is essential.","All parts of the body are equally affected by stress"],"a":4,"e":"Not all parts of the body are equally affected — the digestive tract is described as the area most easily influenced by stress, followed by the neurological, circulatory, and muscular systems."},{"q":"Which of the following nutrients are commonly depleted by stress?","o":["A, E, C, and the B vitamins","Zinc, selenium, and calcium","Magnesium, iron, potassium, sulfur, and molybdenum","A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"This comprehensive list of vitamins and minerals are all named as depleted by stress."},{"q":"Low levels of which nutrient may lead to impotence, a low sperm count, and a loss of sexual interest?","o":["B12","Niacin","Zinc","Vitamin C"],"a":2,"e":"Zinc is the tested nutrient for these specific sexual-health associations."},{"q":"Which of the following are considered monounsaturated fats?","o":["Olives, pecans, and walnuts","Olive oil, sesame oil, and fish","Avocados, pecans, and corn","Avocados, olives, and cashews"],"a":3,"e":"Avocados, olives, and cashews are the grouping most consistently rich in monounsaturated fat among these options."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about environmental pollution and radiation is FALSE?","o":["Buying and eating certified organic foods as much as possible will help minimize exposure to pesticides and chemicals","Glutathione is helpful when utilized as an oral supplement to protect the lung membranes","A salt-soda bath following airline flights or long hours at a computer can be used for detoxification","Miso is known to protect against pollution and radiation.","With any radioactive iodine tests or exposure to iodine fallout, take kelp or iodine for several weeks before and after the test."],"a":1,"e":"Oral glutathione has poor bioavailability and is largely broken down during digestion, making this specific claim about protecting lung membranes questionable compared to the other statements."},{"q":"Which body system removes foreign cells and proteins, eventually dumping them into the blood to be broken down?","o":["Endocrine system","Lymphatic system","Nervous system","Urinary system"],"a":1,"e":"The lymphatic system collects foreign cells/proteins and eventually releases them into the blood for breakdown."},{"q":"Which of the following statements relating to the immune system is FALSE?","o":["When an infection is present, it can commonly be felt in the lymph nodes in the area closest to the infection","Phagocytic white blood cells patrol the body for foreign substances and microorganisms, which they surround and kill or dissolve with their chemicals","Low protein intake and vitamin A and zinc deficiencies are most relevant to immune suppression","Fasting and detoxification diets can strengthen immune functions and reduce immune overload and reactions, as can be seen in allergies, infections, or autoimmune problems, such as rheumatoid arthritis","When fighting an infection, it is important to reduce a fever as quickly as possible, keeping the internal body temperature below 100 degrees Fahrenheit"],"a":4,"e":"Rapidly suppressing fever below 100°F is not the program's position — this is the deliberately false statement."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a key risk factor for cancer?","o":["Undernutrition","Excess sunlight and radiation","Dietary excesses","Psychological influences","All of the above are risk factors"],"a":4,"e":"Undernutrition, excess sunlight/radiation, dietary excesses, and psychological influences are all named cancer risk factors."},{"q":"Which nutrient has been shown to be deficient in a large proportion of smokers who develop lung cancer, as it seems to specifically protect cells of the mucous membranes?","o":["Ascorbic acid","Beta–carotene","Vitamin E","Zinc"],"a":1,"e":"Beta-carotene is specifically tied to mucous-membrane protection and lung cancer risk in smokers."},{"q":"Which nutrient reduces carcinogen production and strengthens immune cells and cell membranes against the penetration of viruses and toxic chemicals?","o":["Ascorbic acid","Beta-carotene","Vitamin E","Zinc"],"a":0,"e":"Ascorbic acid (vitamin C) reduces carcinogen (nitrosamine) production and strengthens cell membranes against viruses and toxins."},{"q":"Which of the following foods can stimulate detoxifying enzymes and inhibit enzymes that create carcinogens?","o":["Cruciferous vegetables","Onion","Garlic","B and C","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Cruciferous vegetables, onion, and garlic are all named for this detoxifying-enzyme effect."},{"q":"Which of the following foods should a person following a cancer-prevention diet NOT avoid?","o":["Pickled food","Poultry","Barbecued foods","Excessive proteins","Smoked foods"],"a":1,"e":"Poultry is the food that does NOT need to be avoided — the others (pickled, barbecued, smoked foods, excessive protein) are the ones to reduce."},{"q":"A primary risk factor for cardiovascular disease is:","o":["Cigarette smoking","High cholesterol","High blood pressure","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Smoking, high cholesterol, and high blood pressure are all named as primary CVD risk factors."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding cardiovascular disease and prevention is FALSE?","o":["A prevention approach for minimizing blood vessel inflammation is to follow a primarily alkaline diet higher in fresh fruits and vegetables.","The ratios of total cholesterol to HDL and LDL to HDL are considered important parameters in evaluating cardiovascular risk","Focus should be on lowering HDL cholesterol while increasing LDL","Increased fiber – at least 20-30 grams daily – will help reduce blood pressure levels in those with elevations","Managing stress is an important key to cardiovascular health"],"a":2,"e":"This is backwards — the goal is to raise HDL and lower LDL, not the reverse, making this the false statement."},{"q":"Which of the following statements below describes an element of atherosclerosis?","o":["The hardening of the inner arterial walls with lipids, smooth muscle cells from the blood vessel walls themselves, fibrin and calcium","Stimulated and added to by platelets and white blood cells","Is thought to begin with minor microinjuries to the vascular linings","A blood vessel usually must be more than half closed before symptoms, such as chest pain occurs","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All of these describe recognized elements of the atherosclerosis process, including that vessels are typically more than half closed before symptoms like angina appear."},{"q":"Which of the following is a good recommendation for a balanced diet for athletes?","o":["50 to 60% carbs, 15 to 20% protein, 25 to 30% fat","30 to 40% carbs, 20 to 30% protein, 30 to 40% fat","30 to 40% carbs, 30 to 40% protein, 10 to 20% fat","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"50-60% carbohydrate, 15-20% protein, 25-30% fat is the tested athlete ratio."},{"q":"A deficiency of which vitamin (associated with sperm motility) has been related in part to male infertility?","o":["Vitamin E","Vitamin A","Vitamin C","Vitamin K"],"a":0,"e":"Vitamin E is associated with sperm motility and male fertility."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding nutrition and athletes is FALSE?","o":["Regular sweating causes the loss of water, vitamin B1, sodium, potassium, chloride, and magnesium","Protein is important for athletes, but excess intake can produce clogging of the colon and stress on the kidneys","Calcium-magnesium cellular exchange supports delivery of blood to the muscles","Our maximum aerobic exercise heart rate (calculated simply) is 220 minus our age"],"a":2,"e":"The calcium-magnesium cellular exchange is more directly tied to muscle contraction/relaxation and nerve conduction than literally \"delivering blood\" to muscles, making this the imprecise/false statement."},{"q":"Our cell membranes can be protected only through a diet that is rich in antioxidant nutrients.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"The word \"only\" makes this an overly absolute claim — cell membrane protection isn't exclusively a matter of antioxidant intake."},{"q":"A detox diet may be useful at times of intense stress.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the program's stated recommendation for lighter, detox-type eating during intense stress."},{"q":"Lactic acid buildup helps fight free radicals in the body.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Lactic acid is not an antioxidant and is not described as fighting free radicals."},{"q":"Glucosamine has been shown to help in joint pain and stiffness, as it appears to be used by the body to support and build normal cartilage.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the program's stated glucosamine/cartilage claim."},{"q":"If vegetarians regularly eat a balanced diet, deficiencies are rare and supplementation is not recommended","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"While deficiencies being rare on a balanced vegetarian diet is accurate, the added claim that supplementation is \"not recommended\" makes the full statement false."},{"q":"Supplementing with branched-chain amino acids can be helpful to people working on muscle building. When supplementing with BCAAs, it is necessary to take them together, about 30 to 60 minutes before a workout.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the program's stated BCAA timing recommendation."},{"q":"A primarily vegetarian diet is generally helpful in preventing cancer.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the program's stated position connecting vegetarian diets with cancer prevention."}],"perStepChapter":false},{"n":12,"title":"Fatigue, Weight and Conditions","kicker":"Lesson 12 of your course, with your official Lesson 12 Test.","steps":[{"id":"ns12-0","chapter":"A symptom with many possible roots","title":"A symptom with many possible roots","html":"<p>The assessment frames fatigue as multifactorial — allergies, poor digestion, colon toxicity, and hypoglycemia are all named as contributing factors, alongside underlying issues like low thyroid function, adrenal function, and Candida.</p> <p>AllergiesPoor digestionColon toxicityHypoglycemiaLow thyroidAdrenal functionCandida</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🍽️ <b>The fatigue diet:</b> easily digested complex carbohydrates + adequate protein + at least 2 quarts of filtered water daily. 🔋 <b>Key mineral:</b> magnesium — supports cell respiration and energy production. 🦋 <b>Key nutrient for thyroid-related fatigue:</b> iodine. 💪 <b>Amino acid for energy/endurance:</b> L-carnitine (500-750mg twice daily), important for fatty-acid metabolism and efficient fat use in cells.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns12-1","chapter":"A multifaceted approach","title":"A multifaceted approach","html":"<p>The assessment describes a multi-part approach to CFS/fibromyalgia: herbal support (ginseng, ginger, turmeric, gingko), avoiding sugar/refined flour/caffeine/alcohol, nutritional therapy (magnesium, B12, other B vitamins, amino acids), and treating chronic infections (bladder, vagina, prostate, sinuses).</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 One item that does <b>not</b> fit this approach: weekly juice fasting to relieve digestive problems. For someone already fatigued and nutritionally taxed, regular fasting risks worsening rather than relieving the underlying issue — a reminder that even \"healthy\" interventions need to match the person's actual condition. Regular vigorous exercise is a similar trap — CFS is associated with post-exertional malaise, so pushing hard exercise can set someone back rather than help.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns12-2","chapter":"Acute vs. chronic, and the nutrient support","title":"Acute vs. chronic, and the nutrient support","html":"<p>The immune response to viruses works primarily through lymphocyte attack functions and the formation of specific antiviral antibodies. Cell membrane integrity — supported mainly by vitamins C, E, A, and essential fatty acids — helps prevent a viral infection from taking hold in the first place.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 💊 <b>Acute viral support:</b> rest, fluids, extra vitamin A, beta-carotene, vitamin C, and zinc — vitamin C especially is used rapidly by a sick/healing body, described as needed almost every hour. Zinc + vitamin A enhance each other's immune-supportive effects. If using higher-dose zinc for an acute infection, add extra copper and manganese (and possibly iron) for several weeks afterward, since high-dose zinc can compete with these minerals. Echinacea is named as a mild \"antibiotic\" option during the initial phase of infection, in place of pharmaceutical antibiotics for appropriate situations.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Acute vs. chronic, and the nutrient support","items":[{"label":"Hepatitis A","detail":"Associated with fatigue, yellowing of skin/eyes, and possibly an initial period of dark urine and pale/gray stools."},{"label":"Hepatitis B & C","detail":"Transmitted through blood contact, with some possible exposure through sexual activity."}]}},{"id":"ns12-3","chapter":"BMR, behaviour patterns, and the toxin-release problem","title":"BMR, behaviour patterns, and the toxin-release problem","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> ⚙️ <b>BMR (Basal Metabolic Rate):</b> the rate the body burns calories to maintain function at rest — affected by gender, age, diet, activity level, thyroid function, sleep, body fat, temperature, weight, and genetics.</p>  <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 Daily weighing to \"stay on track\" is <b>not</b> one of the recommended behaviour patterns — it can create anxiety and an unhelpful, obsessive relationship with the number rather than the behaviour itself.</p> <p>During weight loss, more tissue is broken down and stored toxins can be released in greater amounts — supporting the body's detox capacity matters here (higher-dose vitamin C with vitamin E and selenium is one suggested approach). Lemon water 15-30 minutes before meals is suggested to support digestion and fat utilization.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"BMR, behaviour patterns, and the toxin-release problem","items":[{"label":"Rotate foods","detail":"Supports variety instead of repetitive eating."},{"label":"Eat most food early","detail":"Front-loads calories for better use through the day."},{"label":"Focus on what you eat","detail":"Not on what you're avoiding — a positive framing."}]}},{"id":"ns12-4","chapter":"Hyperthyroidism","title":"Hyperthyroidism","html":"<p>Weight loss or inability to gain weight, alongside rapid heart rate, sweaty palms, and insomnia, points toward hyperthyroidism — a reminder that unexplained weight changes deserve a look beyond diet alone.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns12-5","chapter":"The three-faceted approach","title":"The three-faceted approach","html":"<p>Common features: cravings for and overeating of sweets, breads, or alcohol; sensitivity to molds, dampness, and certain smells; mental symptoms like depression, mood swings, or confusion; and chronic fatigue, indigestion, or food reactions.</p> <p><p>1</p><p>🍗 <b>Starve it</b> — eat foods that don't let yeast thrive: poultry, fish, nuts, seeds, occasional eggs.</p> <p>2</p><p>🧄 <b>Diminish it</b> — fresh garlic, caprylic acid, or oregano oil.</p> <p>3</p><p>🦠 <b>Replenish</b> — restore the colon with friendly bacteria.</p> </p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> STARVE IT → DIMINISH IT → REPLENISH</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns12-6","chapter":"Identifying triggers","title":"Identifying triggers","html":"<p>Foods identified as possible migraine triggers via allergic-type reactions: alcoholic beverages, chocolate, eggs, and cheese.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🔍 <b>Best practical testing for food allergies:</b> an elimination diet — removing suspected foods, then reintroducing them one at a time to observe reactions. This is considered more practical than lab-based methods in many nutrition-practice contexts.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns12-7","chapter":"Blood sugar, hormones, and B vitamins","title":"Blood sugar, hormones, and B vitamins","html":"<p>PMS is aggravated by low blood sugar generated by stress and intake of refined flour/sugar products. Low progesterone levels are pointed to as a main factor in PMS symptoms.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🧪 Magnesium, supplemented at amounts up to <b>1.5x the calcium level</b>, is suggested to help reduce some PMS symptoms. Anxiety symptoms — mood swings, irritability — often respond to extra B vitamins, particularly <b>thiamin (B1)</b> and <b>pyridoxine (B6)</b>.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns12-8","chapter":"Supporting healing","title":"Supporting healing","html":"<p><p>1</p><p>Avoid congestive foods around the time of surgery.</p> <p>2</p><p>Vitamins A and C, plus zinc, can help speed healing time.</p> <p>3</p><p>A gentler detoxification approach can be considered a couple of months post-surgery, especially where general anesthesia was used — allowing the body to stabilize and heal first.</p> </p>","visual":null}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Fatigue diet emphasizes?","back":"Easily digested complex carbs + adequate protein + 2 quarts water daily."},{"front":"Mineral tied to cell respiration/energy?","back":"Magnesium."},{"front":"Nutrient for thyroid-related fatigue?","back":"Iodine."},{"front":"Amino acid for energy/endurance via fatty-acid metabolism?","back":"L-carnitine."},{"front":"What does NOT belong in a CFS/fibromyalgia protocol?","back":"Weekly juice fasting / vigorous exercise."},{"front":"Acute viral support nutrients?","back":"Vitamin A, beta-carotene, vitamin C, zinc."},{"front":"After high-dose zinc for infection, add?","back":"Copper + manganese (possibly iron)."},{"front":"BMR stands for?","back":"Basal Metabolic Rate."},{"front":"NOT a recommended weight-management behaviour?","back":"Weighing yourself daily."},{"front":"Weight loss releases stored?","back":"Toxins (from fat tissue)."},{"front":"Hyperthyroidism symptoms?","back":"Weight loss, rapid heart rate, sweaty palms, insomnia."},{"front":"Three-part yeast syndrome approach?","back":"Starve it, diminish it, replenish good bacteria."},{"front":"Best practical food-allergy test?","back":"Elimination diet."},{"front":"PMS magnesium guideline?","back":"Up to 1.5x the calcium level."},{"front":"B vitamins for PMS mood symptoms?","back":"Thiamin (B1) and pyridoxine (B6)."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of the following are factors contributing to fatigue?","o":["Allergies","Poor digestion","Toxicity from the colon","Hypoglycemia","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Allergies, poor digestion, colon toxicity, and hypoglycemia are all named contributing factors to fatigue."},{"q":"Which of the following are common underlying problems associated with fatigue?","o":["Low thyroid (hypothyroidism)","Adrenal gland function","Candida","A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Low thyroid function, adrenal function, and Candida are all named underlying fatigue problems."},{"q":"Which of the following is the most appropriate diet for people with fatigue?","o":["High in easily digested complex carbohydrates and adequate amounts of protein foods, along with at least 2 quarts of filtered, good-quality water daily","High in protein and adequate amounts of easily digested complex carbohydrates, along with at least 2 quarts of filtered, good-quality water daily","High in easily digested complex carbohydrates and adequate amounts of fats and protein foods, with at least 2 quarts of filtered, good-quality water daily","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"Complex carbohydrates as the primary focus, with adequate (not excessive) protein and plenty of water, is the tested fatigue-diet approach."},{"q":"A deficiency of which mineral is a common cause of low energy, as it supports proper cell respiration and energy production?","o":["Iron","Chromium","Potassium","Magnesium","C and D"],"a":3,"e":"Magnesium is specifically tied to cell respiration and energy production as a cofactor in ATP-related enzyme reactions."},{"q":"Which nutrient is needed for normal thyroid function, which supports proper metabolism and energy production?","o":["CoQ10","Zinc","Iron","Iodine"],"a":3,"e":"Iodine is essential for thyroid hormone synthesis, supporting metabolism and energy."},{"q":"Which amino acid has been shown to improve energy and endurance with supplementation of 500 to 750 mg twice daily, as it is important to fatty acid metabolism and the efficient use of the fats in the cells?","o":["L-carnitine","L-tryptophan","Tyrosine","Glutamine"],"a":0,"e":"L-carnitine shuttles fatty acids into mitochondria for energy production, matching this description and dosing."},{"q":"A multifaceted approach to treating chronic fatigue and fibromyalgia would not include:","o":["Herbal treatment with ginseng, ginger, turmeric, and gingko","Avoidance of sugar, refined flour, caffeine, and alcohol","Nutritional therapies with magnesium especially B12, other B vitamins and amino acids","Treating chronic infections of the bladder, vagina, prostate, and sinuses","Weekly juice fasting to relieve digestive problems contributing to fatigue"],"a":4,"e":"Weekly juice fasting isn't part of a sound multifaceted CFS/fibromyalgia approach — someone already fatigued and nutritionally taxed generally needs more consistent nourishment, not regular fasting."},{"q":"Which of the following statements concerning viral infections is FALSE?","o":["The primary way we heal from viruses is through the immune response, by means of lymphocyte attack functions and through the formation of specific antiviral antibodies","Acute infectious hepatitis A is associated with fatigue and a yellowing of the skin and eyes and possibly an initial period of dark urine and white- or gray-coloured stools","Cell membrance integrity is essential in preventing the beginnings of a viral infection and is supported mainly by vitamins C, E, and A and EFAs","Antibody responses can take anywhere from 2 to 4 days to reach adequate levels to counter the millions of viral organisms","Hepatitis B and C come from blood contact, although there may be some possible exposure with sexual activity"],"a":3,"e":"A primary antibody response typically takes longer than 2-4 days to reach significant levels — this timeframe understates how long adequate antibody levels actually take to develop."},{"q":"Which nutrient is useful in acute viral infections, especially with vitamin A, as the two enhance each other's effects in providing immune support?","o":["Vitamin D","Zinc","Magnesium","None of the above"],"a":1,"e":"Zinc and vitamin A are described as enhancing each other's immune-supportive effects in acute viral infections."},{"q":"Which herb can be used during the initial phases of infections as a mild \"antibiotic\" in lieu of using pharmaceutical antibiotics?","o":["Propolis","Echinacea","Garlic","Myrrh","A and D"],"a":1,"e":"Echinacea is the herb most classically associated with this mild-antibiotic-alternative role during early infection."},{"q":"Which of the following is the rate at which the body burns calories to maintain its functions at rest, and is affected by gender, age, diet activity level, thyroid function, amount of sleep, amount of body fat, body temperature, weight, and likely by genetics?","o":["BMI","Set-point","BMR","Fat–cell"],"a":2,"e":"BMR (Basal Metabolic Rate) is the rate of calorie burning at rest, affected by all these individual factors."},{"q":"Behaviour patterns for optimum weight does NOT include which of the following?","o":["Rotating foods","Eating most foods early in the day for best use of calories","Focusing on what you are eating and not what you are avoiding","Weighing yourself daily to ensure you are staying on track"],"a":3,"e":"Daily weighing is not one of the recommended behaviour patterns — it can create anxiety rather than support sustainable habits."},{"q":"Which of the following statements concerning weight loss is FALSE?","o":["During periods of weight loss, the body is breaking down more tissue that needs recycling or elimination. Toxins that have been stored in fat tissue can also be released in greater amounts during this time","During weight loss, amounts over the DRIs are needed for iron, zinc, copper, manganese, and molybdenum","To handle the extra toxin load, it is suggested to take in the morning 1 to 3 grams daily in 2 or 3 portions of vitamin C, together with vitamin E and selenium","Lemon water taken 15 to 30 minutes before meals can help digestion and utilization of fats","Avoid peppermint leaf tea as it stimulates the appetite by exciting the stomach nerves"],"a":4,"e":"Peppermint is generally associated with calming digestion and reducing appetite, not stimulating it — this reversal makes it the false statement."},{"q":"Which condition is associated with weight loss or the inability to gain weight and is associated with many other symptoms, such as rapid heart rate, sweaty palms, and insomnia?","o":["Hyperthyroidism","Yeast syndrome","Anorexia","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"These are classic hyperthyroidism symptoms."},{"q":"Which of the following are common to patients with yeast syndrome?","o":["Cravings for (and overeating of) sweets, breads, or alcohol","Sensitivity (and exposures) to molds, dampness, and certain smells","Mental symptoms, such as depression, mood swings, or confusion","Chronic fatigue, indigestion, or food reactions","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"All of these are named as common features of yeast syndrome (Candida)."},{"q":"Which of the following is part of a three-faceted approach to treating yeast syndrome?","o":["Eat foods that do not allow the yeast to thrive and divide, such as poultry, fish, nuts, seeds, and occasional eggs","Diminish the amount of yeast present. Fresh garlic, caprylic acid, or oregano oil can help with this","Replenish the colon with friendly bacteria","A and B only","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"The three facets are: starve the yeast, diminish it directly, and replenish beneficial bacteria — all three together."},{"q":"Which foods have been identified as possibly triggering an allergic reaction in the form of migraine headaches?","o":["Alcoholic beverages, chocolate, eggs, and cheese","Chocolate, strawberries, tomatoes, and eggs","Corn, wheat, milk, and alcoholic beverages","Eggs, sulfites, citrus fruits, and peanuts"],"a":0,"e":"Alcohol, chocolate, eggs, and cheese are the classic migraine-trigger grouping tested here."},{"q":"What is the best type of practical testing for food allergies?","o":["RAST","NAET","Elimination diet","All of the above"],"a":2,"e":"An elimination diet — removing and systematically reintroducing suspected foods — is considered the most practical testing approach."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about PMS is FALSE?","o":["PMS is aggravated by low blood sugar generated by stress and an intake of refined flour and sugar products","Low progesterone levels seem to be the main factor in PMS symptoms","Supplementing magnesium at amounts equal to up to 1.5 times the calcium level is helpful in reducing some PMS symptoms","Anxiety symptoms, such as mood swings and irritability, often respond to extra B vitamins, particularly thiamin and pyridoxine","None of the statements are false"],"a":4,"e":"All four PMS statements reflect this course's own teaching — none of them are false."},{"q":"Which of the following would be a good recommendation for pre/post surgery?","o":["Initiate a detoxification two or three months post-surgery, especially if general anesthesia was used during surgery","Avoid congestive foods","Vitamins A and C, and Zinc can speed up healing time","A and C","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Allowing the body to stabilize before a deeper detox program, avoiding congestive foods, and using A/C/zinc for healing are all reasonable pre/post-surgery recommendations together."},{"q":"People with chronic fatigue syndrome (CFS) can increase energy levels by engaging in regular vigorous exercise.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"CFS is associated with post-exertional malaise, where vigorous exercise tends to worsen rather than improve symptoms."},{"q":"Fasting one day a week on water or juices can be a valuable tool for many people wanting to lose or maintain weight, in that it re-emphasizes the importance of food choices and food awareness.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated framing of weekly fasting as a tool for food awareness in weight management."},{"q":"If taking higher amounts of zinc to help an acute viral infection, it is important to take extra copper and manganese and possibly iron for several weeks afterwards.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"High-dose zinc can compete with copper and manganese absorption, so replenishing them afterward is recommended."},{"q":"Symptoms of disease are not the real problem, rather they are a result of people's lives - what they eat, do, and feel and their deeper issues, attitudes, beliefs, and emotions.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's holistic framing of symptoms as downstream of lifestyle and deeper issues."},{"q":"The keys for treating acute viral conditions are rest, fluids, and extra vitamin A, beta carotene, vitamin C, and zinc.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated acute-viral-support protocol."},{"q":"Juice fasting is recommended for those with chronic viral infections.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Chronic viral infections generally call for sustained nutritional support rather than fasting, which could be counterproductive for someone managing an ongoing illness."},{"q":"Vitamin C is used so rapidly by a sick and healing body that it is needed almost every hour.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated high-frequency vitamin C guidance during acute illness."},{"q":"50-75% of people who lose weight on fad diets regain their lost weight and sometimes even more","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches well-known statistics on weight regain after fad dieting."},{"q":"Hyperactivity can be helped with a good diet and nutritional supplement program.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated position on diet/supplement support for hyperactivity."},{"q":"Depression can often be helped naturally through the elimination of chemicals, sugar, caffeine, tobacco, alcohol, and refined foods in addition to regular, moderate exercise.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's lifestyle-based approach to supporting mood naturally."}],"perStepChapter":false},{"n":13,"title":"Detoxification and Cleansing","kicker":"Lesson 13 of your course, with your official Lesson 13 Test.","steps":[{"id":"ns13-0","chapter":"A spectrum, not one thing","title":"A spectrum, not one thing","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> A toxin is any substance that creates irritating/harmful effects, undermines the body's health, stresses biochemical or organ functions, or actually damages tissues and organs — a spectrum from mild irritation to genuine organ damage, not a single fixed category.</p>  <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🏭 The body also produces toxins <b>internally</b>, simply through its normal everyday metabolic functions — detoxification isn't only about external exposure.</p>","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"A spectrum, not one thing","items":[{"label":"Breathing them","detail":"Inhaled pollutants and airborne chemicals."},{"label":"Ingesting them","detail":"Through food, water, and supplements."},{"label":"Contacting them","detail":"Direct physical/skin contact."}]}},{"id":"ns13-1","chapter":"Liver leads, skin and lymph support","title":"Liver leads, skin and lymph support","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> THE LIVER IS THE MOST IMPORTANT DETOXIFICATION ORGAN</p> <p>The liver transforms many toxic substances into harmless agents. Skin and dermal structures — sweat glands, sebaceous glands, and tears — form another detoxification pathway, working alongside the lymphatic, urinary, and respiratory systems.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 💧 If asked for the single most important <i>detoxifier</i> (as opposed to detox <i>organ</i>), the answer shifts to <b>water</b> — it underlies virtually every elimination pathway the body has, from urine to sweat to bowel movements.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns13-2","chapter":"Individualized, not automatic","title":"Individualized, not automatic","html":"<p>Who needs to detoxify and when is based in part on individual lifestyle and needs — not a one-size-fits-all schedule. That said, a general seasonal rhythm is suggested: at least a 1-2 week detox program in <b>spring</b> and <b>autumn</b>.</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 Toxicity is framed as a much greater concern now than in the past, and a long list of common complaints — arthritis, allergies, obesity, skin problems, fatigue, aches, coughs, immune weakness, and headaches — are all connected to toxicity in this framework.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns13-3","chapter":"Purifiers and laxatives","title":"Purifiers and laxatives","html":"","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Purifiers and laxatives","items":[{"label":"Cayenne pepper","detail":"Described as a blood purifier that increases fluid elimination and sweat."},{"label":"Senna leaf (+ licorice + dandelion)","detail":"The classic herbal laxative grouping used in detoxification support."}]}},{"id":"ns13-4","chapter":"Three substances with their own detox profile","title":"Three substances with their own detox profile","html":"","visual":{"kind":"tiles","title":"Three substances with their own detox profile","items":[{"label":"Sugar","detail":"Linked to yeast overgrowth, alcoholism, anxiety, and depression. Sugar-detox symptoms can include fatigue, anxiety, irritability, and palpitations. Nutrients that help reduce sugar cravings: B vitamins, L-glutamine, zinc, and chromium."},{"label":"Caffeine","detail":"Negative effects include anxiety, heartburn, and mild arrhythmias/tachycardia/palpitations, plus a kidney-stone association. It does not increase mineral absorption — if anything, caffeine can interfere with calcium absorption."},{"label":"Alcohol","detail":"Impairs (rather than increases) the liver's ability to break down estrogen; damages the heart/blood vessels; affects carbohydrate metabolism (risking hypoglycemia); and slows fat metabolism, allowing fat to build up in the liver."}]}},{"id":"ns13-5","chapter":"Benefits, planning, and the hardest days","title":"Benefits, planning, and the hardest days","html":"<p>Named benefits of fasting: reduced allergies, creativity, better sleep, and anti-aging effects. Grapefruit, lemon, cucumber, and greens are named as the juices most helpful specifically for weight loss.</p> <p><p>1</p><p>The first two days of a fast are generally the most challenging.</p> <p>2</p><p>Regular exercise during a cleanse stimulates sweating and skin elimination.</p> <p>3</p><p>Breaking a fast must be planned and executed slowly and carefully to avoid triggering symptoms or sickness.</p> </p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🚨 During an actual fast, the emphasis is on giving the digestive system a rest — heavy supplementation during the fast itself runs somewhat counter to that purpose. More toxins are released during detoxification generally, and greater intake of water, fibre, and antioxidants is suggested to help protect against them.</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns13-6","chapter":"Don't confuse these on the exam","title":"Don't confuse these on the exam","html":"<p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> 🧠 Toxins stored in fatty tissue: many lipophilic (fat-soluble) toxins accumulate in the body's fat stores over time — part of why weight loss can release stored toxins (Unit 12 connection).</p>","visual":null},{"id":"ns13-6-q0","title":"Quick check","teach":[],"act":{"type":"choice","prompt":"\"Homeostasis\" means the body's functions are out of balance. True or false?","options":[{"label":"True","correct":false,"feedback":""},{"label":"False — homeostasis means internal balance/stability, the opposite of this definition","correct":true,"feedback":""}]}},{"id":"ns13-7","chapter":"Bottlenecks in elimination, not elimination itself","title":"Bottlenecks in elimination, not elimination itself","html":"<p>Poor digestionReduced liver functionPoor kidney elimination</p> <p class=\"note\"><b>Key idea:</b> ✅ By contrast, <b>excessive sweating is an elimination pathway, not a cause of increased toxicity</b> — sweating removes toxins rather than adding to the load. The pattern to notice: toxicity tends to build up when an elimination pathway is impaired, not when the body is actively using one.</p>","visual":null}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Three ways we acquire toxins?","back":"Breathing, ingesting, physical contact."},{"front":"Does the body make toxins internally?","back":"Yes, through normal everyday metabolic function."},{"front":"Most important detoxification organ?","back":"The liver."},{"front":"Most important single detoxifier?","back":"Water."},{"front":"Suggested detox timing?","back":"1-2 weeks in spring and autumn."},{"front":"Cayenne pepper's detox role?","back":"Blood purifier; increases fluid elimination and sweat."},{"front":"Classic herbal laxative?","back":"Senna leaf."},{"front":"Does caffeine increase mineral absorption?","back":"No — it does not, and can interfere with calcium absorption."},{"front":"Alcohol's effect on estrogen breakdown?","back":"Impairs it (doesn't increase it)."},{"front":"Hardest days of a fast?","back":"The first two days."},{"front":"What does homeostasis mean?","back":"Internal balance/stability — not imbalance."},{"front":"Does excessive sweating increase toxicity?","back":"No — it's an elimination pathway, not a cause of toxicity."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of the following problems is associated with sugar intake?","o":["Yeast overgrowth","Alcoholism","Anxiety","Depression","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Sugar intake is linked to all of these: yeast overgrowth, alcoholism, anxiety, and depression."},{"q":"Sweat and sebaceous glands and tears are part of which of the following general detoxification systems?","o":["Skin and dermal","Lymph","Urinary","Respiratory","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"Sweat glands, sebaceous glands, and tears are grouped under the skin/dermal detoxification system."},{"q":"Which of the following describes how we acquire toxins from our environment?","o":["Breathing them","Ingesting them","Contacting them physically","A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Breathing, ingesting, and physical contact are all named routes of toxin exposure."},{"q":"Which of the following statements about detoxification and cleansing is FALSE?","o":["The proper elimination of toxins is essential to health","The liver helps transform many toxic substances into harmless agents","Who needs to detoxify and when is based in part on individual lifestyle and needs","Detoxification therapy – particularly fasting and juice cleansing - is a relatively new and beneficial way to treat many common health complaints","At least a 1 to 2 week detox program is suggested in the spring and autumn"],"a":3,"e":"Fasting and cleansing practices are actually ancient, used across cultures for centuries — describing them as \"relatively new\" is the false statement."},{"q":"Which of the following are potential symptoms of a sugar detox?","o":["Fatigue","Anxiety","Irritability","Palpitations","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Fatigue, anxiety, irritability, and palpitations are all named sugar-detox symptoms."},{"q":"Which of the following cleansing herbs is a blood purifier and increases fluid elimination and sweat?","o":["Garlic","Prickly ash bark","Cayenne pepper","Ginger"],"a":2,"e":"Cayenne pepper is classically described as a blood purifier that increases sweat and fluid elimination."},{"q":"Which of the following is considered the most important detoxification organ?","o":["Kidney","Liver","Colon","Skin"],"a":1,"e":"The liver is the most important detoxification organ, transforming toxic substances into harmless agents."},{"q":"Which of the following is not a negative effect of caffeine?","o":["General anxiety","Increased heartburn","Increased absorption of iron and calcium","Mild arrhythmias, tachycardia, and palpitations","Kidney stones"],"a":2,"e":"Caffeine does not increase mineral absorption — if anything it can interfere with calcium absorption — making this the one that isn't a real negative effect."},{"q":"How much caffeine is in a 6-oz cup of percolated coffee?","o":["50 to 100 mg","60 to 120 mg","70 to 80 mg","80 to 110 mg"],"a":1,"e":"60-120mg is the closest tested range for caffeine content in a 6oz cup of percolated coffee."},{"q":"Which of the following foods help heal anemia, the liver, the intestines, and breath?","o":["Wheatgrass","Spinach","Lemon","Parsley"],"a":3,"e":"Parsley is specifically noted for supporting breath freshness alongside these other benefits."},{"q":"Which of the following would be considered a benefit of fasting?","o":["Reduction of allergies","Creativity","Better sleep","Antiaging effects","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Reduced allergies, creativity, better sleep, and anti-aging effects are all named benefits of fasting."},{"q":"Juices most helpful in weight loss include:","o":["Grapefruit, lemon, cucumber, and greens","Grapefruit, oranges, carrots, and greens","Oranges, lemon, cucumber, and greens","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"Grapefruit, lemon, cucumber, and greens — the lower-sugar grouping — is tested as most helpful for weight loss."},{"q":"Which of the following statements regarding the risks of alcohol is FALSE?","o":["Alcohol increases levels of the liver enzyme that breaks down estrogen","Alcohol intake and abuse is damaging to the heart and blood vessels and leads to cardiovascular diseases and dysfunctions","Carbohydrate metabolism is affected by alcohol and can lead to hypoglycemia and diabetes","Slows fat metabolism resulting in fat building up in the liver"],"a":0,"e":"Alcohol actually impairs (decreases) the liver's ability to break down estrogen, rather than increasing it — making this the false statement."},{"q":"Nutrients that can help reduce sugar cravings are:","o":["B vitamins","L-glutamine","Zinc","Chromium","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"B vitamins, L-glutamine, zinc, and chromium are all named as helpful for reducing sugar cravings."},{"q":"During detoxification, more toxins are released into the body. Greater intake of which of the following will provide protection against those toxins?","o":["Water","Fibre","Antioxidants","A and C","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Water, fibre, and antioxidants are all named as protective during increased toxin release."},{"q":"Which of the following does NOT add to increased toxicity?","o":["Poor digestion","Reduced liver function","Poor elimination through the kidneys","Excessive sweating"],"a":3,"e":"Excessive sweating is an elimination pathway that removes toxins, rather than a cause of increased toxicity."},{"q":"What is a toxin?","o":["Any substance that creates irritating and/or harmful effects in the body","Any substance that undermines the body's health","Any substance that stresses the biochemical or organ functions","Any substance that actually damages tissues and organs","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"A toxin spans this whole spectrum, from irritation to genuine organ damage."},{"q":"Which of the following could be considered the most important detoxifier?","o":["Fibre","Water","Vegetables","Antioxidants"],"a":1,"e":"Water underlies virtually every elimination pathway the body has, making it the most important single detoxifier."},{"q":"Which of the following herbs are useful in detoxification as a laxative?","o":["Yellow dock, rhubarb root, and ginger root","Dandilion, horsetail, and licorice","Goldenseal, peppermint, and rhubarb root","Senna leaf, licorice, and dandelion"],"a":3,"e":"Senna leaf is the classic herbal laxative, grouped here with licorice and dandelion."},{"q":"Toxicity is of much greater concern now than ever before.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated position on modern toxicity concerns."},{"q":"Breaking a fast must be well planned and executed slowly and carefully to prevent the creation of symptoms and sickness.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches standard fasting guidance about carefully breaking a fast."},{"q":"It is recommended to take natural supplements during a fast.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"The purpose of a fast is largely to give the digestive system a rest — heavy supplementation during the fast itself runs counter to that goal."},{"q":"When fasting, the first two days are generally the most challenging.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches common fasting experience, where initial symptoms are often hardest before adaptation."},{"q":"Regular exercise is important during a cleanse, as it stimulates sweating and encourages elimination through the skin.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated connection between exercise and detox via sweating."},{"q":"An acidic diet causes more nicotine cravings than an alkaline diet.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Urine pH affects nicotine excretion rate — a more acidic state increases nicotine excretion, which can increase cravings for more nicotine to compensate."},{"q":"People who over-consume protein seem to crave sugar and carbohydrate-rich foods.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's stated observation connecting high-protein intake with carbohydrate cravings."},{"q":"Many of the poisons (toxins) that we ingest or make are stored in the fatty tissues.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Lipophilic (fat-soluble) toxins are well known to accumulate in fatty tissue."},{"q":"\"Homeostasis\" is a term that means the body functions are out of balance.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Homeostasis actually means internal balance/stability — the opposite of this definition."},{"q":"Internally, the body produces toxins through its normal everyday functions.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Normal metabolism produces waste products/toxins as part of everyday bodily function."},{"q":"Arthritis, allergies, obesity, many skin problems, fatigue, pains, coughs, immune weakness, and headaches can all be related to toxicity.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"This matches the course's broad framing connecting many common complaints to underlying toxicity."}],"perStepChapter":false}]};
